WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.720
- The following is a conversation
with Khabib Nurmagomedov,

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:07.880
one of the greatest fighters of
all time, who retired from the

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:14.060
UFC undefeated with a perfect twenty-nine
and O record. It was a huge honor for me

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:18.919
to meet him, to train together, and
to have this deep conversation.

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:23.580
We had this conversation
entirely in Russian.

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:27.420
This was my first time
doing so on this podcast.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:32.049
And even these words I'm saying
right now are spoken in Russian.

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:36.460
So if you're hearing me in English,
you're actually listening to the

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:40.400
English dub. On YouTube, you can
switch between language audio

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:44.380
tracks by clicking the settings
gear icon, then clicking

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:49.000
audio track, and then selecting
the language you prefer.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:54.120
For the original Russian
version, select Russian. And

00:00:54.300 --> 00:01:00.740
for the English dub, select English. Subtitles
are also available in both languages.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:05.440
The translation and dubbing
are a collaborative effort of

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:10.560
translators, real human beings,
including myself and AI.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:15.520
Thanks to the incredible team
at ElevenLabs for their help.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:20.500
And now, please allow me to take
this moment to say thank you

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:25.900
from the bottom of my heart for your
love and support all these years.

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:32.420
I love you all. This is the Lex Fridman
Podcast. And now, dear friends,

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:38.820
here's Khabib Nurmagomedov. Let's try in
Russian if we can, like you suggested.

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:40.520
- I can help you out if you need.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:44.600
- Yeah, thanks, brother. I kind of
feel it in my heart and soul, but

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.460
sometimes I can't find the right words.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.740
- I know, brother. We got this.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:55.060
- Great. Let's start from the beginning.
So that gym back in Dagestan where you

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:58.800
trained is legendary for
its spartan conditions.

00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:03.460
There was just one shower for
seventy people, only cold

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:07.440
water. And yet, this
humble little gym managed

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:12.100
to raise more than twenty world champions,
including several UFC champions.

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:16.320
Can you try to explain or maybe reflect on

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:21.220
what factors in that gym, those
training sessions, that life in

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:24.000
Dagestan created so many champions?

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:29.320
- Sure. So we're talking about the
gym, the one located in Makhachkala.

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:33.620
And Makhachkala, for the people who
don't know, it's the capital of

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:37.620
Dagestan. This was back around the
years from two thousand to two

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:41.580
thousand and ten, and even
later on. In twenty nineteen,

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:45.660
we opened up a big gym, a
major facility that is still

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:49.240
functioning to this day.
But before twenty nineteen,

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:53.260
we all actually trained in that
gym where there was practically

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:57.200
nothing. It was just a school
gym with nothing but mats, a

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:03.680
single punching bag, one climbing rope,
and a kind of indoor basketball court.

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:08.560
And if we go even further back, before
two thousand, before we moved to

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:10.360
Makhachkala, we lived in a village.

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:15.120
In the village, we had a gym right
inside our house. My father,

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:19.600
he built a gym at home, and he was training
all the local village kids right there.

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:24.480
And there were times back when I
was a child, let's say when I was

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:28.780
maybe six or seven years old, I would
occasionally wake up to the sound of,

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.680
people training, doing their
drills right there in the gym.

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:38.200
You know, like with judo or freestyle
when they're drilling, one guy throwing

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:42.180
another. Those are my first
conscious memories. I was maybe

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:46.180
five, six, seven years old
at that time. So my father

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:50.200
set up this gym in the village
and trained local kids, including

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:53.870
me, my older brother, and all
our close and distant relatives,

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:57.360
anyone who wanted to come.
A lot of kids came to him.

00:03:57.860 --> 00:03:59.800
- So you would wake up and
there are these sounds.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:04.420
- Yeah. Yeah. Yeah. You hear the
hitting, the throwing over and

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:08.460
over. And my father's voice,
how he's shouting, giving

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:12.260
instructions, pushing someone,
coaching, teaching. I

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:16.140
lived with those sounds. But that
was in the village. And later

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:17.959
when we moved to Makhachkala,

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:22.740
well, you couldn't say the gym
there was wow either. There were

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:26.720
only mats. And of course, the
most valuable thing an athlete

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:30.840
can have, a coach, my father.
So he trained us there.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:36.700
And to be honest, let's say, until I

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:40.700
became a champion and had
big success, we never had a

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:43.520
good big gym where we could, you know,

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:47.880
all take a shower together at
the same time after training.

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:53.140
We always had just one shower for
everyone, no proper amenities.

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:57.380
- So your life story began in
the mountains of Dagestan in

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:01.240
Sildi, then Kirovaul,
and then Makhachkala.

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:06.300
And in all these situations, there
are very interesting stories. But

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:10.180
one of the things I heard you say is that
the best results are born out of the worst

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:12.659
conditions. What can you say about that?

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:17.440
Philosophically, is it correct to say
that comfort is kind of the enemy for

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:18.100
champions?

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:23.260
- It depends on the sport. For example,
let's say in football, you have to

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:27.200
have conditions. You can't really
play if there is practically no

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:31.200
field. Of course, you can play in the
dirt, and it happens sometimes. You

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:35.200
can start there. But when you are ten or
twelve years old, you already have to

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:40.520
be at a certain level. You must have
conditions. But if we look at our sport,

00:05:42.170 --> 00:05:44.500
then what is the most
important thing? The coach.

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:47.630
You also need sparring partners who will

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:51.800
push you in the gym, as they
say, right? And you need the

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:55.840
mats. Our sport is not
expensive. You just buy your

00:05:55.900 --> 00:05:59.780
wrestling shoes, singlets,
shorts, a T-shirt. And

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:03.400
if there's a mat, you wrestle,
you become a champion, you fight.

00:06:04.248 --> 00:06:08.168
But when you live too well, let's
say, you know, you're never

00:06:08.208 --> 00:06:12.188
in need. For example, you were
born into a wealthy family

00:06:12.198 --> 00:06:13.788
and you already have everything.

00:06:14.668 --> 00:06:18.408
Would that same wealthy father, would
he force you to do such a brutal

00:06:18.528 --> 00:06:24.108
sport? A sport where you come
home every day with injuries

00:06:25.348 --> 00:06:29.228
where you come home with an exhausted
body, you come home with bruises.

00:06:29.568 --> 00:06:33.528
Would he really do that? That's the
question. And the kid himself, who

00:06:33.588 --> 00:06:36.628
lives a life of luxury, would
he even want to do this?

00:06:38.088 --> 00:06:41.988
When we look at it statistically,
the answer is no. This

00:06:42.048 --> 00:06:45.968
is where you need that hunger. You have
to be in a position where you are truly

00:06:46.008 --> 00:06:46.408
in need.

00:06:47.168 --> 00:06:50.328
- And it's interesting that there's
a psychological side to this as

00:06:50.388 --> 00:06:53.908
well, that you need a tough
situation to create champions.

00:06:54.828 --> 00:06:58.528
But what's really interesting is how
Dagestan has produced so many wrestling

00:06:58.628 --> 00:07:02.018
champions. You mentioned
football. Let's look at other

00:07:02.108 --> 00:07:06.008
countries. Why has Brazil produced
so many great football players?

00:07:06.968 --> 00:07:10.728
Why does Argentina have so many
football players? Of course, you have

00:07:10.788 --> 00:07:11.968
Germany, France, and all that.

00:07:12.048 --> 00:07:12.748
- European, yeah.

00:07:12.768 --> 00:07:16.328
- The whole European side. But those
are completely different cultures.

00:07:16.348 --> 00:07:20.308
- Yes. Well, in Brazil, their strength is
in their numbers. How many people do

00:07:20.348 --> 00:07:24.048
they have? The population is huge,
more than two hundred million.

00:07:24.498 --> 00:07:28.488
And people really love it there.
It's a huge tradition. Because, you

00:07:28.548 --> 00:07:32.028
know, without a real love for this
sport, you can never achieve any

00:07:32.108 --> 00:07:35.888
success. You truly have to love what
you are doing. So they have the

00:07:35.948 --> 00:07:39.848
tradition and they have the love for it,
and that is why they play for all the

00:07:39.888 --> 00:07:43.068
top clubs. Well, I think
they just win by numbers.

00:07:43.328 --> 00:07:47.128
- Yeah, that's a powerful aspect of
their culture. How much people,

00:07:47.568 --> 00:07:51.548
how much the whole nation loves to play
football. And if you add to that a

00:07:51.608 --> 00:07:55.508
system that creates strong teams, that's
how you can produce so many football

00:07:55.568 --> 00:07:56.288
legends.

00:07:56.348 --> 00:08:00.188
- Yes. Take Dagestan, for example. We
love football. I just know how much

00:08:00.228 --> 00:08:04.148
people in Dagestan love football. Yes, we
have wrestlers, fighters. There are many

00:08:04.188 --> 00:08:08.148
of us. But for football players, there
is no ecosystem like that. Not so well

00:08:08.268 --> 00:08:12.228
organized where a kid can, for
example, play at school, then go to

00:08:12.268 --> 00:08:16.148
some academy, then get noticed there, and
then get picked up by a club. We don't

00:08:16.188 --> 00:08:19.568
have that system. It's just
kids running on the streets,

00:08:21.028 --> 00:08:25.368
playing on the streets, following football.
Let's say playing on PlayStation.

00:08:27.748 --> 00:08:31.548
There is no system. There is no
selection process where kids can go and

00:08:31.628 --> 00:08:35.468
show themselves, so they can be
noticed and get signed. I think

00:08:35.508 --> 00:08:39.368
Brazil and other countries, and
of course Europe, they have it.

00:08:40.468 --> 00:08:44.217
So yeah, we have big problems with
football. However, in our sports

00:08:44.648 --> 00:08:48.568
where you can, where you can wrestle,
where you can fight, in that sense, we

00:08:48.608 --> 00:08:52.548
have a system. For example, the gyms,
you know, in Makhachkala, let's say

00:08:52.588 --> 00:08:55.988
for combat sports. I'm not
even counting like freestyle

00:08:56.108 --> 00:09:00.008
wrestling or other styles. There
may be forty or fifty gyms for

00:09:00.088 --> 00:09:04.048
combat sports, and all are full. If
you win in Dagestan, you move on to

00:09:04.058 --> 00:09:08.008
the North Caucasus level, then to the
Russian nationals. If you're champion

00:09:08.068 --> 00:09:12.058
of Russia, you go to the Worlds.
In amateurs, they notice you and

00:09:12.088 --> 00:09:15.228
you make a name for yourself.
There is a certain ladder here.

00:09:16.628 --> 00:09:20.468
But in many other sports here in
Dagestan, we don't have such a

00:09:20.528 --> 00:09:22.217
well-structured ladder
for people to climb.

00:09:23.328 --> 00:09:27.217
- Can we look at this philosophically again?
I mean, you've seen so many wrestlers,

00:09:27.548 --> 00:09:31.448
fighters. Why exactly were
these gyms creating so

00:09:31.528 --> 00:09:35.368
many champions? I'm just honestly
curious because it's rare

00:09:35.428 --> 00:09:40.408
in any sport, one gym or a small number
of gyms creating so many champions.

00:09:40.448 --> 00:09:44.988
- Well, first important factor is the
competition level is incredibly high.

00:09:47.288 --> 00:09:50.848
You come to the gym, and in every
spot you have good wrestlers,

00:09:51.248 --> 00:09:54.528
ten or fifteen guys right there,
and the coaches are great.

00:09:56.988 --> 00:10:00.328
You know, it so happened that my
father broke through in the media,

00:10:01.268 --> 00:10:06.368
and then I became a champion, and
the other guys became champions too.

00:10:07.248 --> 00:10:12.188
And my father's philosophy, it really
resonated, and he became known for it.

00:10:13.568 --> 00:10:17.528
But there are coaches in Dagestan
that not many people know

00:10:17.628 --> 00:10:21.428
about. They are known
locally, but they are

00:10:21.528 --> 00:10:25.288
very good, and actually they have
the knowledge, they have the

00:10:25.368 --> 00:10:27.328
approach, they have the philosophy.

00:10:28.398 --> 00:10:32.328
They could also be at the same level
as my father was. I believe there

00:10:32.388 --> 00:10:36.088
are really quite a few of them.
In terms of the coaching, I think

00:10:36.148 --> 00:10:39.888
Dagestan was extremely lucky.
It probably goes back to the

00:10:39.948 --> 00:10:43.788
nineteen fifties when wrestling
really took off here, starting

00:10:43.868 --> 00:10:47.828
with Ali Aliyev, our legendary
wrestler, a five-time world champion

00:10:47.908 --> 00:10:51.768
who went to the Olympics three times.
That's where it started, with his

00:10:51.828 --> 00:10:55.648
generation. There was Zagalav
Abdulbekov, Ali Aliyev.

00:10:55.688 --> 00:10:59.638
Those are the well-known names. I mean,
those guys are famous, but there were

00:10:59.668 --> 00:11:03.268
so many other less known wrestlers
who later on, after they

00:11:03.308 --> 00:11:07.158
retired, they all became great coaches
themselves. And these coaches, they

00:11:07.208 --> 00:11:10.808
raised a very large number of
guys who are really good at

00:11:10.828 --> 00:11:14.588
wrestling. And then some of
those coaches, they kind of

00:11:14.648 --> 00:11:18.468
switched over to fighting, let's say to

00:11:18.488 --> 00:11:22.228
grappling, to MMA, and they
started coaching in combat Sambo

00:11:22.568 --> 00:11:27.048
as well. And it just kept going like
that, you know. It was moving forward

00:11:28.448 --> 00:11:32.208
step by step. And so we have
a huge number of really great

00:11:32.308 --> 00:11:36.008
coaches. I believe this is one of
the single most important keys

00:11:36.408 --> 00:11:41.928
to the success of Dagestani wrestlers and
fighters, the coaching staff of Dagestan.

00:11:42.248 --> 00:11:48.118
- Is it more about technique or is it really
just about serious, incredibly hard work?

00:11:48.728 --> 00:11:52.638
- Well, you have to look at the traditions,
because generally it's just the way it is

00:11:52.688 --> 00:11:56.528
in Dagestan. Everyone wrestles.
Everyone fights. It's part of

00:11:56.548 --> 00:12:00.728
our culture. You know yourself, fighting
is like fifty percent wrestling.

00:12:02.208 --> 00:12:05.788
Every parent wants their children to
wrestle, to be physically strong.

00:12:06.220 --> 00:12:09.920
Our approach to kids is that
a child must be strong.

00:12:10.300 --> 00:12:13.350
They must be able to stand
up for themselves, for their

00:12:13.380 --> 00:12:17.090
family. It's something that
has traditionally always

00:12:17.180 --> 00:12:21.960
been in every single one of our
clans, every family, every village.

00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:24.440
It's just in our blood.

00:12:25.960 --> 00:12:29.820
- I mean, it's worth pointing out, and you've
actually written about this, that this isn't just a

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:32.980
recent thing. It's been going
on for hundreds of years.

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:37.220
- Yes, that's right. These
lands, Dagestan specifically,

00:12:37.640 --> 00:12:41.140
have seen powerful armies sweep
through time and time again.

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:45.720
Because of that, people here have
always had to fight to survive.

00:12:46.120 --> 00:12:51.060
- Refusing to back down, refusing to give
up their land or lose their identity.

00:12:51.350 --> 00:12:55.460
- Their homes, their villages,
yeah, their families, and land.

00:12:56.660 --> 00:13:00.300
If we look back a thousand years in
history, maybe fifteen hundred years,

00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:05.380
there was the Khazar Khaganate,
the Arabic Caliphate, then

00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:09.320
the Iranian Empire, or the
Persian Empire, I should say.

00:13:10.700 --> 00:13:14.540
Then the Russian Empire,
Timur passed through

00:13:14.620 --> 00:13:18.140
there, the Mongols. And if
we look at the geographical

00:13:18.200 --> 00:13:22.160
location of Dagestan, for
those who don't know, the

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:26.080
Caucasus range runs across the
whole region like a wall, and

00:13:26.120 --> 00:13:29.620
then it slopes down right where
the Caspian lowlands are.

00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:34.700
So you've got the sea on the right,
the mountains on the left, and the

00:13:34.820 --> 00:13:41.580
Caspian lowlands right here. And there was
a city there that's still standing today.

00:13:41.740 --> 00:13:45.660
It's one of the oldest cities in
the world, not just in Russia. It's

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:48.760
Derbent. It used to be called Babul Abwab.

00:13:49.880 --> 00:13:53.720
It means the gate of gates.
Very major trade routes

00:13:53.780 --> 00:13:57.660
went through there. Very large
armies passed through, and it

00:13:57.700 --> 00:14:00.680
was bound to leave a mark on the people.

00:14:02.520 --> 00:14:06.160
we have so many languages, more than
thirty different languages, and

00:14:06.260 --> 00:14:08.280
people just don't understand each other.

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:12.940
Take Islam and me. We have different
languages, come from different backgrounds.

00:14:13.080 --> 00:14:16.960
Islam is Lak, and I'm Avar.
When we talk, it's not like

00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:19.380
it's a dialect. We don't
understand each other at all.

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:24.440
But since Islam has been with us
for nearly twenty-five years,

00:14:24.980 --> 00:14:28.660
he actually picked up
some Avar over time. But

00:14:28.720 --> 00:14:32.600
anyway, the deep history
of the region, it all left

00:14:32.660 --> 00:14:37.080
its mark. It created many different
languages and traditions.

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:42.020
Basically, of course, Islam
is our primary religion, but

00:14:42.080 --> 00:14:46.000
Dagestan has actually absorbed
a lot of cultures and

00:14:46.140 --> 00:14:53.440
faiths. Even our food culture, it is incredibly
rich. We have European and Asian and

00:14:53.500 --> 00:15:00.720
Caucasian, our own unique cuisine. So on
the people, on the people themselves, the

00:15:00.780 --> 00:15:04.640
last fifteen hundred years
of history have left a huge

00:15:04.720 --> 00:15:08.480
mark. If you're into history
and you start reading about

00:15:08.520 --> 00:15:12.480
Dagestan, I think you'll get
completely immersed and be amazed

00:15:12.540 --> 00:15:14.260
by what a unique place it is.

00:15:15.140 --> 00:15:19.050
So all of this left a huge mark. Look
at the terrain. It's a coastal plain,

00:15:19.440 --> 00:15:22.920
and then the mountains rise up.
For instance, take someone like

00:15:22.940 --> 00:15:26.660
Timur. When he passed through with a
huge army, he stayed in the Dagestan

00:15:26.700 --> 00:15:30.640
lowlands but never went up into
the mountains. Why? Because

00:15:30.700 --> 00:15:34.620
you've got hundreds of thousands
of troops, soldiers traveling with

00:15:34.660 --> 00:15:38.640
horses and supplies. If they
had started going up into

00:15:38.680 --> 00:15:42.060
the mountains where it's
cold and there are no roads,

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:46.540
it would have been too tough for
them. So the highlanders somehow

00:15:46.640 --> 00:15:52.660
managed to stay independent. But the
lowlands, the Caspian lowlands of

00:15:52.700 --> 00:15:56.100
Dagestan, they kept
changing hands. Massive

00:15:56.180 --> 00:16:00.460
empires would roll in, and all of
that left a mark on the people.

00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:05.860
Gate of the gates, Babul Abwab.
Historians wrote about it, say,

00:16:06.120 --> 00:16:09.980
a thousand or fifteen hundred
years ago, great historians

00:16:10.060 --> 00:16:13.400
from various countries. People
have written so much about

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:17.180
Dagestan. It went by different
names back then, but

00:16:17.260 --> 00:16:20.280
they wrote a ton about this exact region.

00:16:21.580 --> 00:16:24.420
Many people, many empires
invaded our land.

00:16:25.160 --> 00:16:29.949
- So do you feel that inside you, in your
blood, your heart, like all of this history?

00:16:30.960 --> 00:16:34.680
- Well, in the past, it's not like I
really felt it. I knew Dagestan's

00:16:34.740 --> 00:16:38.580
history, but maybe with time, you
start to see it in a whole new

00:16:38.640 --> 00:16:44.760
light. Every year, you change a bit
on a mental and psychological level.

00:16:46.480 --> 00:16:51.720
Now I understand. I really
do. You cannot just

00:16:52.780 --> 00:16:56.120
be born physically and
mentally strong. Your

00:16:56.160 --> 00:17:00.080
environment shapes you no matter
what, where you grew up, how you

00:17:00.120 --> 00:17:04.080
grew up, the conditions, and who
you had around you. And after

00:17:04.140 --> 00:17:06.930
all, genetics are also
a part of who you are.

00:17:07.640 --> 00:17:11.560
So when you add all this up, yeah,
I just feel like all of this

00:17:11.640 --> 00:17:13.150
has come together inside me.

00:17:14.560 --> 00:17:18.240
- It's interesting because in
sports, we know many of the

00:17:18.320 --> 00:17:20.740
names of the great fighters and wrestlers.

00:17:22.260 --> 00:17:25.900
Yet when it comes to war, there
are so many people who fought,

00:17:26.440 --> 00:17:33.740
heroes, truly great warriors. Some we know
by name, but most of them we'll never

00:17:33.920 --> 00:17:35.009
actually know and-

00:17:35.300 --> 00:17:35.860
- We'll never know.

00:17:36.780 --> 00:17:38.320
- You write about Imam Shamil,

00:17:39.220 --> 00:17:43.960
that he's an important figure for
this region and a hero as well.

00:17:44.040 --> 00:17:49.800
- Well, Imam Shamil, he was known for
resisting an empire as huge as Russia.

00:17:51.260 --> 00:17:55.200
Dagestan is so small, and he
had so few people with him. How

00:17:55.240 --> 00:17:59.230
did he get famous? He got famous because he
held out for a very long time. He didn't

00:17:59.300 --> 00:18:03.160
just resist for a couple
of years. He was like a

00:18:03.180 --> 00:18:06.980
regular fighter, let's say,
a soldier for ten years, and

00:18:07.020 --> 00:18:12.020
then when he became the leader, he
went on for another twenty-five years.

00:18:13.012 --> 00:18:16.752
So, 35 years of his conscious
life he spent fighting

00:18:17.012 --> 00:18:20.892
not just some random empire,
but what was then, if not

00:18:20.932 --> 00:18:25.812
the most powerful, definitely one of
the strongest empires of that era.

00:18:27.712 --> 00:18:29.892
And that is what made him famous.

00:18:30.852 --> 00:18:34.792
And so there are villages in
Dagestan that deeply honor the

00:18:34.852 --> 00:18:38.552
memory of particular warriors
who came from their villages,

00:18:39.472 --> 00:18:47.152
from their clan, from their ethnic group.
But in, well world history, they're

00:18:47.192 --> 00:18:52.632
barely known. Like, take Shamil. He's
a leader, right? So everybody knows

00:18:52.712 --> 00:18:56.632
him. But there are plenty of heroes
who've been forgotten. But yeah, their

00:18:56.652 --> 00:18:57.712
own people remember.

00:18:58.452 --> 00:19:02.252
- Villages hold on to history.
Plus, we have to admit

00:19:02.312 --> 00:19:06.232
that conditions in the mountains are
incredibly harsh. That's part of it

00:19:06.312 --> 00:19:09.232
too. Just surviving up
there is a real struggle.

00:19:10.252 --> 00:19:13.892
- See, back then, people in the mountains
worked all through the summer just to

00:19:13.972 --> 00:19:17.522
survive the winter, because winters
are harsh, with heavy snow,

00:19:17.812 --> 00:19:23.172
completely cutting off villages. People
just stay inside and try to outlast it.

00:19:24.212 --> 00:19:28.152
And in the summer, they work. Terrace
farming was a huge thing for us.

00:19:29.152 --> 00:19:33.012
Our terraces are among the
most, you could say, famous and

00:19:33.192 --> 00:19:37.072
ancient in the world. They're
known as the Chokh terraces. Now,

00:19:37.132 --> 00:19:40.672
those are the famous ones. But
every village had terraces.

00:19:41.092 --> 00:19:44.792
There's a place out there called
Marghit, right across from our

00:19:44.832 --> 00:19:48.732
village. People worked over there,
but here, in the village, is

00:19:48.792 --> 00:19:52.772
where they lived. I mean, you'd think,
why didn't they just build a village

00:19:52.832 --> 00:19:56.412
right there, in the place where
we grow all our food? Because

00:19:56.612 --> 00:20:00.532
enemies could easily ride in
on horseback. An army could

00:20:00.572 --> 00:20:04.212
easily get to you there, so they'd
build a village in rugged terrain-

00:20:04.252 --> 00:20:05.592
... somewhere tough to invade.

00:20:07.092 --> 00:20:11.052
For instance, our village is Sildi.
It's from the word si, which is like

00:20:11.132 --> 00:20:13.272
a castle or a fortress or a tower.

00:20:14.212 --> 00:20:18.032
They built it that way so an army
couldn't reach them. You'd have to hike

00:20:18.052 --> 00:20:21.392
down one mountain and then
climb up a whole different

00:20:21.452 --> 00:20:25.952
mountain. And right there, they'd
plant things like grain, potatoes,

00:20:26.912 --> 00:20:28.592
basically, you know, all their food.

00:20:29.732 --> 00:20:33.592
See, if we had built over there, if
we lived there and grew our crops

00:20:33.772 --> 00:20:37.492
in the same place, since it's all
terraced land, an army could just walk

00:20:37.572 --> 00:20:41.672
right up, launch raids, and so on. Back
in the day, that's how people lived.

00:20:42.992 --> 00:20:49.532
And so all of this took a toll on people,
physically, psychologically, and mentally.

00:20:50.512 --> 00:20:53.852
And honestly, well, you
just can't be born in these

00:20:53.892 --> 00:20:57.412
conditions, live here, stay
here, and be mentally weak.

00:20:58.592 --> 00:21:01.312
You just have to be tough.
There's no other way to survive.

00:21:02.552 --> 00:21:04.932
- So you just built a sports complex there.

00:21:05.692 --> 00:21:07.832
Pretty legendary, you know.
Could you talk about that?

00:21:08.652 --> 00:21:12.552
- Yes, that was my father's whole
plan. He really wanted it way up in

00:21:12.592 --> 00:21:16.492
the mountains. First, it's great
for the fighters. Right now,

00:21:16.772 --> 00:21:20.612
I just flew out here to Las
Vegas. We're sitting here, like,

00:21:20.752 --> 00:21:24.492
doing this podcast together, but
I came out here for Umar's fight.

00:21:25.712 --> 00:21:31.052
And I should say, Umar did his main
training there at the new base in Sildi.

00:21:32.052 --> 00:21:36.032
He spent almost a month at
that place. And because it's

00:21:36.072 --> 00:21:44.101
1500 to 1600 meters above sea level,
man, it's tough to train there. And

00:21:44.952 --> 00:21:48.812
And the thing is, when you spend
about three to four weeks there,

00:21:48.952 --> 00:21:52.512
you just have more oxygen in
your blood, you have more

00:21:52.552 --> 00:21:56.192
testosterone, you improve
physically, you know, your

00:21:56.392 --> 00:22:00.332
stamina builds up, and so on. And

00:22:00.372 --> 00:22:06.872
this base was sort of my father's dream.
He started building it back in 2016, 2017.

00:22:08.092 --> 00:22:12.572
Money was tight back then. It was really
hard, but we built it little by little.

00:22:13.572 --> 00:22:17.212
In 2020, my father passed
away, but I kept the

00:22:17.252 --> 00:22:21.332
project going just a little
differently, on a bigger scale.

00:22:22.752 --> 00:22:26.512
Right now, the base is open, and
100 people can be there at the

00:22:26.592 --> 00:22:29.632
same time, training, eating, sleeping.

00:22:31.192 --> 00:22:34.892
And our guys hold their
training camps up there.

00:22:37.012 --> 00:22:42.952
So this was sort of my father's dream
that I have pretty much brought to life.

00:22:44.172 --> 00:22:47.982
- It's a powerful story. To return to the
place where you were born up in the

00:22:48.012 --> 00:22:53.392
mountains in order to build a sports
complex where champions train.

00:22:53.472 --> 00:22:57.152
Makhachkala Gym is a great place,
but the mountains are special.

00:22:58.032 --> 00:22:59.002
- Mountains are mountains.

00:22:59.032 --> 00:22:59.772
- Mountains are mountains.

00:22:59.852 --> 00:23:03.722
- Your home village, no matter what,
on some sort of deeper level,

00:23:04.132 --> 00:23:07.832
somehow gives you that, you know,
like Americans love to say,

00:23:07.992 --> 00:23:11.312
positive energy. Not everything
has to be done for money.

00:23:11.792 --> 00:23:15.532
And you know, you shouldn't
just look for profit in

00:23:15.712 --> 00:23:17.392
everything, let's say financially.

00:23:18.312 --> 00:23:22.132
There are certain things that don't
bring me anything at all except for

00:23:22.172 --> 00:23:26.352
expenses, but I still do
all of it because, um,

00:23:27.672 --> 00:23:30.672
because it brings me a lot
of happiness and meaning.

00:23:31.432 --> 00:23:36.752
First of all, it creates a lot of jobs.
People can work and earn a living.

00:23:37.952 --> 00:23:41.452
Second, it's because my father
really wanted this. Third,

00:23:41.992 --> 00:23:45.612
let's say the guys can
train and improve up there,

00:23:45.772 --> 00:23:50.972
both physically and spiritually. There
are just so many benefits there.

00:23:51.052 --> 00:23:54.892
- I don't know if it's okay to ask something
like this, but I would dream of coming

00:23:54.972 --> 00:23:58.852
out there to Dagestan, and not
just to Makhachkala, but to Sildi.

00:23:59.772 --> 00:24:00.852
Is that even possible for me?

00:24:02.792 --> 00:24:06.932
- I think we could film a really good story
out there. I'm not sure about a podcast,

00:24:08.352 --> 00:24:11.812
but I'm sure we could shoot
a really nice vlog there.

00:24:13.232 --> 00:24:17.252
- So can you tell me a bit about the journey
from Makhachkala all the way to Sildi?

00:24:18.084 --> 00:24:19.464
- Four hours. Well, 3:40.

00:24:19.544 --> 00:24:20.054
- Through the mountains?

00:24:20.104 --> 00:24:23.924
- Yeah, 3:30, 3:40 through the
mountains. The road is a real winding

00:24:23.984 --> 00:24:27.744
one, not an easy ride. If it gets to be
too hard for you, we can just take a

00:24:27.754 --> 00:24:33.124
helicopter. It's no problem at
all. Like 40 to 50 minutes from

00:24:33.164 --> 00:24:37.024
Makhachkala to Sildi. Otherwise,
keeping it more down to earth, we go

00:24:37.144 --> 00:24:39.484
by car, so there's absolutely no problem.

00:24:39.884 --> 00:24:43.724
- I could never get used to a wealthy life.
It's just, it's not in my blood to be

00:24:43.824 --> 00:24:44.744
like that.

00:24:44.754 --> 00:24:46.584
- All right, then we'll go by car.

00:24:46.644 --> 00:24:48.764
- Yeah, we could go on horseback,
but a car is better.

00:24:49.604 --> 00:24:52.384
- No, no, by car is better. On
horseback takes way too long.

00:24:52.944 --> 00:24:55.074
- Um, so you mentioned money,

00:24:57.044 --> 00:25:00.533
and we were just talking about comfort
because right now there are so many

00:25:00.564 --> 00:25:03.984
champions. They win so
much. Things are going so

00:25:04.144 --> 00:25:08.084
well. How much harder is it to
stay a champion when the money

00:25:08.094 --> 00:25:11.604
and the fame start coming in, when
all that stuff starts showing up?

00:25:13.564 --> 00:25:17.444
- Well, those are all distracting
things. When you become a champion,

00:25:17.704 --> 00:25:21.564
there's a massive amount of attention
on you. Sometimes it's contracts,

00:25:22.003 --> 00:25:25.804
sometimes you end up having to fly a
lot, you start getting busy with things,

00:25:26.064 --> 00:25:29.864
and little by little you begin to
miss your workouts. You miss one, you

00:25:29.924 --> 00:25:31.284
miss two, you miss three.

00:25:33.144 --> 00:25:37.084
And all of that takes a toll, and the
person who used to train with such

00:25:37.144 --> 00:25:41.124
a drive, with a hunger to be the
best, as soon as they actually

00:25:41.164 --> 00:25:44.964
become the best, they just
don't know what to do next. And

00:25:45.144 --> 00:25:52.984
they live their whole life just
to become, stay on the top.

00:25:54.764 --> 00:25:58.564
As soon as they get there,
what are they supposed to do?

00:25:59.404 --> 00:26:03.324
That's it. They already made it. They've
reached their goal, and there are just so

00:26:03.384 --> 00:26:07.134
many distracting factors:
money, flights, you

00:26:07.164 --> 00:26:11.024
know, meetings, contracts,
video shoots. There

00:26:11.064 --> 00:26:14.844
are just so many distracting
factors. And so if a

00:26:14.884 --> 00:26:17.024
person doesn't know how
to handle all of this,

00:26:18.244 --> 00:26:22.104
then that person comes down from
the top real fast. And I'm saying

00:26:22.144 --> 00:26:26.064
this based on my own experience,
from what I've seen all around me

00:26:26.124 --> 00:26:27.504
with the ones who came before me.

00:26:28.443 --> 00:26:32.244
And back then when I was training
with my father, his students who

00:26:33.384 --> 00:26:37.254
went on to become world champions, just
how they finished their careers and

00:26:37.344 --> 00:26:41.144
so on. And it's what I saw from my

00:26:41.244 --> 00:26:44.764
own observations of other types of
sports, how they came back down,

00:26:45.204 --> 00:26:49.494
as far as I've learned. This is a
very delicate moment. If a person

00:26:50.984 --> 00:26:54.904
climbing all the way to the top, if
they can handle all of that, that

00:26:54.964 --> 00:26:58.684
already shows they have an incredibly
serious mindset. Reaching the

00:26:58.744 --> 00:27:02.554
championship and staying there,
reaching it is one thing, but

00:27:02.624 --> 00:27:06.763
staying a champion, that's totally
different. It's incredibly tough.

00:27:07.264 --> 00:27:12.504
- You're saying that even on a local level,
once you become a champion, that's already

00:27:13.924 --> 00:27:16.244
the tough moment because a lot of people

00:27:17.064 --> 00:27:21.004
who start winning, like you
said, they stop fighting,

00:27:21.104 --> 00:27:26.964
they quit doing MMA and switch over to
fitness. They find themselves that comfort.

00:27:27.984 --> 00:27:32.124
But holding onto it is hard,
even on a local level.

00:27:32.964 --> 00:27:36.944
Every single time you become more
and more of a champion, you won the

00:27:36.984 --> 00:27:41.094
Sambo championship, you keep winning more
and more, and every time it gets harder.

00:27:42.784 --> 00:27:46.564
- Yes, and it depends on the person's
goals. If you want to become an

00:27:46.624 --> 00:27:50.524
amateur champion just to prove
something to yourself, that's one

00:27:50.564 --> 00:27:54.504
thing. But if you view amateur
sports as a springboard

00:27:54.544 --> 00:27:58.404
to jump right into professional
sports and become a champion among

00:27:58.444 --> 00:28:01.984
professionals, that's a whole
different thing. There are different

00:28:02.044 --> 00:28:05.924
paths. First, you build an
amateur career just to get

00:28:05.984 --> 00:28:09.764
into the pros and go even
further from there. Second,

00:28:10.224 --> 00:28:13.904
after you've made it into the
pros, you become a champion. And

00:28:14.044 --> 00:28:17.704
third, after you become a
champion, you have to stay there.

00:28:18.614 --> 00:28:22.564
Not everyone has what it takes. It is
incredibly tough. If you look at the

00:28:22.604 --> 00:28:26.414
numbers, you can literally count these
champions on one hand. How many of

00:28:26.444 --> 00:28:29.924
them are there? Maybe 15, 20, 30 people?

00:28:30.364 --> 00:28:34.183
Let's say 50 over the last
30 years, and how many have

00:28:34.224 --> 00:28:37.744
tried? For example, 50
achieved it over 30 years,

00:28:38.324 --> 00:28:41.944
over 35 years of MMA combat sports.

00:28:42.324 --> 00:28:46.244
But how many tried? Hundreds of
thousands. And look at that number. Such

00:28:46.304 --> 00:28:48.464
a tiny amount actually
reached the end goal.

00:28:49.924 --> 00:28:53.804
So for you to actually make it
into this tiny list, you really

00:28:53.864 --> 00:28:57.844
have to be like just a truly unique,

00:28:58.844 --> 00:29:02.654
person and a fighter and,
and you know, a student,

00:29:04.024 --> 00:29:07.824
and a person who can accept
guidance from a coach

00:29:07.904 --> 00:29:11.624
who when they are being
guided, discard the cons and

00:29:11.724 --> 00:29:15.464
take only the pros. But there
are people who take criticism

00:29:15.584 --> 00:29:19.384
very hard and very negatively,
and are very sensitive,

00:29:19.444 --> 00:29:22.364
let's say, when they take criticism-

00:29:22.644 --> 00:29:29.584
... from a mentor. So it all has to click,
and some accept criticism at the very

00:29:29.664 --> 00:29:34.373
beginning, but then once they become
champions, it's hard for them to take it.

00:29:34.744 --> 00:29:38.704
It happens differently. A man
sometimes doesn't even know what

00:29:38.724 --> 00:29:42.184
he is capable of. Sometimes
he just can't really

00:29:42.284 --> 00:29:45.944
understand himself until
he achieves success or

00:29:46.024 --> 00:29:49.724
becomes famous, wealthy,
and powerful. That

00:29:49.844 --> 00:29:53.064
is exactly when he discovers
his true qualities.

00:29:54.264 --> 00:29:58.204
When he is limited in something,
in money, in power, in

00:29:58.284 --> 00:30:00.664
everything that success
eventually brings him,

00:30:02.184 --> 00:30:05.484
he doesn't really know
how he's going to act

00:30:07.364 --> 00:30:11.304
in this new role, doesn't know
how he'll behave once he becomes

00:30:11.384 --> 00:30:15.254
big, famous, and a champion.
Nobody knows. Some people

00:30:15.404 --> 00:30:20.024
say and wish, "Oh, if only I'd made it
like that, I would do things this way."

00:30:20.840 --> 00:30:24.800
I think it's better if people
don't throw around such big words

00:30:25.900 --> 00:30:30.200
because you never know how you'll carry
yourself once you actually get there.

00:30:31.420 --> 00:30:36.919
You know, it's such a delicate thing,
getting up there and still remaining human.

00:30:39.120 --> 00:30:43.100
- When it comes to you, I see that
everyone here just loves you. Even early

00:30:43.160 --> 00:30:47.100
on in your career, millions of
people loved you. That is also a,

00:30:48.920 --> 00:30:53.100
a tough thing to deal with because
maybe you start believing it.

00:30:53.360 --> 00:30:58.180
And you can lose yourself in all of
it. So is it all in the mind? I mean,

00:30:59.240 --> 00:31:03.360
what actually goes on in a
champion's head? In yours

00:31:04.500 --> 00:31:08.560
when you sat alone thinking about
your life, "Who am I really,

00:31:09.680 --> 00:31:13.100
and what do I do to keep being
a champion or to become one?"

00:31:14.700 --> 00:31:18.320
- Well, first of all, from the very
beginning, if you realize that what you're

00:31:18.380 --> 00:31:22.180
chasing isn't the most important
thing in life, then it gets

00:31:22.320 --> 00:31:25.820
easier for you. And then, you
know, there are some people

00:31:25.980 --> 00:31:32.260
whose, let's say, ultimate goal is to be
successful, powerful, and to make it big.

00:31:33.140 --> 00:31:38.640
At the end of the day, after all, we are
not really such big, important people.

00:31:39.840 --> 00:31:47.000
And, like, I believe it's wrong to
just crown yourself king because,

00:31:49.460 --> 00:31:55.560
uh if we put it, to put it bluntly,
if we're being honest here,

00:31:57.020 --> 00:32:04.860
we can't even control the basic need to go
to the restroom. Maybe to some... Yeah.

00:32:05.780 --> 00:32:09.740
To some people this will probably
seem a bit crude, but we all go

00:32:09.800 --> 00:32:13.420
to the restroom, excuse my
language. If we can't control

00:32:13.580 --> 00:32:16.060
even this, so what can we actually

00:32:16.980 --> 00:32:20.900
control in this life? Look how many
people we know, how many great

00:32:21.120 --> 00:32:24.480
people have left this earth. It
doesn't really matter how, like,

00:32:25.160 --> 00:32:29.120
big, strong, or famous you are, how
wealthy you are, champion or no

00:32:29.160 --> 00:32:32.740
champion. Either way, they put us all
in the exact same grave, you know,

00:32:33.060 --> 00:32:36.940
two meters down or, like, a
meter or a meter and a half.

00:32:37.460 --> 00:32:41.420
Different confessions, different religions,
they bury people in different ways.

00:32:42.320 --> 00:32:47.460
So I actually believe that every single
person should have this realization that we

00:32:48.640 --> 00:32:52.380
are just not some kind of big, important

00:32:52.440 --> 00:32:57.560
people here who just decide and get to
control everything. We don't get to do that.

00:32:59.440 --> 00:33:04.340
In my case, I was raised under the guidance
of my father. My father was a coach,

00:33:05.600 --> 00:33:12.860
and my goal was always to become... To prove
to my father that out of everyone, even

00:33:12.940 --> 00:33:17.840
though he trains many other people, he
trains everyone, I mean, I'm also here.

00:33:18.680 --> 00:33:22.660
I can show him. He doesn't have to
look very far. I'm right there,

00:33:23.260 --> 00:33:27.140
and under his guidance, I
can become a champion. I

00:33:27.220 --> 00:33:32.130
looked up to him and followed his example,
trained hard, and it was like, you know,

00:33:33.980 --> 00:33:37.860
a kind of a challenge to
prove to him that, "Look, I

00:33:37.920 --> 00:33:41.760
am right here, Father, and
everything you teach, I will take it

00:33:41.860 --> 00:33:45.740
all in zealously, worthily.
I'll follow your program,

00:33:45.860 --> 00:33:49.510
do it right, and I will become
a champion." And that was

00:33:49.520 --> 00:33:53.500
exactly the kind of goal I had,
to prove it first and foremost

00:33:53.570 --> 00:33:57.480
to my father and secondly to
the entire world that I can

00:33:57.560 --> 00:34:01.380
become the best wrestler
or fighter. But I never

00:34:02.520 --> 00:34:06.340
thought of being crowned
king after I became a

00:34:06.380 --> 00:34:10.280
champion. I was never going to
walk around like, "Look how cool

00:34:10.400 --> 00:34:14.100
I am." That was never in my
head, and I'm really glad

00:34:14.120 --> 00:34:18.010
that the Almighty protected me
from all of that because many

00:34:18.100 --> 00:34:21.240
people end up in a bad place
if they act like that.

00:34:22.930 --> 00:34:25.940
- This is a profound wisdom, not
thinking about being crowned.

00:34:26.860 --> 00:34:30.600
But even if you don't, a fire should
burn within you, driving you to

00:34:30.640 --> 00:34:34.380
become a champion, right? As you
said, hundreds of thousands,

00:34:34.460 --> 00:34:37.820
millions of people want to be a
champion, but only one can be.

00:34:37.880 --> 00:34:41.660
- Yeah, but that is actually
about you confronting your

00:34:41.720 --> 00:34:45.680
opponent. You've got to put
a barrier inside your mind

00:34:46.040 --> 00:34:50.009
where on one side you're a
humble person, but on the

00:34:50.080 --> 00:34:53.920
other side you're different because
you have a goal, your goal.

00:34:54.440 --> 00:34:58.220
Of course, you should still follow a
code of honor. You should be a man,

00:34:58.400 --> 00:35:03.380
a righteous man, and know what you can
and cannot do. You should always do that.

00:35:04.280 --> 00:35:09.800
But in the end, your opponent is the
person who wants to stop your dream.

00:35:10.140 --> 00:35:13.540
So here the boundaries
expand just a little bit.

00:35:14.040 --> 00:35:17.700
Right here, you're already not playing
humble. Right here, you just step out

00:35:18.080 --> 00:35:25.430
and you want to clear your opponent out
of your way, and that's it, because your

00:35:25.520 --> 00:35:28.660
goal is to be the best in the
world and they are blocking you.

00:35:29.180 --> 00:35:34.920
Excuse me, but in this situation,
there's no way you can act all humble.

00:35:35.820 --> 00:35:39.220
- But when you were training in the gym,
what was it that gave you this strength?

00:35:39.980 --> 00:35:42.920
A hatred of losing or a love for winning?

00:35:45.160 --> 00:35:49.100
- I think it was the hatred
of losing. Yes, definitely.

00:35:49.720 --> 00:35:53.680
I mean, you know, of course you
want to win, but for me there

00:35:53.720 --> 00:35:59.700
was always this driver like, "No
way. I am not losing," you know.

00:36:00.540 --> 00:36:04.420
When they would give me a fight, I always
tell this to my guys, whenever they

00:36:04.440 --> 00:36:09.180
would give me a fight on a
professional level, 100 days before it

00:36:10.900 --> 00:36:14.830
I was... How do you say it? Addiction.

00:36:16.040 --> 00:36:19.940
I was fixated like when people
have this addiction. But for me it

00:36:20.000 --> 00:36:23.720
was commitment, that I absolutely
had to prepare perfectly for the

00:36:23.840 --> 00:36:27.548
fight. I couldn't miss any training
sessions. I would literally have,

00:36:27.988 --> 00:36:31.908
like, this panic. Like, for
example, I'd fly somewhere and

00:36:32.028 --> 00:36:35.928
I have, let's say, eighty-five
days left until the fight.

00:36:36.288 --> 00:36:39.868
Like, I knew that I had to fly.
For example, if I was flying to

00:36:39.908 --> 00:36:43.268
Moscow, I would always
pick a ticket so I could

00:36:43.368 --> 00:36:47.038
absolutely do my morning
training session first. Then I'd

00:36:47.248 --> 00:36:50.868
take off around lunchtime. And
once I arrived in Moscow, for

00:36:50.928 --> 00:36:54.938
whatever reason, I had to fit it
all around my training schedule.

00:36:56.508 --> 00:37:00.478
Either I had to meet up before my training
sessions or I had to meet up after my

00:37:00.508 --> 00:37:04.208
training sessions. And I simply could
not miss that evening training session

00:37:04.328 --> 00:37:08.088
because I had to train during
the day from four to five

00:37:08.148 --> 00:37:11.768
hours, two in the morning and two
to two and a half in the evening.

00:37:12.368 --> 00:37:16.128
And I needed to I would prepare
myself that way. Looking

00:37:16.208 --> 00:37:20.168
at who was in Moscow right then,
wondering who I could, like

00:37:20.548 --> 00:37:24.348
pull into a training session there. Well,
they also had to-- the person had to

00:37:24.408 --> 00:37:28.268
match my level as well. Not just like I
showed up there, you know, played around with

00:37:28.308 --> 00:37:29.668
him. I needed a fight back.

00:37:31.248 --> 00:37:35.148
I would arrange in advance who was gonna
come. Maybe some sparring, maybe I'd do

00:37:35.168 --> 00:37:38.828
wrestling, maybe I'd hit the
weights, maybe a striking workout.

00:37:40.368 --> 00:37:43.888
So I had this, let's say I
always had this tough and

00:37:43.968 --> 00:37:49.968
scientific approach to it. So I believe
that more than anything, I didn't want to

00:37:51.188 --> 00:37:54.908
lose rather than I wanted to win,
you know? Of course, winning is

00:37:54.988 --> 00:37:58.228
also a huge pleasure, but more than
anything, I just didn't want to lose.

00:37:59.268 --> 00:38:03.068
- So in all those hard training sessions,
you were thinking about not losing.

00:38:03.288 --> 00:38:04.088
That's the fuel.

00:38:04.098 --> 00:38:04.398
- The fuel.

00:38:04.828 --> 00:38:06.388
- Four to five hours every single day.

00:38:06.448 --> 00:38:06.968
- Every day.

00:38:07.328 --> 00:38:10.968
- There is a story that I heard. Sadulaev,

00:38:11.328 --> 00:38:15.048
the legendary wrestler, told a
story that he went to a hotel,

00:38:15.648 --> 00:38:19.228
I think it was in Moscow, to
train in the gym, and he was the

00:38:19.328 --> 00:38:23.998
only-- It was during the night, I don't
know. He was the only one who was there.

00:38:24.948 --> 00:38:30.088
And you came down- ... And you actually
trained together. And he said he, he

00:38:30.128 --> 00:38:33.168
said a lot of great things about
you. He said that you gave him some

00:38:33.208 --> 00:38:40.928
really deep wisdom, which helped him to
win the gold medal, at the Olympic Games.

00:38:42.208 --> 00:38:46.128
I'm curious, could you tell us now what you
told him? What kind of wisdom did you give

00:38:46.168 --> 00:38:46.308
him?

00:38:47.768 --> 00:38:50.628
- I can't remember exactly, but we
had really great conversations.

00:38:51.028 --> 00:38:54.748
- It's a great story that the two of
you, like two legends like that, just

00:38:54.828 --> 00:38:56.188
met completely by chance.

00:38:56.788 --> 00:38:59.028
- Well, look, he's a two-time
Olympic champion, right?

00:38:59.508 --> 00:38:59.678
- Right.

00:39:00.468 --> 00:39:04.128
- I think he doesn't skip his training
either. He never missed one back

00:39:04.228 --> 00:39:08.108
then. I believe that we both
ended up there because we both,

00:39:08.468 --> 00:39:12.308
you know, hate to lose,
and we both really want to

00:39:12.348 --> 00:39:16.128
win. And, you know, recently
I came across this idea.

00:39:17.828 --> 00:39:21.688
There are athletes who simply want
to win, and then there are some for

00:39:21.768 --> 00:39:25.448
whom victory isn't enough.
They want domination.

00:39:25.768 --> 00:39:29.738
I believe that he is one of those as
well. They want domination. And for

00:39:29.828 --> 00:39:33.188
that, what does a person have
to do? They have to train.

00:39:34.568 --> 00:39:37.068
They have to grind morning and night. And

00:39:38.768 --> 00:39:42.628
there was this thing with us.
After the morning, my father never

00:39:42.668 --> 00:39:46.448
let us sleep. When I say the
morning, I mean after Fajr,

00:39:46.688 --> 00:39:50.628
the dawn prayer. He just wouldn't
sleep himself, so we couldn't either.

00:39:51.248 --> 00:39:54.668
So every morning, six, seven,
we'd be out doing our warm-up.

00:39:55.868 --> 00:39:59.788
Some people might call it a warm-up, but
when you show up at the stadium in the

00:39:59.848 --> 00:40:03.768
morning, you run five kilometers,
you do sprints, you work with

00:40:03.828 --> 00:40:07.548
resistance bands, you work on the
pull-up bar, you do push-ups,

00:40:07.928 --> 00:40:15.328
dips, jumps, you know the bands then
work with a partner, drilling and so on.

00:40:15.748 --> 00:40:19.468
That's about two hours of work.
You drop two, two and a half kilos

00:40:19.548 --> 00:40:23.388
there. Not really a warm-up, but
Father always called it that, right?

00:40:23.888 --> 00:40:27.788
That was a full morning training
at seven AM. Then I came

00:40:27.808 --> 00:40:30.928
home to rest, and at four
o'clock, we'd head to the gym.

00:40:32.008 --> 00:40:35.788
The training would start at
five o'clock and end at seven.

00:40:36.408 --> 00:40:40.068
By eight, I'd be home, and
by eleven, I'd rest up.

00:40:41.068 --> 00:40:44.088
And yet I kept thinking
that something was missing.

00:40:45.588 --> 00:40:49.428
Like, I know that I have a start,
a competition coming up in

00:40:49.468 --> 00:40:53.368
like forty or thirty days, and I would
wake up and I'd be like, "Let me go

00:40:53.408 --> 00:40:57.328
to the sea. I'm gonna go down to the
sea, do some running, some sprints on

00:40:57.348 --> 00:41:01.308
the sand, work out, and so on."
And so many times, I ended

00:41:01.348 --> 00:41:03.588
up going out for a third
training session as well.

00:41:05.128 --> 00:41:08.828
Sometimes Father would say, "Rest. In
the morning, we're gonna have a heavy

00:41:08.838 --> 00:41:12.618
workload." But sometimes I wouldn't
tell Father. I'd just go out at

00:41:12.648 --> 00:41:16.598
night, head out, and train. At
night when everyone's asleep, when

00:41:16.628 --> 00:41:20.608
nobody's around, I'd grab one of my
close friends, give them a call, have

00:41:20.628 --> 00:41:24.487
them pick me up, then hop in the
car and head to the sea. I'd train

00:41:24.528 --> 00:41:27.928
for an hour or an hour and a
half, come back, and crash.

00:41:28.308 --> 00:41:33.248
Next morning, I'd get up and start all over
again. And just this routine, nonstop.

00:41:34.128 --> 00:41:37.838
And when I see guys, you know, when
they do just one training session or

00:41:37.888 --> 00:41:41.648
like they say, "On Wednesday, we
have a morning training, then an

00:41:41.708 --> 00:41:42.698
evening sauna session."

00:41:43.948 --> 00:41:47.748
What do you mean? You have to
fight in fifty days. You have a

00:41:47.788 --> 00:41:51.088
huge fight ahead and you want to take
a rest in the middle of the week?

00:41:52.408 --> 00:41:53.468
Well, rest on Sunday.

00:41:54.608 --> 00:41:58.408
On Saturday, do a morning training, hit the
sauna in the evening, take a complete rest on

00:41:58.468 --> 00:42:00.427
Sunday, and start all
over again on Monday.

00:42:02.088 --> 00:42:06.008
That was just my approach. Everyone
does it differently. I don't know.

00:42:06.088 --> 00:42:10.808
I just think it has to be in your head.
You've got to want it incredibly badly.

00:42:12.328 --> 00:42:16.308
- So you believe that to be a champion, you have
to train like that, you have to be that serious

00:42:17.068 --> 00:42:17.928
for the majority of people?

00:42:18.588 --> 00:42:24.668
- Let's say I believe that to be
at the top, the top tier, right?

00:42:25.528 --> 00:42:27.348
One of the best in the world

00:42:28.996 --> 00:42:32.756
A person has to always be, you
know, a little bit, let's say

00:42:32.856 --> 00:42:36.176
just a little bit crazy,
but in a good sense of the

00:42:36.196 --> 00:42:40.136
word. You have to be addicted to
it and to want this incredibly

00:42:40.216 --> 00:42:43.936
bad. You have to take a fanatical
approach to the training process.

00:42:45.576 --> 00:42:49.056
Just showing up and working
out, that's what hundreds of

00:42:49.136 --> 00:42:52.996
thousands of guys are doing around
the world. To stand out from

00:42:53.116 --> 00:42:56.956
that, from this crowd, let's
say, who have the exact

00:42:57.036 --> 00:43:00.956
same goals as you do, to
stand out from that, you have

00:43:01.015 --> 00:43:03.836
to take a fanatical approach to it.

00:43:03.876 --> 00:43:07.276
- Well, look, I'm training
with John Danaher.

00:43:07.316 --> 00:43:10.356
And they train like that too.
They train three times a day.

00:43:10.756 --> 00:43:14.416
And it actually feels right this way. I mean,
of course, this is how you have to train.

00:43:15.376 --> 00:43:17.056
- 'Cause of that, John has one of the best-

00:43:17.556 --> 00:43:18.345
- Yeah, he makes champions

00:43:18.356 --> 00:43:21.536
- ... students in the world, and after all,
they don't just take some random place.

00:43:21.636 --> 00:43:23.476
I mean, they're not
somewhere in the middle.

00:43:24.636 --> 00:43:29.756
They're at the top, and look at
their whole approach. Some people...

00:43:31.976 --> 00:43:35.916
Look, a person with that kind of approach
just can't be somewhere in the middle. It's

00:43:35.976 --> 00:43:41.336
impossible. If you have a fanatical
attitude, the right attitude toward

00:43:41.416 --> 00:43:46.056
the training process, on top of that,
you have to be fanatical about it.

00:43:47.516 --> 00:43:49.796
You love what you do, and in the end,

00:43:51.196 --> 00:43:55.116
there will be results. Well, just look
at John and look where his guys are.

00:43:55.896 --> 00:44:00.556
He creates champions. He finds a champion
in everyone, across every category, right?

00:44:01.836 --> 00:44:05.616
- He creates champions, not just one or two,

00:44:05.696 --> 00:44:10.956
but all of them. He finds a champion
in everyone, across every category.

00:44:11.396 --> 00:44:14.996
- Look, the people who come to him,
he has a system, let's say, right?

00:44:15.816 --> 00:44:19.356
Under this roof, under his
roof, there is a system.

00:44:19.676 --> 00:44:23.276
People come here, and they
become champions in this system.

00:44:24.036 --> 00:44:29.516
So there are people there who are champions.
These are specific established names.

00:44:30.516 --> 00:44:33.476
And there are other
people who came and left.

00:44:34.696 --> 00:44:39.776
They entered that black room, so to say,
an uncomfortable place. They went in,

00:44:40.736 --> 00:44:44.556
stood for a year, a year and a
half or two, and left. Nobody

00:44:44.596 --> 00:44:48.136
talks about them. These are the
people who came in, broke mentally,

00:44:48.536 --> 00:44:52.396
and left, and there aren't just
a few of them. There are a lot.

00:44:52.796 --> 00:44:56.576
Usually, most people give up.
There are many who give up, but

00:44:56.616 --> 00:45:00.136
then there are the ones who come
in and stay there for a long time.

00:45:00.896 --> 00:45:07.296
Long, long time. And after some time, they
achieve success. They become the best.

00:45:08.536 --> 00:45:12.496
They prove to themselves that once
they enter the system, they could stay

00:45:12.556 --> 00:45:16.056
there for a long time,
endure, and become the best.

00:45:16.916 --> 00:45:19.736
They prove that they are
already capable of many things.

00:45:21.156 --> 00:45:24.896
They can be trusted to supervise
big, important projects.

00:45:26.296 --> 00:45:30.216
I would even say, for example,
2,000 years ago, they

00:45:30.276 --> 00:45:32.696
could easily rule entire cities.

00:45:34.316 --> 00:45:38.276
So a person who has gone through
it physically, who has lived

00:45:38.286 --> 00:45:44.476
through it, who has faced challenges, passed
the tests, they are capable of a lot,

00:45:45.296 --> 00:45:48.996
highly capable. You don't just walk
into martial arts and become the

00:45:49.056 --> 00:45:52.076
best. It's very difficult. It means that

00:45:52.896 --> 00:45:56.736
you go through pain, you
make sacrifices, you give

00:45:56.796 --> 00:46:00.436
your time, you face setbacks
and failures in training.

00:46:01.025 --> 00:46:04.956
The mental strength and
capacity mean a lot there. And

00:46:05.096 --> 00:46:08.736
when I talk about his system,
look, I mean those schools that

00:46:08.776 --> 00:46:12.276
became the best, let's
say, in football or in

00:46:12.356 --> 00:46:16.316
freestyle wrestling or, let's
say, in MMA, in grappling,

00:46:16.476 --> 00:46:20.356
or in judo. It's really hard
to enter one of the best

00:46:20.416 --> 00:46:23.696
schools in the world, to stay
when they are breaking you,

00:46:23.996 --> 00:46:27.616
throwing you, choking you,
to improve as a person

00:46:27.856 --> 00:46:31.796
and as an athlete, and to
become a champion. It takes

00:46:31.856 --> 00:46:38.096
at least 12 years, 10 to 12 years,
maybe 15. For some, it took longer.

00:46:39.036 --> 00:46:42.836
And to get through the grind
for these 15 years and

00:46:42.896 --> 00:46:46.396
then to break through and
become the best is worth a lot.

00:46:47.596 --> 00:46:52.896
- Have you had those tough moments where
you're almost broken psychologically?

00:46:53.956 --> 00:46:57.236
You know, just sitting there,
finding it hard to breathe,

00:46:57.956 --> 00:47:02.186
and everything is going wrong,
lost in some situations.

00:47:03.556 --> 00:47:06.346
And when you were young, was
there ever a moment like that?

00:47:08.056 --> 00:47:10.876
'Cause the guys from Dagestan on the team,

00:47:11.936 --> 00:47:16.656
really great guys, by the way, all say
they've never seen you broken in training,

00:47:18.076 --> 00:47:22.396
not even losing really. But when
you were young, did it ever happen?

00:47:23.376 --> 00:47:27.276
- Look, I've been in this
grind since I was a kid.

00:47:27.336 --> 00:47:31.176
Wherever my father coached, there
were always... Let's take the

00:47:31.256 --> 00:47:35.096
year 2004, for example.
My father put together

00:47:35.136 --> 00:47:39.076
the Dagestan national team for
combat Sambo and in mixed martial

00:47:39.086 --> 00:47:42.836
arts, which was called
no-holds-barred fighting. There were

00:47:42.896 --> 00:47:48.276
training camps, too. I was 15.
It was the winter of 2004,

00:47:49.576 --> 00:47:53.316
and I was the youngest guy
there. The others were, let's

00:47:53.376 --> 00:47:57.196
say, between 22 and 30, all active

00:47:57.236 --> 00:48:01.696
world champions. At that time,
I was, like, 14, 15 years old.

00:48:03.516 --> 00:48:08.676
I wrestled with them, grappled, was in
this uncomfortable environment for myself,

00:48:09.736 --> 00:48:13.666
where we always had the rule that
the youngest has to do everything,

00:48:13.876 --> 00:48:19.126
and he is always wrong, and
I had to deal with that.

00:48:19.156 --> 00:48:22.956
Especially in the gym, they throw
you, hit you again and again.

00:48:23.316 --> 00:48:27.096
You have to show up morning and
evening, day after day. This

00:48:27.196 --> 00:48:31.136
place hardens you. They say
How the steel was tempered.

00:48:31.416 --> 00:48:35.396
Steel isn't tempered for nothing.
How many times do you have to

00:48:35.576 --> 00:48:40.576
strike the steel before it becomes a
sword? That doesn't just happen by itself.

00:48:41.176 --> 00:48:42.536
- Did you believe in yourself then?

00:48:43.436 --> 00:48:47.116
- Some of the guys who were at those
camps back then, they're grown men

00:48:47.156 --> 00:48:50.836
now. Even then, they were older than me by

00:48:50.896 --> 00:48:55.656
10, 12, maybe 15 years. And they
always used to say, "Khabib,

00:48:56.456 --> 00:48:59.816
we honestly thought you were
going to become some kind of

00:48:59.956 --> 00:49:03.616
scholar or scientist." Because my father

00:49:03.736 --> 00:49:07.036
was always making me read
and write, read and write.

00:49:09.156 --> 00:49:13.076
I would copy out entire books
by hand. There was always

00:49:13.116 --> 00:49:16.796
this control. It wasn't like
he'd say, "Khabib, go do this",

00:49:17.276 --> 00:49:21.036
and you could just walk away and not
do it. When he said, "Go do it", you

00:49:21.156 --> 00:49:24.596
did it. My father never
forgot anything. He had a

00:49:24.676 --> 00:49:28.496
phenomenal memory. He'd be like,
"Did you do it?" And everything he

00:49:28.536 --> 00:49:30.516
told you, he remembered. He just did.

00:49:31.116 --> 00:49:31.696
- He checked you.

00:49:31.876 --> 00:49:35.856
- He was like, "Come here." And he'd
check. I knew it wasn't something

00:49:35.956 --> 00:49:39.756
I could get away with. So,
you know, since childhood, I

00:49:39.816 --> 00:49:43.736
was lucky that my father
always kept me close, and I

00:49:44.596 --> 00:49:50.216
found myself in this environment
among fighters who at that time-

00:49:50.556 --> 00:49:54.456
... were, I don't know, they just didn't
compete in America and Europe back

00:49:54.496 --> 00:49:58.836
then. But in the post-Soviet space,
they were the best at that time.

00:50:00.036 --> 00:50:03.176
And it helped me a lot to
become mentally strong.

00:50:04.796 --> 00:50:08.896
- Tell me more about your
father. You've read a lot.

00:50:09.916 --> 00:50:13.726
You didn't just fight. You were well-rounded
and you told several stories about

00:50:13.796 --> 00:50:18.776
this. There was a book, what is
it called? Countries and Peoples.

00:50:19.616 --> 00:50:20.696
- Yes, from my childhood.

00:50:20.936 --> 00:50:21.516
- It is, really.

00:50:22.016 --> 00:50:22.216
- Yes.

00:50:22.676 --> 00:50:23.426
- And it went on-

00:50:23.816 --> 00:50:25.456
- The approach was the same.

00:50:25.876 --> 00:50:29.296
- Tell that story because I think it's
important for the parents listening to us

00:50:29.796 --> 00:50:33.116
to understand this. You're
not just trying to create a

00:50:33.176 --> 00:50:36.916
champion. You're trying to
raise a well-rounded person, a

00:50:36.956 --> 00:50:39.336
wise person, a person with depth.

00:50:40.316 --> 00:50:44.076
- Yes, you have to give the right
motivation to kids with proper

00:50:44.176 --> 00:50:47.946
communication. My father
was very good at this. I

00:50:47.976 --> 00:50:51.756
now realize interacting with
my own kids how wise he

00:50:51.816 --> 00:50:54.656
was and how deeply he understood this.

00:50:55.936 --> 00:50:59.756
Because when a child is eight,
10 years old, and when they are

00:50:59.816 --> 00:51:03.766
just beginning to form as a
person, it is important to have

00:51:03.816 --> 00:51:08.726
the right approach. I now understand how
farsighted my father's approach was.

00:51:09.446 --> 00:51:13.236
First, he tried not to give
me anything for free. Only

00:51:13.696 --> 00:51:17.496
sometimes I could get something
for free. For example, before a

00:51:17.536 --> 00:51:21.456
holiday, like Eid al-Fitr or before school

00:51:21.516 --> 00:51:25.136
started, Father would
give money. I'd go buy

00:51:25.196 --> 00:51:28.916
clothes at the market for back to
school, that kind of thing, you know?

00:51:29.496 --> 00:51:33.356
And at other times, he always told me,
"Well, if you want it, you need to

00:51:33.416 --> 00:51:36.396
earn it. You want me to give
you money just like that?"

00:51:37.456 --> 00:51:41.056
There would be some work around the
house and so on that I had to do.

00:51:41.736 --> 00:51:44.836
And I knew that encouragement
would always be there.

00:51:45.576 --> 00:51:49.276
My father always had this kind
of approach. He set a very

00:51:49.336 --> 00:51:53.296
strong personal example, very
strong, and I really learnt a

00:51:53.376 --> 00:51:57.156
lot from him. Took a lot.
And then, as you mentioned,

00:51:57.576 --> 00:52:01.216
there was the book, Countries
and Peoples. Someone gave it to

00:52:01.256 --> 00:52:05.176
him as a gift. My father read
through it, and he realized

00:52:05.236 --> 00:52:13.136
that it was perfect for me, for a child
who was 10, 11, maybe 12 at the time.

00:52:13.236 --> 00:52:18.436
That book was part of my life for about
two or three years, something like that.

00:52:18.476 --> 00:52:23.396
- It was about 193 countries. The
geography, history, all the details.

00:52:23.936 --> 00:52:27.576
- For each country, it describes
its geographical location,

00:52:27.916 --> 00:52:31.756
which countries it bordered,
its major rivers, mountains,

00:52:32.076 --> 00:52:35.496
cities, history, economy,
finances. Just brief

00:52:35.556 --> 00:52:39.416
information about every country.
My father said, "You'll

00:52:39.456 --> 00:52:42.616
tell me about each country, and
I'll give you money for it."

00:52:44.116 --> 00:52:45.356
And I thought, "All right."

00:52:46.176 --> 00:52:50.396
I knew this was like my own little capital
just sitting there waiting for me.

00:52:51.376 --> 00:52:55.316
All I had to do was come, take
that book, read it, understand

00:52:55.376 --> 00:52:57.806
it. Then my father would
take the book and say,

00:52:58.756 --> 00:53:01.426
"Now, tell me what you
read." And he would check.

00:53:02.356 --> 00:53:06.316
He would look through it. The countries
were arranged alphabetically. A

00:53:06.356 --> 00:53:10.296
for Andorra, Angola, and so
on. B for Belgium, Brazil, and

00:53:10.356 --> 00:53:14.216
so on. Everything went in
alphabetical order. I started

00:53:14.276 --> 00:53:17.196
from A and read them all
straight to the very end.

00:53:18.076 --> 00:53:21.996
Like every single time when
I needed the money, I did

00:53:22.005 --> 00:53:25.996
that. And for example, I go
over the first 40 countries,

00:53:26.336 --> 00:53:32.216
he'd check a box, and then I continued.
That's how my father would test me.

00:53:34.316 --> 00:53:40.756
Let's just say that my knowledge
of geography comes from back then.

00:53:41.916 --> 00:53:43.896
- Did you realize how big the world was?

00:53:44.816 --> 00:53:44.976
- No.

00:53:45.096 --> 00:53:46.476
- As a child, you don't understand?

00:53:46.736 --> 00:53:50.676
- At that time, I didn't understand.
But since then, I've had this desire

00:53:50.696 --> 00:53:54.376
to travel. I really wanted
to travel, visit different

00:53:54.436 --> 00:53:58.676
countries, see wonders of the
world, the Colosseum, the pyramids.

00:53:59.576 --> 00:54:03.856
But all of this is from childhood,
from what I read and wrote.

00:54:04.996 --> 00:54:09.996
It wasn't just about reading. I also had
to rewrite it. I had to write it down.

00:54:10.876 --> 00:54:14.656
I always wrote down what
I read. And actually,

00:54:16.396 --> 00:54:20.316
this is very good advice for
parents, you know. They can use this

00:54:20.356 --> 00:54:27.556
with their children too. They can tell
them to sit down and copy books by hand.

00:54:28.376 --> 00:54:35.472
First of all, eyesight improves. Reading
improves. Writing, how you write improves.

00:54:36.112 --> 00:54:39.652
Your handwriting. And it stays
with you when you read and write.

00:54:41.052 --> 00:54:45.902
It really develops a child.
Not only I believe that,

00:54:46.772 --> 00:54:50.532
I actually do advise parents
to motivate their kids

00:54:50.592 --> 00:54:55.012
this way. You can simply
create some kind of challenge

00:54:55.932 --> 00:55:01.212
that children benefit from and
reward them for it after it is done.

00:55:02.512 --> 00:55:05.312
This really helps develop a child's mind.

00:55:06.592 --> 00:55:10.192
- I actually saw advice
from the great writers

00:55:11.312 --> 00:55:14.572
who said that they remembered doing
the same thing when they were young.

00:55:15.492 --> 00:55:18.372
When they first started writing,
they just simply copied

00:55:19.232 --> 00:55:25.152
other legendary writers. You read their
work and then you type it out yourself,

00:55:26.052 --> 00:55:28.852
and you start absorbing that
style into your bloodstream.

00:55:29.662 --> 00:55:32.772
- Well, it needs to be done in childhood.

00:55:32.832 --> 00:55:36.312
When the child is ready to
learn and his mind is open,

00:55:37.232 --> 00:55:40.672
let's say as a metaphor,
when his table is clean,

00:55:41.072 --> 00:55:44.712
not occupied with clutter,
then as a father,

00:55:45.172 --> 00:55:49.732
you can help them place all the right
things on the table in their proper place.

00:55:51.272 --> 00:55:57.192
Let's say put the food in one place,
flowers in another, something

00:55:57.252 --> 00:56:04.312
like that. It is important. That's
why I believe I was very lucky. My

00:56:04.412 --> 00:56:08.642
father did the right thing. He provided
me with the right kind of information,

00:56:09.712 --> 00:56:13.472
and I learned a lot just
from being around him. I

00:56:13.532 --> 00:56:15.292
learned so much just from his presence.

00:56:16.392 --> 00:56:20.572
He was there at home, and then
we also went to competitions.

00:56:21.692 --> 00:56:27.432
He was always there at trainings and at
camps, so I absorbed a lot from him,

00:56:28.572 --> 00:56:32.212
and it was interesting
for me too. Of course,

00:56:34.132 --> 00:56:37.972
when you're a teenager, sometimes
you're tired, sometimes you want to

00:56:37.992 --> 00:56:41.992
break free. All of that was there. But

00:56:44.132 --> 00:56:48.652
my father had the right approach. He
influenced me a lot and in the right way.

00:56:49.392 --> 00:56:52.112
- And there's the physical
component too of hard work.

00:56:53.192 --> 00:56:57.062
There's a story you told about the
time he made you dig a big hole

00:56:57.712 --> 00:57:02.252
just because it was difficult.
There's wisdom in that.

00:57:02.312 --> 00:57:04.952
That you just need to work. You
need to stay busy all the time.

00:57:05.742 --> 00:57:09.672
- These were training camps from two thousand
and five to two thousand and seven.

00:57:09.952 --> 00:57:13.712
In the summer, we went to the mountains.
There was a children's camp there, an

00:57:13.812 --> 00:57:18.532
old Soviet-style pioneer camp where we
stayed doing our runs in the forest.

00:57:19.432 --> 00:57:23.412
Together, we built a small
training area there with a rope, a

00:57:23.472 --> 00:57:27.322
punching bag, a pull-up bar. We
did sprints there. We pulled

00:57:27.412 --> 00:57:31.352
resistance bands. After running in
the mountains, we would come back

00:57:31.412 --> 00:57:35.072
and work there. There were almost
no mats. We built the base

00:57:35.132 --> 00:57:38.852
ourselves. It had a dining
room on the first floor and an

00:57:38.912 --> 00:57:42.652
attic space where we slept. We
would come downstairs, eat,

00:57:42.892 --> 00:57:46.452
go outside, train in that little
sports area, then run in the

00:57:46.532 --> 00:57:50.132
forest. It was somewhere
around twelve hundred meters

00:57:50.332 --> 00:57:54.312
above sea level, a conifer
forest. We spent the whole

00:57:54.352 --> 00:57:58.092
summer there. There we'd train
in the morning, then there was

00:57:58.132 --> 00:58:02.352
nothing to do all day, and in the
evening we had training sessions again.

00:58:03.292 --> 00:58:06.912
But during the day, so we wouldn't
be busy with unnecessary things,

00:58:07.412 --> 00:58:11.112
my father sometimes gave us
work. And we'd ask, "Why are we

00:58:11.152 --> 00:58:14.992
digging this pit?" He'd say, "Well,
people used to use it like a

00:58:15.032 --> 00:58:18.932
refrigerator. When you dig
two or three meters down, it

00:58:18.992 --> 00:58:22.972
is cold inside. You can put milk there,
cheese, things like that, so the

00:58:23.092 --> 00:58:26.972
food does not go bad." And
we were like, "Okay, but do

00:58:27.052 --> 00:58:29.112
we really need these
refrigerators every day?"

00:58:30.212 --> 00:58:34.122
So in that sense, this was his
approach back then. He always had this

00:58:34.152 --> 00:58:35.372
kind of unique approach.

00:58:36.192 --> 00:58:39.972
- Well, what's the philosophy here?
That you gotta be busy all the time,

00:58:40.752 --> 00:58:42.792
working hard, doing something difficult.

00:58:42.832 --> 00:58:46.732
- Yes, it's very dangerous to
give a child free time in

00:58:46.772 --> 00:58:50.532
adolescence. They should
always be busy learning new

00:58:50.592 --> 00:58:54.412
information, improving,
acquiring new skills. A person

00:58:54.452 --> 00:58:58.192
should always be busy. When you're
sixteen, well, what can be on your

00:58:58.232 --> 00:59:02.012
mind? You should constantly
develop and improve.

00:59:03.032 --> 00:59:06.972
And by this time, you should already, I
believe, have decided what you want to

00:59:06.992 --> 00:59:13.892
become. You should already be investing
in what you want to turn into.

00:59:15.572 --> 00:59:19.512
- By the way, you had several very
good conversations with Nurlan, with

00:59:19.592 --> 00:59:23.172
Adam, which I highly recommend.
In one of them, your brother

00:59:23.192 --> 00:59:26.752
Abubakar told a story
that when you were young,

00:59:27.492 --> 00:59:31.312
there were several street fights,
maybe many, I don't know. He

00:59:31.432 --> 00:59:34.852
said he was the one who got you
into it. You were kind of bigger,

00:59:35.772 --> 00:59:39.312
and he needed protection at that
time. You were that protection.

00:59:39.912 --> 00:59:45.172
Can you tell us these stories? How often
did you fight on the streets of Dagestan?

00:59:46.012 --> 00:59:49.832
- Well, let's start with the fact
that I never bullied anyone. Never.

00:59:50.372 --> 00:59:54.492
Although I physically could bully,
yes, let's say I had the capability.

00:59:56.472 --> 00:59:59.532
I wasn't the kind of person
who started the fights.

01:00:00.792 --> 01:00:01.352
- You didn't start?

01:00:01.872 --> 01:00:02.572
- I didn't. Well—

01:00:03.052 --> 01:00:03.892
- Tried to finish.

01:00:04.032 --> 01:00:10.052
- Well, maybe there were some moments,
but mostly no. Mostly I didn't start.

01:00:10.792 --> 01:00:12.852
Abubakar, sure, he was like that.

01:00:13.832 --> 01:00:16.732
In my circle, I had some guys
who were a little bit like that.

01:00:17.712 --> 01:00:19.382
- He said that he's a bit of—

01:00:19.452 --> 01:00:21.452
- He was a troublemaker.

01:00:21.492 --> 01:00:26.092
He was a troublemaker, and because of
him, I got into trouble many times.

01:00:27.112 --> 01:00:29.172
When you're, let's say, fourteen,

01:00:29.992 --> 01:00:33.808
fifteen years old and your
brother is fighting. You don't

01:00:33.868 --> 01:00:37.348
really ask who's right or
who's wrong. You just go in,

01:00:37.788 --> 01:00:41.658
you start to, as we say, you
interfere. You just have

01:00:41.688 --> 01:00:45.168
to go in, right? But when
you're older, more conscious,

01:00:45.998 --> 01:00:48.588
when you're twenty, twenty-five
or thirty years old,

01:00:49.468 --> 01:00:52.398
this is when you really start telling him,

01:00:53.448 --> 01:00:57.408
"You know, Abubakar, I think
you're wrong here. Yes,

01:00:57.688 --> 01:01:01.538
we'll finish this right now, but
in the future, I wouldn't want

01:01:01.608 --> 01:01:04.848
you to get involved because
I think it's wrong, right?"

01:01:05.428 --> 01:01:09.308
Well, as kids, we just didn't
ask. We just went right in.

01:01:09.928 --> 01:01:13.668
When we moved to Makhachkala in 1999,

01:01:13.968 --> 01:01:19.648
let's say the end of 1999, so
there were—we were many brothers,

01:01:20.608 --> 01:01:24.588
more than ten of us. We lived in one
house. Some were in school, some

01:01:24.648 --> 01:01:28.548
in college, some in university.
But we all had one routine. We

01:01:28.588 --> 01:01:32.208
trained in the morning, then went
to school. We'd come home, rest,

01:01:32.448 --> 01:01:35.968
do homework, then train in the
evening, come home and rest,

01:01:36.428 --> 01:01:40.288
without mothers, just by
ourselves. Father gathered

01:01:40.348 --> 01:01:43.848
everyone. He took in close
friends and family, his

01:01:43.868 --> 01:01:47.548
nephews, cousins, relatives,
those who didn't have a father,

01:01:47.818 --> 01:01:51.568
and so on. He took them in, and
we all lived in one house. We

01:01:51.688 --> 01:01:55.568
cooked, cleaned, did laundry
all by ourselves. It was like,

01:01:55.708 --> 01:02:01.888
well, some kind of army life, you know?
And when the brothers had some problems,

01:02:03.268 --> 01:02:08.968
let's say misunderstandings, we
didn't go easy on those guys.

01:02:10.168 --> 01:02:13.428
And even those who lived
around there near that house,

01:02:14.888 --> 01:02:18.828
in that district, in the settlement,
everyone knew that there were lots

01:02:18.868 --> 01:02:25.708
of brothers living there. So they had to
be careful. And we had a football field

01:02:25.788 --> 01:02:31.308
there. It was right in front of the house,
and everyone who came to play there,

01:02:32.228 --> 01:02:38.348
we constantly had fights there,
misunderstandings. There were older brothers.

01:02:39.068 --> 01:02:43.028
They were always watching us, keeping us
from trouble. "Don't do this. Don't do"

01:02:43.108 --> 01:02:46.368
that. Watch your mouth." But
we had our own little group.

01:02:47.188 --> 01:02:51.068
The age difference between
us was maybe two, three

01:02:51.148 --> 01:02:56.048
years. There were five or six of us. We'd
always go outside and play football.

01:02:58.288 --> 01:03:02.168
If our team was losing,
then the game would just

01:03:02.228 --> 01:03:06.988
turn into a whole different sport,
and that's how we were growing up.

01:03:07.988 --> 01:03:14.868
We spent six, seven years there, almost ten
years. We were living together, growing,

01:03:15.228 --> 01:03:20.158
training. A lot has happened over
that time, but I was never...

01:03:21.788 --> 01:03:25.568
Well, maybe just a couple of
times, okay? Mostly, I was

01:03:25.588 --> 01:03:29.608
never the initiator of
fights with some exceptions.

01:03:30.448 --> 01:03:33.548
I just remember since childhood,
my father always told me,

01:03:34.868 --> 01:03:40.868
"You don't need to use force on the
street." My father looked at all of this

01:03:41.728 --> 01:03:43.228
in a completely different way.

01:03:44.388 --> 01:03:48.888
- What is your understanding of the difference
between the street fight and sports?

01:03:49.788 --> 01:03:54.788
- There is a very big difference.
I saw many fights where

01:03:56.688 --> 01:04:02.368
if you put those same guys into sparring,
in wrestling, MMA or combat Sambo,

01:04:03.958 --> 01:04:07.828
one guy could beat the other
very easily. But the street is

01:04:07.868 --> 01:04:12.788
completely different. There are
no rules, no referees, no points.

01:04:13.768 --> 01:04:17.948
You just fight there, and
it's totally different.

01:04:18.768 --> 01:04:24.128
I can say I actually had a
major turning point in my life.

01:04:26.908 --> 01:04:33.928
Let's say the one that, in terms of
psychology, really changed my whole attitude

01:04:33.948 --> 01:04:36.328
towards MMA.

01:04:37.248 --> 01:04:41.328
Back in twenty oh two, twenty oh
three, my father kept me in wrestling.

01:04:42.348 --> 01:04:46.308
He trained MMA combat Sambo,
but wanted me to stay

01:04:46.368 --> 01:04:50.528
in wrestling. My cousin, who was
older than me by a couple of months,

01:04:52.088 --> 01:04:55.468
he was training with my father.
His freestyle wrestling was weak,

01:04:56.048 --> 01:04:59.468
so my father sent him to me, to
the place where I was training.

01:05:00.648 --> 01:05:04.488
It was called the School of Higher
Sports Mastery. The local guys

01:05:04.568 --> 01:05:10.948
from the city know this place. He came
to practice and got into it with a guy

01:05:11.028 --> 01:05:14.768
in our gym who was heavier, maybe one

01:05:14.848 --> 01:05:18.088
class heavier. This guy was
the best in his weight,

01:05:18.568 --> 01:05:24.328
winning Dagestan and the city
too. So they got into a conflict.

01:05:25.748 --> 01:05:32.768
They were arguing. My brother
had just joined. He was new to

01:05:32.868 --> 01:05:36.648
our freestyle wrestling gym,
right? But I knew the other guy

01:05:36.708 --> 01:05:41.508
well. They paired up and they got into
it. Well, that's my brother, right?

01:05:42.908 --> 01:05:46.478
So the guy goes, "Let's
meet up after." And they

01:05:46.548 --> 01:05:50.388
agreed, as we say, to step
outside after the training.

01:05:50.688 --> 01:05:58.418
I remember it was raining heavily, a real
downpour. The guy brought his brother. I

01:05:58.468 --> 01:06:04.488
came with mine. We went outside and
they started fighting one-on-one.

01:06:05.548 --> 01:06:08.808
I remember he shot in,
lifted and took him down.

01:06:09.088 --> 01:06:12.687
Abdurakhman, my brother, threw up
a triangle and he choked him out.

01:06:15.408 --> 01:06:21.088
When we went out, I was thinking
Abdurakhman will lose to this guy. I even

01:06:21.168 --> 01:06:26.578
visualized it happening, the whole thing.
Like, this guy beats him, and then

01:06:27.548 --> 01:06:30.988
I start fighting. Well, I'm not
going there for my brother to lose.

01:06:31.748 --> 01:06:33.748
In my head, I was getting into a fight.

01:06:34.920 --> 01:06:38.880
But he threw him locked up in a
triangle. For the first time I saw

01:06:38.920 --> 01:06:42.500
with my own eyes how someone
chokes another person with a

01:06:42.560 --> 01:06:45.000
triangle and they lose consciousness.

01:06:45.860 --> 01:06:46.210
- Wow.

01:06:46.730 --> 01:06:53.480
- They fought, he put him to sleep. We had
to pull them apart, me and other guys.

01:06:55.220 --> 01:06:59.600
Fights over, Abdurrahman won. And on the
way home, I'm like, "Abdurrahman, wow.

01:07:01.280 --> 01:07:05.760
You impress me." I came home
and immediately told my father.

01:07:07.189 --> 01:07:11.730
I said... That was in 2002
or '03, if I'm not mistaken.

01:07:12.980 --> 01:07:16.600
I am 14 years old. I said, "I'm
switching to combat Sambo."

01:07:17.120 --> 01:07:20.880
I said, "What Abdurrahman did,
the one he choked out, he's

01:07:20.960 --> 01:07:24.730
not just anyone in the gym.
He's one of the best wrestlers,

01:07:24.820 --> 01:07:26.130
but he couldn't do anything."

01:07:27.700 --> 01:07:31.260
My father said, "You'll go to
wrestling, but you'll come to me,

01:07:31.580 --> 01:07:34.540
I'll teach you. You'll learn
techniques here and so on.

01:07:36.520 --> 01:07:40.320
But I want you to gain wrestling
skills." I started going three

01:07:40.420 --> 01:07:42.850
days to train with my father,
three days to wrestling.

01:07:44.000 --> 01:07:48.160
And at that moment, I realized that
no matter how strong the wrestler is,

01:07:49.420 --> 01:07:53.280
lying on the back is where
the wrestling normally ends,

01:07:53.720 --> 01:07:57.310
but MMA only begins there. And I was like,

01:07:58.420 --> 01:08:02.560
I thought, "I need this. I need this
mixed style." I realized this was for me.

01:08:03.460 --> 01:08:07.280
I started going to my father's
trainings, despite that he didn't

01:08:07.300 --> 01:08:11.060
let me. He didn't want me to do
fighting. I think most people

01:08:11.140 --> 01:08:14.920
don't know this. My father wanted
me to do freestyle wrestling or

01:08:15.060 --> 01:08:18.740
judo, but in my head, I already
had this mixed style. I

01:08:18.760 --> 01:08:22.420
always wanted to compete. I
was already getting tapes to

01:08:22.480 --> 01:08:26.100
watch. I always wanted to
compete where there are no

01:08:26.180 --> 01:08:28.960
rules, where I could fully use my skills.

01:08:29.540 --> 01:08:30.370
- Maximum freedom.

01:08:30.920 --> 01:08:33.980
- Yes, judo, Sambo, freestyle, grappling,

01:08:34.080 --> 01:08:37.520
jujitsu. More people knew
jujitsu than grappling.

01:08:39.240 --> 01:08:42.820
After that, I switched, and
from there my journey slowly

01:08:42.880 --> 01:08:48.180
started, which I had already begun. That's
when I started the amateur journey.

01:08:49.520 --> 01:08:53.380
Then when I started getting
good results as an amateur, I

01:08:53.440 --> 01:08:57.420
realized that I can switch
to pro and achieve success.

01:08:57.740 --> 01:09:01.600
Sambo. In combat Sambo.
Combat Sambo. I competed in

01:09:01.700 --> 01:09:05.440
all kinds of sports. Everything
that involves wrestling and

01:09:05.540 --> 01:09:09.180
fighting. I competed everywhere.
I competed in freestyle, in

01:09:09.280 --> 01:09:13.080
judo, in grappling, in combat
Sambo. I competed in sport

01:09:13.140 --> 01:09:16.940
Sambo, in hand-to-hand combat,
in pankration. My father

01:09:17.060 --> 01:09:20.820
put me in all competitions.
He would say, "You have a

01:09:20.860 --> 01:09:24.360
competition here and another
one there. Now you are

01:09:24.380 --> 01:09:28.280
preparing for this one. You will
compete in this weight, under these

01:09:28.340 --> 01:09:32.160
rules, in this sport." And he was
sending me everywhere. He would tell

01:09:32.200 --> 01:09:36.140
me, "You will need this in the future.
The more competition experience you have,

01:09:36.200 --> 01:09:39.660
the more relaxed and confident you'll
feel. It builds up over time."

01:09:40.260 --> 01:09:44.080
When I was young, I was losing as
well. I go to a judo competition,

01:09:44.300 --> 01:09:48.280
win two matches and lose two matches.
I would ask, "Father, why are

01:09:48.320 --> 01:09:52.260
you sending me there? This is not my
sport." And he'd say, "No, go and compete."

01:09:53.380 --> 01:09:57.220
So, okay, I went, I cut
weight. The next day you

01:09:57.280 --> 01:10:01.200
come in, you see 50 competitors
in your weight class. You

01:10:01.260 --> 01:10:05.180
let this weight, this
competitive energy pass

01:10:05.200 --> 01:10:08.940
through you, and psychologically
you get better, better,

01:10:09.060 --> 01:10:12.100
better each time. You become
more relaxed about it.

01:10:13.300 --> 01:10:17.260
And this moment really affected
me, although then I didn't

01:10:17.320 --> 01:10:21.140
agree with my father. Back
then I thought, "Why should I

01:10:21.200 --> 01:10:27.020
compete in other sports?" He said, "You
can wrestle, right? Well, go wrestle."

01:10:28.140 --> 01:10:32.020
I was saying, "But judo isn't my
sport. It's hard for me there."

01:10:32.640 --> 01:10:36.460
He says, "Well, you went on
four times. Look, you won

01:10:36.520 --> 01:10:40.120
twice, then lost twice. You
competed four times. You're

01:10:40.140 --> 01:10:42.340
15 years old. You're just developing.

01:10:43.500 --> 01:10:46.620
Do you know how valuable
this experience is for you?

01:10:47.480 --> 01:10:51.300
Four matches in one day."
Now when I'm coaching,

01:10:51.980 --> 01:10:58.760
I understand the psychological approach,
how important it is. I realize now how

01:10:58.840 --> 01:11:02.320
correctly he prepared
me for the big stage.

01:11:03.240 --> 01:11:06.960
The preparation was already
happening then when I was

01:11:06.980 --> 01:11:10.940
15 years old. For example,
when I went into the biggest

01:11:11.040 --> 01:11:14.980
fight in combat sports
history, when I fought in

01:11:15.300 --> 01:11:22.060
2018 here in Las Vegas. At that point,
I was already at my peak, prime time.

01:11:23.600 --> 01:11:29.720
I was 30 years old. I went through all
possible competitions as an amateur

01:11:29.960 --> 01:11:34.120
and a professional. And you
know what? I was ready.

01:11:35.760 --> 01:11:39.600
This preparation, it started
back then when I was 15.

01:11:40.140 --> 01:11:44.000
It's not like they give you a fight
and you prepare for two months.

01:11:44.360 --> 01:11:48.600
Preparation starts long before
you even step into the octagon.

01:11:50.140 --> 01:11:55.700
- So this experience of competitions, which
started at 15, it continued, it expanded.

01:11:55.940 --> 01:11:56.640
- Yeah, of course.

01:11:56.800 --> 01:12:00.180
- So can you explain what
was going on in your head?

01:12:01.460 --> 01:12:05.080
- I didn't want to lose. I just didn't. It

01:12:05.200 --> 01:12:08.880
never occurred to me that I could
get hurt, that someone could

01:12:09.000 --> 01:12:12.800
hurt me, that I might break
a finger somewhere. I never

01:12:12.860 --> 01:12:17.460
had that in my head, that I could get
hit, that I'd get a scratch, and so on.

01:12:18.300 --> 01:12:20.700
I don't know if you can
call it perfectionism.

01:12:21.800 --> 01:12:25.789
I didn't want to lose. And
to not lose, I knew I needed

01:12:25.840 --> 01:12:29.760
to compete a lot and improve. Training is

01:12:29.840 --> 01:12:33.740
one thing, but competing is
different. I understood that the more

01:12:33.860 --> 01:12:37.660
I competed, the more experience I
would gain. I had to go through it.

01:12:38.356 --> 01:12:42.216
It was constantly on my mind.
This understanding that I

01:12:42.276 --> 01:12:46.136
must compete a lot came to
me at an early age, since

01:12:46.276 --> 01:12:49.916
I was fifteen, sixteen,
seventeen, eighteen, and I

01:12:50.036 --> 01:12:55.256
never stopped thinking about it. So
it was always in the back of my mind.

01:12:56.276 --> 01:12:57.856
- Were you confident that you would win?

01:12:58.696 --> 01:12:59.516
- In my youth? No.

01:13:00.156 --> 01:13:01.076
- Were you afraid to lose?

01:13:01.476 --> 01:13:04.876
- Yeah, in my youth, I wasn't
always so sure. I just wasn't.

01:13:05.476 --> 01:13:08.976
Because I never stood out.
Yes, I had a coach for a

01:13:09.076 --> 01:13:13.316
father, and how many, how
many trainers have kids who

01:13:14.156 --> 01:13:18.116
compete but aren't successful?
And then when I went to

01:13:18.136 --> 01:13:22.126
the big leagues, you know, I
had already fought. I had done

01:13:22.196 --> 01:13:25.466
sixteen professional fights
across the post-Soviet space.

01:13:26.096 --> 01:13:29.236
Later, when I was already
signed to the UFC,

01:13:30.036 --> 01:13:33.706
there I already had this
feeling. You know, with

01:13:33.856 --> 01:13:37.596
each fight, I was getting
mentally stronger.

01:13:37.636 --> 01:13:41.996
My mind was becoming more and
more solid, like concrete.

01:13:43.195 --> 01:13:49.856
When I went out there, I knew, yes, these
are fights. Yes, you can take a hit.

01:13:50.616 --> 01:13:54.946
Yes, anything can happen when
knees, elbows, fists are flying,

01:13:55.796 --> 01:13:57.976
when you can get caught
in a choke, you know?

01:13:59.146 --> 01:14:04.096
But I thought, "If they don't knock
me out, how will they beat me?

01:14:05.056 --> 01:14:10.076
I have fifteen minutes. Fifteen minutes
is enough to make a person tired."

01:14:11.076 --> 01:14:16.396
I'm like, "Okay, if you wanna stop me,
come here. I'll give you that chance.

01:14:17.476 --> 01:14:22.336
Let's get in the octagon." I had
this impatience during fight week.

01:14:23.256 --> 01:14:27.216
I wanted to go in there and
feel my opponent. I just

01:14:27.276 --> 01:14:31.056
wanted to be inside the
cage for them to close

01:14:31.116 --> 01:14:35.416
us in, for the referee to
say, "Ready? Ready. Fight."

01:14:36.376 --> 01:14:40.176
And here I wanted to feel,
to grapple with him and

01:14:40.356 --> 01:14:43.946
feel his strength, his skills, his energy,

01:14:44.376 --> 01:14:48.265
and for it not to stop.
And with any opponent I

01:14:48.336 --> 01:14:52.856
felt from the beginning, and I didn't
actually have superhuman strength.

01:14:53.976 --> 01:14:57.936
But from the beginning, when I
grabbed them in the first minute and

01:14:57.996 --> 01:15:02.336
then in the sixth or seventh
minute, I could feel them fading.

01:15:04.156 --> 01:15:08.626
I was, let's say, like squeezing
this energy out of them,

01:15:09.836 --> 01:15:13.696
and I was getting better and
better minute by minute.

01:15:15.196 --> 01:15:18.456
- Can you break down the technical
details of your top pressure?

01:15:19.196 --> 01:15:24.356
That pressure you put on in
wrestling, on the ground, all of it.

01:15:26.056 --> 01:15:28.736
How does it work? Can you explain
the philosophy behind it?

01:15:29.736 --> 01:15:33.716
- Over the years, I've learned to
use my strength properly. There's

01:15:33.776 --> 01:15:37.576
this sport called aikido.
I don't like it much, but

01:15:37.716 --> 01:15:41.936
I like their philosophy, where your
strength might work against you.

01:15:43.196 --> 01:15:47.016
I think for a lot of listeners, this is
gonna be a brand-new sport. But there

01:15:47.076 --> 01:15:50.736
is this sport called aikido,
and the whole philosophy

01:15:50.776 --> 01:15:55.976
behind it is using your opponent's force
against them. That's what I learned to do.

01:15:57.216 --> 01:16:00.876
And I have learned to force
an opponent into positions

01:16:01.996 --> 01:16:05.516
where they have to escape wasting their
energy. Like when we were sparring

01:16:05.556 --> 01:16:11.216
yesterday. That's exactly what I wanted.
I wanted you to feel that energy,

01:16:11.476 --> 01:16:14.656
that heavy top pressure. We
could have just played around.

01:16:15.756 --> 01:16:20.356
I could have gone easy on you, but I wanted
you to feel the real heavy pressure.

01:16:21.166 --> 01:16:24.896
And of course, please don't think it was a
sign of disrespect. It's quite the opposite.

01:16:25.276 --> 01:16:26.156
- No, it was respect.

01:16:26.576 --> 01:16:28.376
- I just wanted you to feel that,

01:16:30.516 --> 01:16:34.476
you know, how exhausting it can be.
I think you felt it yourself that it

01:16:34.536 --> 01:16:38.476
was a bit different from the
kind of grappling you've dealt

01:16:38.516 --> 01:16:39.776
with before. I'm sure of it.

01:16:40.416 --> 01:16:44.156
- The only one who's even close to that is,
I mean, you're the absolute number one.

01:16:44.516 --> 01:16:48.396
But the only thing is, when I trained with
Roger Gracie in a different sport, you get that

01:16:48.436 --> 01:16:50.896
same feeling with him. Like,
the guy is just insanely heavy.

01:16:50.916 --> 01:16:54.516
- Mm-hmm. Pressure. Pressure.
Well, Roger, he's the

01:16:54.576 --> 01:16:58.476
greatest, too. He's the
greatest in his own sport. What

01:16:58.576 --> 01:17:03.336
makes a person unique is his
style, his style and his approach

01:17:04.716 --> 01:17:10.156
to his work. I always approach my
craft very professionally, you know,

01:17:10.276 --> 01:17:14.556
respectfully. I believe that if
you're gonna be in this game,

01:17:15.476 --> 01:17:19.616
in this sport, you have to
completely dedicate yourself to it.

01:17:20.256 --> 01:17:23.676
- But I think you had a deep understanding
of how to break an opponent, both

01:17:23.736 --> 01:17:27.116
physically and psychologically,
just looking at your style.

01:17:28.416 --> 01:17:32.956
- I've always been right there, always
fighting for position. I always--

01:17:34.516 --> 01:17:38.356
Look, I mean that I just always
had this thought in my head.

01:17:41.796 --> 01:17:45.576
My grandfather always told me, "A
dog that latches onto that one weak

01:17:45.716 --> 01:17:46.996
spot always wins."

01:17:48.716 --> 01:17:52.616
It's the same with a fighter. When
you're fighting on pure instinct, you

01:17:52.676 --> 01:17:56.636
feel your opponent's weakness. You know
where they're weak, and you have to

01:17:56.676 --> 01:18:03.556
keep pressing right there. For
example, let's say I'm fighting Conor,

01:18:03.836 --> 01:18:10.516
right? His strengths are his striking,
his timing and his speed, his boxing.

01:18:11.596 --> 01:18:15.436
Why would I stand and strike with him?
What am I supposed to do? I have to

01:18:15.476 --> 01:18:18.136
put pressure on his weak spot. So,

01:18:18.876 --> 01:18:22.796
That's what separates the
fighters who reach success, the

01:18:22.836 --> 01:18:26.476
highest level of success, and
become the best of the best.

01:18:27.476 --> 01:18:31.416
They know how to find an
opponent's weakness, and

01:18:31.476 --> 01:18:35.156
they pressure it relentlessly. So, mm,

01:18:36.216 --> 01:18:39.166
so it all comes down to having
the right strategy here.

01:18:40.420 --> 01:18:42.460
- Your fight with Conor, I
think, was the biggest

01:18:43.260 --> 01:18:45.720
fight in UFC history. That's for sure.

01:18:46.580 --> 01:18:50.390
Well, actually, in all of sports. Do
you think there will ever be another

01:18:50.560 --> 01:18:53.280
fight of that magnitude in
the UFC? Is it even possible?

01:18:55.520 --> 01:18:58.740
- Well, let's look at the history here.

01:19:00.420 --> 01:19:04.400
I mean, our sport is still
very young, say thirty-five

01:19:04.440 --> 01:19:08.980
years old, starting from
the '90s. Put it this way.

01:19:09.880 --> 01:19:12.820
Take, for example, the
sport most similar to MMA,

01:19:14.260 --> 01:19:18.900
which is, well, let's say boxing. How old
is boxing? A hundred and fifty years?

01:19:19.720 --> 01:19:23.840
Like, when you think boxing, what pops
into your head? Thrilla in Manila?

01:19:24.720 --> 01:19:27.580
- Yeah, yeah. Well, I love
Mike Tyson a lot too, so.

01:19:27.880 --> 01:19:31.220
- Well, Tyson had big fights,
but Mike Tyson never had that,

01:19:31.680 --> 01:19:35.620
Signature rival like Muhammad
Ali had in Joe Frazier.

01:19:35.680 --> 01:19:36.900
Yeah, he just didn't have one.

01:19:38.060 --> 01:19:42.600
I mean, who do we have? Evander Holyfield?
Look, Holyfield-Tyson is no Muhammad Ali.

01:19:44.340 --> 01:19:47.020
Take Ali and Frazier,
Thrilla in Manila, right?

01:19:48.900 --> 01:19:50.600
People called it the greatest rivalry,

01:19:51.800 --> 01:19:55.740
and it still is. In the 20th
century, there was nothing

01:19:55.760 --> 01:19:59.500
that could compare. In the 21st
century, the only thing you could

01:19:59.540 --> 01:20:03.780
compare to it, purely based on the
numbers, is Pacquiao and Mayweather.

01:20:04.900 --> 01:20:08.760
Yes, there was public interest, but there
wasn't the energy that there was with Muhammad

01:20:08.820 --> 01:20:09.860
Ali and Joe Frazier.

01:20:11.420 --> 01:20:14.400
- Yeah, if I can just pause for a
second, I'll check on Perplexity.

01:20:15.260 --> 01:20:18.980
List the biggest boxing fights of all
time. It's Ali and Frazier, like you said.

01:20:19.500 --> 01:20:19.880
- Yeah, yeah.

01:20:20.180 --> 01:20:21.920
- The first, the third.

01:20:22.010 --> 01:20:25.340
- Well, when he lost, and then the
third fight, the Thrilla in Manila.

01:20:25.750 --> 01:20:27.600
- And Foreman, I must say
Ali versus Foreman.

01:20:28.100 --> 01:20:30.960
- Well, Foreman, that was the
year, I think, '74, right?

01:20:31.490 --> 01:20:34.100
- The fight was watched by up to
a billion people worldwide.

01:20:34.660 --> 01:20:38.560
- Yeah, yeah, yeah. It was one of those
things. Politics got mixed into it too.

01:20:39.260 --> 01:20:41.340
Well, it was actually a very big fight.

01:20:41.860 --> 01:20:45.700
- Sugar Ray Leonard, Duran, Sugar
Ray Leonard versus Thomas Hearns,

01:20:46.040 --> 01:20:47.880
Hagler versus Hearns.

01:20:47.900 --> 01:20:51.100
- Well, Hagler. Hagler
and Duran in Montreal.

01:20:51.259 --> 01:20:55.120
Sugar Ray Leonard and Duran in
Montreal when they fought, when

01:20:55.180 --> 01:20:59.100
he admitted after the fight that he lost
the mental game and broke down before

01:20:59.140 --> 01:21:01.480
the fight. But then Sugar
Ray beat him later.

01:21:02.400 --> 01:21:03.360
- Yeah, he came back and won.

01:21:03.820 --> 01:21:08.720
- Yeah. Well, if you look at it out of
everything that stands out, I think,

01:21:08.920 --> 01:21:12.860
You can mention Frazier and
Muhammad Ali. And there was also

01:21:12.940 --> 01:21:16.660
Joe Louis against German
Max Schmeling. Hitler stood

01:21:16.700 --> 01:21:20.660
behind Schmeling, and Roosevelt stood
behind Louis. For those who don't know

01:21:20.680 --> 01:21:22.840
this story, I just watched it recently.

01:21:24.100 --> 01:21:28.540
It was something incomparable. They
were so big. They were two of the most,

01:21:29.560 --> 01:21:35.740
the most famous athletes of their time.
Roosevelt was promoting Joe Louis, you know?

01:21:37.060 --> 01:21:40.880
At that moment for America, it was
like something incredible that a

01:21:40.940 --> 01:21:44.800
white president was promoting
a Black world champion.

01:21:45.060 --> 01:21:48.480
And on the other hand, Hitler
stood behind Schmeling.

01:21:48.540 --> 01:21:52.090
- It was what? Nineteen thirty-six,
nineteen thirty-eight. They had-

01:21:52.640 --> 01:21:53.360
- They fought twice?

01:21:53.400 --> 01:21:53.860
- Twice.

01:21:54.500 --> 01:21:55.970
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.
They fought two times.

01:21:56.480 --> 01:22:00.360
- This wasn't just a good boxing
match, just a good fight.

01:22:00.400 --> 01:22:03.160
This was politics. Those were nations.

01:22:03.240 --> 01:22:05.740
- This was politics. This was nations.

01:22:05.800 --> 01:22:06.310
- Everything.

01:22:06.400 --> 01:22:07.240
- It all came together.

01:22:07.260 --> 01:22:11.040
- And it's the same thing with Ali
too. Muhammad Ali with the Vietnam

01:22:11.100 --> 01:22:12.200
War, all that stuff.

01:22:12.740 --> 01:22:13.240
- Yes, yes.

01:22:13.410 --> 01:22:16.960
- And his, and even religion is tied into
it. Everything here came together.

01:22:17.440 --> 01:22:19.820
- They simply went beyond sports.

01:22:20.740 --> 01:22:24.560
It was the exact same thing
when I fought Conor. That fight

01:22:24.640 --> 01:22:29.080
went beyond our sport. It
impacted every sport because

01:22:30.440 --> 01:22:32.600
athletes of all kinds of different sports,

01:22:33.620 --> 01:22:36.970
everyone who loves to compete,
it didn't matter if it was

01:22:37.100 --> 01:22:41.080
football, basketball, soccer,
or boxing, everyone was

01:22:41.140 --> 01:22:46.240
following this. And honestly,
will there be a fight like that?

01:22:47.300 --> 01:22:51.060
Well, it's hard for me to say, but
I think it'll happen. It'll happen

01:22:51.180 --> 01:22:55.040
for sure. There will always be champions.
There will always be rivalries.

01:22:55.360 --> 01:22:58.500
But when it'll happen, that's
just a matter of time.

01:22:59.260 --> 01:23:02.380
- So it's not entirely about the athletes.
It's about everything combined.

01:23:02.440 --> 01:23:06.340
- Yeah. It's everything combined. The way
he talked, what he stirred up, the lines

01:23:06.360 --> 01:23:09.840
he crossed, touching on
family, religion. And

01:23:09.920 --> 01:23:13.520
then somehow on one side
you had Conor, this really

01:23:13.600 --> 01:23:17.440
interesting champion who knew how to hype
himself up, and on the other side, you

01:23:17.480 --> 01:23:21.960
had this undefeated somebody from
nowhere, from the mountains.

01:23:22.820 --> 01:23:28.200
You know how it is. It all somehow, all
of it just kind of happened naturally.

01:23:28.480 --> 01:23:32.330
And it wasn't staged. It
was real. We had a real

01:23:32.380 --> 01:23:36.090
rivalry, unlike the way a lot of people
now just hype up interest in a fight,

01:23:36.420 --> 01:23:39.340
but people feel that energy.
It doesn't go anywhere.

01:23:40.440 --> 01:23:44.020
People can tell what's real and what's
fake. And because of that, we went,

01:23:44.760 --> 01:23:49.040
we went beyond our own sport. That's
what made this fight so massive.

01:23:49.920 --> 01:23:52.260
- Well, when you look into your
heart back then and now,

01:23:53.080 --> 01:23:55.740
did you have anger? Did
you have hatred for him?

01:23:57.420 --> 01:24:01.300
- Back then and even now, I'd
still keep a cool head.

01:24:02.820 --> 01:24:06.680
Nothing has changed. When you
let your emotions take over,

01:24:06.900 --> 01:24:10.380
when they're off the charts
and you completely lose

01:24:10.460 --> 01:24:14.560
control, that's a very bad place to be. So

01:24:15.620 --> 01:24:23.420
you'll tire out faster. You'll fall. You'll
fall for any kind of bait and so on. I

01:24:23.480 --> 01:24:28.180
just knew that I needed
to stay calm, composed,

01:24:29.640 --> 01:24:35.960
to stick to the plan no matter what happened,
and not go beyond my emotional limits.

01:24:37.640 --> 01:24:43.780
This is a very, very dangerous moment
when you give in to your emotions

01:24:45.200 --> 01:24:49.140
You have to control them. Every
single detail, every little thing

01:24:49.240 --> 01:24:52.900
has to be perfectly in place.
The second you let it all

01:24:53.060 --> 01:24:55.740
slip, everything becomes a total mess.

01:24:57.020 --> 01:25:01.079
So once the fight was finally done,
I just let all my emotions out.

01:25:02.140 --> 01:25:06.100
I had to step right in and prove
it, and punish him, and show them

01:25:06.240 --> 01:25:10.180
I'm better, that I'm better than him,
and put a final end to this whole

01:25:10.240 --> 01:25:12.860
bitter rivalry. When all of that was over-

01:25:13.420 --> 01:25:14.380
- Your emotions came out?

01:25:14.580 --> 01:25:18.240
- ... that's when my emotions just went
over the edge. But until the fight was

01:25:18.260 --> 01:25:21.940
completely finished, starting
from training, you know,

01:25:22.600 --> 01:25:26.390
at the press conferences, at the weigh-ins,
everywhere, in front of the media,

01:25:26.500 --> 01:25:30.080
everywhere, I kept myself completely
under control, and I was very good at it.

01:25:31.120 --> 01:25:35.740
Honestly, looking back it's
been almost eight years now.

01:25:37.520 --> 01:25:41.440
Looking back, well, I'm a little
surprised. At that point, I was a very

01:25:41.480 --> 01:25:45.420
emotional guy, too. Over these
eight years a lot has happened

01:25:45.460 --> 01:25:49.360
in my life, but I'm surprised
that I kept it together. And

01:25:49.440 --> 01:25:53.320
well, you know, it wasn't
easy for me to keep myself

01:25:53.380 --> 01:25:57.180
within my limits, to keep
myself under control like that.

01:25:57.840 --> 01:26:01.740
But here, again, I'll come
back to what I said, I was

01:26:01.840 --> 01:26:08.640
very lucky. My father called me at that time
I was here two months before the fight.

01:26:10.640 --> 01:26:13.910
His advice helped me out so
much because for me it was

01:26:15.020 --> 01:26:18.930
not just that it mattered, it
was actually the most important

01:26:18.930 --> 01:26:22.490
thing for me back then just to know my

01:26:22.520 --> 01:26:26.080
father's reaction, how
he was taking everything

01:26:26.600 --> 01:26:30.120
that was happening, because
there was just so much media

01:26:30.200 --> 01:26:33.680
coverage, so I knew my
father was watching.

01:26:34.440 --> 01:26:38.260
It was important for me to
know his take. I'd call and

01:26:38.360 --> 01:26:42.240
ask, and my father would even tell
me things I hadn't even asked him

01:26:42.320 --> 01:26:46.280
about. He'd bring it up on his
own. Because you know the way it

01:26:46.340 --> 01:26:50.260
is with us, between me and my
father, in our traditions, in our

01:26:50.380 --> 01:26:54.160
part of the world, in our family for
generations, in our village, there's

01:26:54.199 --> 01:26:58.190
just always this distance with your
father. Like, when you walk in, there

01:26:58.220 --> 01:27:02.060
are just so many things you can't let
yourself do in front of your father.

01:27:02.380 --> 01:27:06.300
You have to be like, sort of
like a soldier in front of a

01:27:06.340 --> 01:27:10.220
general, right? If you can even
make that comparison. If a soldier

01:27:10.300 --> 01:27:16.320
stands at attention like that, then with
us it's even, even stricter. Like even,

01:27:18.100 --> 01:27:20.760
just to give an example
here. For instance,

01:27:22.400 --> 01:27:26.380
imagine you're a father sitting
there, right? And your two-year-old

01:27:26.420 --> 01:27:28.620
kid walks up to you and
wants to play with you.

01:27:29.740 --> 01:27:33.000
You can't even, like, do that baby
talk thing with your own kid.

01:27:33.560 --> 01:27:34.720
- You don't have that kind of friendship.

01:27:35.080 --> 01:27:36.329
- You know it's a friendship, but-

01:27:36.500 --> 01:27:37.280
- It's different, yes.

01:27:37.520 --> 01:27:41.480
- There's a certain distance that you must
keep around your father. You always

01:27:41.520 --> 01:27:44.500
have to be, like, completely buttoned up.

01:27:45.220 --> 01:27:49.150
You have to stand like a soldier in front
of your father. You don't let your emotions

01:27:49.180 --> 01:27:53.000
take over. At that point, just to know

01:27:53.060 --> 01:27:56.840
what my father thought, who was,
like, all the way across the ocean,

01:27:57.280 --> 01:28:01.120
who couldn't just fly out and
be there to support his son

01:28:02.780 --> 01:28:06.580
and just be there by my side. But
for me, since he didn't have a

01:28:06.620 --> 01:28:10.580
visa, I just had to know exactly
what he thought. I didn't care what

01:28:10.640 --> 01:28:14.000
anyone else thought. I didn't
care about the opinions of, like,

01:28:14.220 --> 01:28:18.190
millions, tens of millions,
hundreds of millions watching this

01:28:18.260 --> 01:28:22.140
fight. I only ever cared about
one person and his take,

01:28:22.620 --> 01:28:26.960
what he thought and what he'd say to
me. But I couldn't call him and say,

01:28:28.120 --> 01:28:29.900
"Father, what do you think about this?"

01:28:30.720 --> 01:28:34.620
Well, we never had that. Well, you
had to wait. I would just call

01:28:34.760 --> 01:28:38.520
and wait to hear what he'd
say. And my father, he knew

01:28:38.560 --> 01:28:42.420
exactly where to draw the line
because he was in the exact

01:28:42.500 --> 01:28:46.260
same position with his own father
since his own father was still

01:28:46.320 --> 01:28:49.609
around. And then he had a son, too, right?

01:28:50.340 --> 01:28:53.720
And he understood me, how I
felt about him, since his

01:28:53.740 --> 01:28:56.079
relationship with his own
father was the same way.

01:28:57.780 --> 01:29:01.740
And in that sense, back then, my father
really kept me grounded mentally.

01:29:02.800 --> 01:29:07.580
He even told me something once.

01:29:08.500 --> 01:29:14.260
Do you know how diamonds are, like,
actually made? Under great pressure.

01:29:14.680 --> 01:29:18.099
He says, "You're under great
pressure, and you have a choice.

01:29:19.720 --> 01:29:23.680
You can break down and give up,
or you can get through it all

01:29:24.940 --> 01:29:26.920
properly, clearly, with dignity.

01:29:28.060 --> 01:29:31.700
Go in, prepare right, make
weight, recover, go in, win, and

01:29:31.740 --> 01:29:35.580
settle it once and for all."
And those exact kinds of

01:29:35.600 --> 01:29:39.420
moments, they really,
they deeply impacted me.

01:29:40.600 --> 01:29:44.940
- So because of those words, you
could cut everything off? Focus.

01:29:45.720 --> 01:29:45.900
- Well-

01:29:46.460 --> 01:29:46.840
- Mentally.

01:29:47.360 --> 01:29:51.160
- Mentally. Like, if a million
people showed up right there

01:29:51.280 --> 01:29:54.940
and told me right outside where I
was staying, at the hotel, right?

01:29:55.240 --> 01:29:58.940
Like, if a million people gathered
right under my window and

01:29:59.000 --> 01:30:00.640
said, "Hey, we've got your back."

01:30:01.500 --> 01:30:05.400
And honestly, just one call from
my father, it wouldn't even

01:30:05.440 --> 01:30:09.200
compare, because that one call
from my father, that one piece of

01:30:09.280 --> 01:30:12.960
advice, his support, his
voice, it charged me up

01:30:13.120 --> 01:30:16.000
way more than absolutely
everything else combined.

01:30:17.980 --> 01:30:21.840
- But I must say, José Aldo,
who is one of the greatest

01:30:21.880 --> 01:30:25.630
fighters of all time, and still Conor was

01:30:25.700 --> 01:30:29.490
arguably able to
psychologically break him.

01:30:29.520 --> 01:30:33.240
But with you, it seems like
Conor was not able to mentally

01:30:33.250 --> 01:30:37.280
affect you. Did he manage to get
into your head even a little?

01:30:37.420 --> 01:30:43.660
- No, not at all. I had a
clear understanding that

01:30:44.700 --> 01:30:51.000
physically and mentally I was just way
stronger than him. I didn't doubt myself for

01:30:51.900 --> 01:30:54.692
even 1%. Well, people,

01:30:56.132 --> 01:30:59.912
they can say whatever they want.
He's got a massive fan base too.

01:31:00.612 --> 01:31:02.412
Maybe they won't agree with that.

01:31:03.752 --> 01:31:07.712
But the Almighty gave me this unshakable
confidence that is just really hard for

01:31:07.752 --> 01:31:15.152
me to even put into words. And I just
deeply believe that I was better than

01:31:15.192 --> 01:31:20.032
him and that I was stronger than
him, and that in this matchup, well,

01:31:21.152 --> 01:31:25.212
I was supposed to win. I just had
this rock solid faith in myself.

01:31:26.652 --> 01:31:32.392
- Up until that moment, is it fair to
say that this was your toughest fight?

01:31:32.532 --> 01:31:33.412
- Up until that moment?

01:31:33.752 --> 01:31:34.192
- Correct.

01:31:34.472 --> 01:31:38.902
- One hundred percent. Because
fighting's all psychology, you know?

01:31:40.032 --> 01:31:44.212
It's a mental game. Mentally
speaking, that was my toughest fight.

01:31:47.032 --> 01:31:49.862
At that point, yeah. But a
tougher fight came later.

01:31:50.512 --> 01:31:51.042
- With Gaethje?

01:31:51.312 --> 01:31:51.882
- With Gaethje.

01:31:52.652 --> 01:31:54.612
- There were a lot of factors
that made it so tough.

01:31:55.072 --> 01:31:55.292
- Yes.

01:31:55.632 --> 01:31:59.112
- So can you explain? You said
somewhere that actually,

01:32:00.892 --> 01:32:04.692
unfortunately, or at least from my
perspective, unfortunately, we never

01:32:04.732 --> 01:32:08.262
got to see your absolute
limit. Were you, uh-

01:32:08.292 --> 01:32:12.972
- Not in fights, no. You've
never seen my limit in fights.

01:32:12.992 --> 01:32:16.852
- But you said that Gaethje
was the closest to it

01:32:17.312 --> 01:32:21.202
'cause of the whole process. Can you
explain why it was a tough fight,

01:32:21.412 --> 01:32:22.062
your last one?

01:32:22.992 --> 01:32:26.832
- First and foremost, it was because I
had lost my father. It was hard to pull

01:32:26.872 --> 01:32:30.702
myself together. Everything
piled up at once. My father was

01:32:30.732 --> 01:32:33.282
gone. And training without my father,

01:32:35.432 --> 01:32:39.372
without the person I was doing
all of this for, the person I

01:32:39.412 --> 01:32:43.662
wanted to become champion for, first
and foremost, to prove to him,

01:32:44.852 --> 01:32:49.612
uh, to prove to him that
I could become the best.

01:32:50.892 --> 01:32:54.792
And then the training
camp itself, physically

01:32:54.932 --> 01:32:59.452
I was not in the best shape. I had
injury after injury after injury.

01:33:00.592 --> 01:33:08.212
But mentally speaking, I stayed
completely sharp. Well, this is when you

01:33:08.292 --> 01:33:11.912
got injuries, your mental
game can slip a little,

01:33:12.952 --> 01:33:16.612
or things at home are going bad.
All of that impacts a person.

01:33:16.792 --> 01:33:22.692
One, two, three, four. All these things
affect you. You become, let's say not,

01:33:22.812 --> 01:33:26.712
not sharp. You lose your fire. Your fire

01:33:26.792 --> 01:33:30.052
leaves you, your focus leaves
you, your head is somewhere else.

01:33:30.732 --> 01:33:34.252
Even though you are physically
there, mentally you are somewhere

01:33:34.312 --> 01:33:37.212
else. But I always
stayed there. I knew it.

01:33:38.492 --> 01:33:42.292
Nobody had any idea that was
going to be my last fight. No

01:33:42.392 --> 01:33:44.972
one knew about the talk
I had with my mother.

01:33:45.352 --> 01:33:46.652
- But you knew and you were sure?

01:33:46.722 --> 01:33:50.592
- Well, I knew for myself. This is my
last fight. I was never going to

01:33:50.652 --> 01:33:54.512
step into the cage again. I
knew it, and I had to finish it

01:33:54.752 --> 01:33:55.961
right then and there because

01:33:57.112 --> 01:34:01.092
putting myself through all of that yet
again, it would have just been too

01:34:01.152 --> 01:34:04.932
hard on me if I got injured
and the fight fell through.

01:34:06.912 --> 01:34:10.652
And putting my mother through all that
stress again. I knew that all the

01:34:10.712 --> 01:34:14.692
anxiety I go through for three months
leading up to a fight, my mother

01:34:14.792 --> 01:34:20.282
goes through it too. I understood all of
that. I wanted to put an end to it because

01:34:20.312 --> 01:34:23.152
we'd already had that
talk. We already had an

01:34:23.232 --> 01:34:28.042
agreement. All of that combined,
I mean, mentally it just-- I was,

01:34:28.912 --> 01:34:31.172
you know, I was right on the edge. Like,

01:34:32.972 --> 01:34:35.871
it was a fine line and I
really was on the edge.

01:34:37.292 --> 01:34:39.432
- And I heard or maybe read somewhere

01:34:40.652 --> 01:34:44.592
that you said you were already
starting to miss it while the fight

01:34:44.632 --> 01:34:45.732
was still going on.

01:34:46.342 --> 01:34:50.112
- Yeah, inside the octagon. I knew
it would never happen again.

01:34:50.532 --> 01:34:54.792
I stepped in there and I just took
it all in. I was fighting and just

01:34:55.812 --> 01:35:01.772
every single moment, every moment,
every single move, I felt it deep down.

01:35:02.972 --> 01:35:06.952
And in my head, I already
knew this was the final run.

01:35:07.032 --> 01:35:11.012
Everything I'd been doing my entire
life, it all came down to this.

01:35:11.072 --> 01:35:14.951
That competitive rush, I'll
never feel that anywhere else.

01:35:15.092 --> 01:35:17.272
I knew deep down this was my last fight.

01:35:18.052 --> 01:35:19.272
- What kind of feelings were those?

01:35:20.352 --> 01:35:23.892
- I've never felt anything like that
again. Those feelings could only be

01:35:23.932 --> 01:35:27.892
experienced right there on that
exact day, that very evening, with

01:35:27.952 --> 01:35:31.772
that opponent inside that cage.
I've never had them before that

01:35:31.812 --> 01:35:35.772
night and never had them since.
And I don't think I'll ever be

01:35:35.852 --> 01:35:37.172
able to feel that way again.

01:35:38.732 --> 01:35:42.272
I think Buvaisar said that these
feelings, standing on the podium,

01:35:42.352 --> 01:35:45.972
becoming an Olympic champion, you
only feel them in that moment when

01:35:46.032 --> 01:35:49.452
they announce you. You step
onto the podium. That's it.

01:35:49.972 --> 01:35:53.772
They announced you. You won.
You leave. That's it. You

01:35:53.832 --> 01:35:57.472
only feel them there. You don't feel them
anywhere else. And athletes live for those

01:35:57.532 --> 01:35:59.992
feelings. They train their
whole lives for them.

01:36:01.452 --> 01:36:05.392
- Do you ever think about that? That
in our lives, if we're lucky, we

01:36:05.412 --> 01:36:10.052
only get maybe one or two moments
like that at that absolute peak.

01:36:10.592 --> 01:36:17.732
- Yes. Yes. Yes. Very rarely. It's so rare.
What is happiness really? The way I see

01:36:17.812 --> 01:36:21.752
it is when you experience certain
feelings for a very short

01:36:21.792 --> 01:36:25.732
time. You can't be constantly
happy. Like you achieve

01:36:25.792 --> 01:36:29.852
something and then you're happy for
years. It just doesn't work that way.

01:36:30.772 --> 01:36:34.432
The Almighty didn't build us that
way. You get there even when you

01:36:34.472 --> 01:36:38.152
become a champion and you
fully feel that moment, you

01:36:38.192 --> 01:36:43.852
win. And then you wake up the next morning
and that's it. Is that all I worked for?

01:36:44.852 --> 01:36:49.432
There's nothing more like that. You
know? Like, can I have seconds?

01:36:50.292 --> 01:36:54.432
You can't have seconds. It's like in
our cafeteria. Can I have seconds?

01:36:56.256 --> 01:37:00.236
So well, you become champion, you
win, then you wake up the next

01:37:00.256 --> 01:37:03.455
day. That's it. Your daily routine
continues. It doesn't end.

01:37:04.476 --> 01:37:08.336
You've already got different things
going on, different problems, other

01:37:08.376 --> 01:37:12.276
tasks now, and you're like, "It all
went by so fast." Then you wake

01:37:12.336 --> 01:37:16.176
up the next day, pack your bags,
head to the airport, fly out,

01:37:16.436 --> 01:37:20.256
land back home, everyone's
celebrating. And just like that, the

01:37:20.336 --> 01:37:25.556
next day everyone's gone. That's it.
Everyone lives their own life. And well...

01:37:27.496 --> 01:37:31.336
But later on, there are
moments, well, you know,

01:37:32.256 --> 01:37:35.716
when you still feel the impact
of that success, let's say.

01:37:37.516 --> 01:37:41.396
Like let's say public recognition,
for example, people's recognition

01:37:41.916 --> 01:37:45.796
and recognition is legacy. A lot
of people get it twisted when

01:37:45.856 --> 01:37:51.475
we start talking about legacy. What even
is a legacy? What does legacy mean, right?

01:37:52.496 --> 01:37:56.436
For me personally, it is
public recognition. Like

01:37:56.496 --> 01:38:00.326
when people come up to you, like parents
come up with their kid and they say,

01:38:00.826 --> 01:38:04.696
"You know, you inspire our son to get
into sports. You're a real role model

01:38:04.776 --> 01:38:09.156
for him." And in that exact
moment, it's like you just--

01:38:10.276 --> 01:38:13.056
you start to realize that
none of this was in vain.

01:38:13.996 --> 01:38:17.596
But here's the thing. Does everyone
approach every champion like that?

01:38:17.916 --> 01:38:21.736
Every successful person? Do people
even come up to them at all?

01:38:22.036 --> 01:38:24.916
That's the question. There are
people who became champions.

01:38:26.276 --> 01:38:29.076
There are people who rose
to the top of their field,

01:38:30.096 --> 01:38:32.476
but they just don't inspire anyone.

01:38:33.656 --> 01:38:37.236
And well, people look at
you like, "Yeah, you were a

01:38:37.296 --> 01:38:39.856
champion, you know, but
nobody wants to be like you."

01:38:41.396 --> 01:38:45.956
There are so many people like that
who achieve success, but they,

01:38:47.896 --> 01:38:53.256
they don't inspire the younger
generation because the youth,

01:38:53.696 --> 01:38:57.856
they look at you and they just
don't see you as a good person.

01:38:59.056 --> 01:39:03.896
And looking at it that way, sports,
it's such a double-edged sword.

01:39:06.016 --> 01:39:08.556
- They lost themselves in it
when they became champions.

01:39:09.656 --> 01:39:11.556
Or maybe they never truly were.

01:39:13.516 --> 01:39:17.916
If we can, I want to talk about MMA
and football in that same context.

01:39:18.756 --> 01:39:23.636
Let's start with MMA. Who do you think
are the greatest fighters of all time?

01:39:24.476 --> 01:39:32.396
You said you have a few favorites. Shogun,
George St-Pierre, and Fedor Emelianenko.

01:39:33.436 --> 01:39:37.256
Let's talk about those fighters and
maybe others who interest you. They all

01:39:37.276 --> 01:39:41.216
had different distinct styles.
So tell me why Shogun.

01:39:41.416 --> 01:39:45.336
You've always said that he was important
to you as a fighter, and naturally

01:39:45.396 --> 01:39:49.096
he had legendary moments in
Pride in two thousand and five.

01:39:49.156 --> 01:39:50.016
- His soccer kick.

01:39:50.276 --> 01:39:51.336
- Yes, that's right.

01:39:51.696 --> 01:39:55.656
- Well, Shogun Rua, when I was fifteen,

01:39:55.856 --> 01:39:59.796
sixteen years old, he was the
Pride champion. At that time, for

01:39:59.856 --> 01:40:03.696
me, it was the best organization in
the world. The best fighters in the

01:40:03.776 --> 01:40:07.176
world competed there.
Fedor, Cro Cop, Shogun,

01:40:07.376 --> 01:40:10.996
Wanderlei Silva, Anderson
Silva. Man, they had

01:40:11.076 --> 01:40:14.756
everybody. Overeem, Dan
Henderson, the Nogueira

01:40:14.776 --> 01:40:18.756
brothers, the Emelianenko
brothers. They were the best at

01:40:18.796 --> 01:40:22.576
that time for me and honestly
for MMA as a whole. And

01:40:22.636 --> 01:40:28.556
right there in second place, in my opinion,
shining right behind Fedor was Shogun.

01:40:29.705 --> 01:40:33.486
And the way he fights, no matter how
they took him down, he'd get right back

01:40:33.556 --> 01:40:37.356
up. They couldn't do a thing to him.
Yeah, his wrestling was lacking, but

01:40:37.416 --> 01:40:40.716
his striking, Thai clinch, punches, kicks,

01:40:41.056 --> 01:40:44.656
knees. When he landed those
soccer kicks, well, he really

01:40:44.736 --> 01:40:51.436
appealed to me as a fighter. Very calm,
careful, very emotionally restrained and,

01:40:52.676 --> 01:40:56.216
and his fight style. His style was
actually at a very high level.

01:40:56.836 --> 01:40:59.496
The way he fought, no
other fighter ever did.

01:41:00.476 --> 01:41:04.136
At that time, he really
impressed me. Then he

01:41:04.196 --> 01:41:08.456
crossed over to the UFC and
became the UFC champion.

01:41:11.236 --> 01:41:14.756
And UFC fights and the
Pride fights at that time,

01:41:15.416 --> 01:41:18.076
I didn't have a computer,
internet, none of that.

01:41:19.496 --> 01:41:23.416
I remember there was this
video rental place. We'd grab

01:41:23.536 --> 01:41:27.416
tapes and watch all these
fights with my father.

01:41:29.356 --> 01:41:33.056
Mauricio Shogun Rua to me was
like the gold standard of a

01:41:33.096 --> 01:41:40.376
fighter back then. I'd say right
alongside Fedor and George St-Pierre.

01:41:40.436 --> 01:41:44.236
- And by the way, when you watched the fights
with your father, did you watch them for

01:41:44.296 --> 01:41:47.016
fun or analyze them too?

01:41:47.726 --> 01:41:51.516
- Mostly we analyzed them. My father
always loved to analyze fights.

01:41:52.136 --> 01:41:55.726
We'd sit like this and he'd
say this, this, chokes,

01:41:56.036 --> 01:41:59.096
striking, wrestling. We
were constantly analyzing.

01:42:00.136 --> 01:42:03.746
There was a fight in two thousand
and six or two thousand and

01:42:03.796 --> 01:42:07.296
seven. I don't even
remember the exact year.

01:42:07.716 --> 01:42:11.696
Approximately around that time when
George St-Pierre fought Sean Sherk.

01:42:12.456 --> 01:42:14.996
We even re-watched that
fight a couple of times.

01:42:16.316 --> 01:42:18.816
Then George St-Pierre fought B.J. Penn,

01:42:19.956 --> 01:42:23.716
the second fight, I think, after
the fourth round when there was a

01:42:23.756 --> 01:42:27.476
stoppage. Then, well, there
were many, many fights

01:42:27.716 --> 01:42:31.296
and my father actually
admired the fighting

01:42:31.456 --> 01:42:34.076
style, the fighting style
of George St-Pierre.

01:42:35.196 --> 01:42:38.956
He really impressed us. And
my father, my father always

01:42:39.016 --> 01:42:46.216
emphasized his timing of takedowns after
striking how he closed the distance. Um,

01:42:47.136 --> 01:42:51.096
and honestly, my whole, you
know, like teenage conscious

01:42:51.156 --> 01:42:54.856
years growing up, like my
formative years transitioning

01:42:54.916 --> 01:42:57.716
into an adult, it was all tied to

01:42:58.936 --> 01:43:02.616
tied to Pride in its prime.
And after the UFC bought

01:43:02.756 --> 01:43:06.716
Pride and those fighters
crossed over, that whole boom

01:43:06.796 --> 01:43:11.436
in 2006, '7, '8 and '9,
the rise of the UFC,

01:43:12.476 --> 01:43:16.276
I remember that era so vividly.
My father and I re-watched so

01:43:16.416 --> 01:43:18.056
many of those UFC fights.

01:43:19.476 --> 01:43:23.076
- Well, I have to ask about Fedor. What
can you say about him? He had some

01:43:23.116 --> 01:43:27.936
legendary fights. He beat Cro
Cop, Big Nog, Mark Coleman.

01:43:28.716 --> 01:43:32.616
- Yeah, and Kevin Randleman.
And then he had those

01:43:32.716 --> 01:43:36.436
legendary fights with the
Japanese fighters who were big

01:43:36.576 --> 01:43:40.556
stars over there. Man, I loved
it. Honestly, he inspired

01:43:40.596 --> 01:43:44.476
me just by the fact that he was
from Russia and he was a Sambo

01:43:44.596 --> 01:43:49.316
fighter. He was closer to our school
of training, Sambo, combat Sambo.

01:43:50.916 --> 01:43:56.676
And there was this
moment back in 2004, the

01:43:56.716 --> 01:44:00.036
Dagestan Combat Sambo
Championship. Can you imagine?

01:44:00.416 --> 01:44:06.936
2004, the Dagestan Combat Sambo Championship,
and Fedor showed up. He showed up.

01:44:07.336 --> 01:44:08.136
- He came.

01:44:08.176 --> 01:44:11.916
- To compete in the Dagestan Open
Combat Sambo Championship while

01:44:11.976 --> 01:44:16.396
being the reigning Pride champion.
I was 15 years old at the time.

01:44:17.956 --> 01:44:21.796
I was right there in the venue. My
father actually invited him. He

01:44:21.836 --> 01:44:25.576
was sending out invitations to
different schools back then. It was

01:44:25.676 --> 01:44:31.456
Stary Oskol where he used to train.
Fedor's coaches were sent an invitation.

01:44:32.676 --> 01:44:36.376
He came with his brother, Alexander. They
both made it to the finals back then.

01:44:37.156 --> 01:44:40.676
I just remember $5,000, $3,000, $1,000,

01:44:41.016 --> 01:44:44.616
first, second, third place. At
heavyweight, they gave a car.

01:44:45.916 --> 01:44:49.716
When the two brothers arrived, they
beat everyone to get to the finals.

01:44:50.096 --> 01:44:53.776
They didn't fight each other. One
of them took first place. That was

01:44:53.816 --> 01:44:57.616
Fedor. Alexander took second
place. I remember back then

01:44:57.936 --> 01:45:01.756
when he was warming up, I was standing next
to him while he warmed up. It was this

01:45:01.816 --> 01:45:05.426
building, you know, a venue
that fits maybe 1,500 people,

01:45:05.816 --> 01:45:09.516
and over there was this partition,
like a wall, and everyone was

01:45:09.555 --> 01:45:13.476
warming up behind that wall. At that time,
well, practically very few people even knew

01:45:13.516 --> 01:45:17.136
who Fedor was, but I knew
Fedor. I knew who he was,

01:45:17.506 --> 01:45:21.276
that he was the Pride champion. At
that time, he beat Nogueira, beat

01:45:21.376 --> 01:45:25.296
Mark Coleman, beat Randleman. This
was before his championship fight

01:45:25.376 --> 01:45:28.896
against Cro Cop. I would come over
and just sit there watching how

01:45:28.936 --> 01:45:32.396
he warmed up, what he did.
I just remember very well,

01:45:33.176 --> 01:45:36.676
I don't know, maybe Fedor will see
this podcast. He had a thermos.

01:45:37.856 --> 01:45:41.556
He had a thermos, and he was just
drinking tea. And I remember he

01:45:41.596 --> 01:45:45.586
fought Ibragim Magomedov. He
was also a legendary fighter of

01:45:45.656 --> 01:45:48.116
ours, a heavyweight who
fought in Pride, too.

01:45:49.556 --> 01:45:53.536
He fought against him. It was either
his first or second fight, and he was

01:45:53.576 --> 01:45:57.456
warming up incredibly well. I
remember how he was warming up

01:45:57.496 --> 01:46:01.376
his nose and his ears. I watched
him, and it was unusual for me

01:46:01.476 --> 01:46:05.116
at that time. I never warmed up my
nose and ears. I just remember--

01:46:05.436 --> 01:46:09.326
I just remember how Fedor was really
working on his nose, his ears, his

01:46:09.376 --> 01:46:13.256
whole face. And he went,
fought, came back.

01:46:13.266 --> 01:46:17.216
This was in Makhachkala
2004. It's the same as a

01:46:17.296 --> 01:46:20.436
UFC champion now coming
and fighting at the

01:46:20.856 --> 01:46:24.796
Dagestan Combat Sambo Championship.
Can you imagine that? Back then, it

01:46:24.816 --> 01:46:28.576
was the best heavyweight organization
in the world. He comes there and

01:46:28.616 --> 01:46:33.116
fights. So he finished a fight, goes back,
and he had this big thermos with him.

01:46:34.096 --> 01:46:35.856
His team was sitting
separately over there.

01:46:37.696 --> 01:46:41.296
He was drinking tea. He
just fought, walked back,

01:46:41.516 --> 01:46:45.496
poured some tea, and drank it. He had
something to snack on with it, maybe honey or

01:46:45.536 --> 01:46:49.186
something. I just don't remember. And
I was, "Wow, Fedor is right here."

01:46:49.856 --> 01:46:53.756
For me, that was just such a, you
know, great source of motivation back

01:46:53.816 --> 01:46:57.616
then. It's really hard to
put those emotions into

01:46:57.636 --> 01:47:01.676
words. By the way, maybe you could
ask him about that on the podcast.

01:47:01.916 --> 01:47:02.776
- About the thermos?

01:47:03.286 --> 01:47:08.266
- No, no. About the tea. What was in it?
Thyme? Mint? What did he put in there?

01:47:09.516 --> 01:47:12.356
- Uh, well, I would love to
talk to him on this podcast,

01:47:13.256 --> 01:47:16.096
but I think he rarely does interviews.

01:47:17.976 --> 01:47:20.976
- I think you can reach him. I can help you.

01:47:22.036 --> 01:47:23.516
- Well, if you could put
in a good word for me.

01:47:23.576 --> 01:47:27.676
- Yeah, of course. I think it would be
interesting for people to hear him.

01:47:27.996 --> 01:47:34.656
- Absolutely. But psychologically,
he's kind of like you in a way.

01:47:35.816 --> 01:47:36.456
- You mean calm?

01:47:37.316 --> 01:47:39.916
- Yeah. Yes, that calmness.

01:47:40.876 --> 01:47:46.496
You see the Pride era, those huge
fights, those really tough matchups,

01:47:47.376 --> 01:47:49.536
and still he always stayed calm.

01:47:50.076 --> 01:47:50.776
- It's his aura.

01:47:51.636 --> 01:47:51.836
- Yeah?

01:47:52.256 --> 01:47:56.156
- I don't know about others, but for
me, that was his aura. The fact

01:47:56.216 --> 01:48:00.116
that he'd come out with such a
calm, cool-headed look and knock

01:48:00.156 --> 01:48:03.816
out giants, strong fighters
at that time, right?

01:48:04.976 --> 01:48:09.716
You know, we used to joke
about his face, that he,

01:48:11.216 --> 01:48:16.416
kind of looks like a kindergarten teacher,
you know? And then he goes out there and

01:48:17.616 --> 01:48:19.676
knocks out absolute giants.

01:48:20.896 --> 01:48:24.756
And he was like that. Both before
and after the fight, he never showed

01:48:24.796 --> 01:48:30.356
much emotion. But I think we're a little
different in that regard. I think Fedor,

01:48:31.276 --> 01:48:35.396
by temperament, is calmer than
I am. That's my impression.

01:48:36.336 --> 01:48:42.756
But with that cold-blooded calmness of
his, I think he got to a lot of people

01:48:42.796 --> 01:48:43.366
mentally.

01:48:44.996 --> 01:48:50.516
- It's like something's happening inside
both of you, like something's boiling

01:48:50.576 --> 01:48:53.846
inside. So when we look at your face-

01:48:54.736 --> 01:48:58.496
- Of course it's boiling. What do
you think? You know what? It's

01:48:58.556 --> 01:49:02.516
one thing when it's boiling inside
and you show it, but there are

01:49:02.576 --> 01:49:04.286
some who can't hide their feelings.

01:49:05.900 --> 01:49:09.800
But it's a different thing when it's
boiling inside and on the surface you stay

01:49:09.860 --> 01:49:13.440
cold, cold-blooded, calm, and you
can handle all these different

01:49:13.540 --> 01:49:17.340
emotions that hit you at the same time.
There are a lot of them, and they

01:49:17.420 --> 01:49:21.280
all hit differently. When
you compete, what makes a

01:49:21.440 --> 01:49:25.380
fighter, an athlete at the
highest level so unique is

01:49:25.440 --> 01:49:29.200
that so many different
emotions hit you at the same

01:49:29.320 --> 01:49:33.280
time. Fear, nerves, the
fact that people are

01:49:33.340 --> 01:49:38.240
watching you, the thought that you
might perform badly, that you might

01:49:39.220 --> 01:49:42.800
not show what you've trained
for, that you might mess it up,

01:49:43.320 --> 01:49:47.000
that you might, I don't
know, fall. So much

01:49:47.060 --> 01:49:52.580
enters your head, and you can put each
emotion where it belongs. So, you know,

01:49:53.580 --> 01:49:57.560
if all of that gets inside your
head and turns into chaos, you

01:49:57.600 --> 01:50:00.820
won't be able to handle it
emotionally, but you stay

01:50:00.880 --> 01:50:04.620
cold-blooded. And you
know that if you don't

01:50:04.700 --> 01:50:08.520
handle these emotions that
are coming at you, if you

01:50:08.560 --> 01:50:12.440
don't handle this pressure, then
it will be difficult for you to

01:50:12.500 --> 01:50:16.040
compete because the main competition
is against yourself first.

01:50:16.840 --> 01:50:20.200
You fight yourself, you
beat yourself, and only

01:50:20.320 --> 01:50:23.740
then do you actually start
competing with your opponent.

01:50:24.440 --> 01:50:27.760
So I think it happens to
everyone, not just him or me.

01:50:28.560 --> 01:50:31.440
The big difference here is
how much you can control it.

01:50:32.860 --> 01:50:34.640
- Fedor himself famously said,

01:50:35.470 --> 01:50:39.500
"The only person who can defeat me is me.
The only person who can beat me is me."

01:50:40.080 --> 01:50:44.020
- He was talking about that fight with
yourself, I'm sure. Because if you lose

01:50:44.100 --> 01:50:47.260
to yourself, then you'll lose
to your opponent very easily.

01:50:48.920 --> 01:50:51.020
- There's an interesting
thing I came across.

01:50:52.060 --> 01:50:55.940
Adam Saitiev said that high-level
athletes often make a mistake,

01:50:57.020 --> 01:51:01.060
and what they do wrong is
that when they feel fear,

01:51:02.040 --> 01:51:07.760
they tend to ignore it. He believes
that you have to confront that fear

01:51:08.620 --> 01:51:09.440
and overcome it.

01:51:10.580 --> 01:51:14.400
- Yes. Psychologists talk about
how to work through those

01:51:14.520 --> 01:51:21.400
thoughts when they arise. You know, when
fear comes up and you feel it, that's

01:51:21.520 --> 01:51:26.540
absolutely normal. It's what
the Almighty put in us.

01:51:27.350 --> 01:51:31.320
People get scared, people
get cold, people feel pain,

01:51:31.640 --> 01:51:35.600
people get hungry. These are
things that are inside all of

01:51:35.680 --> 01:51:39.140
us. The real question is
how you deal with them,

01:51:39.580 --> 01:51:41.900
how you overcome them within yourself.

01:51:42.900 --> 01:51:46.880
100% you can't ignore it, and you
must work through it. You have to

01:51:46.890 --> 01:51:50.120
answer the questions, "Why did this
happen? Could I do it this way

01:51:50.180 --> 01:51:54.739
instead?" And this comes only with
experience, only with experience.

01:51:56.560 --> 01:52:00.820
And the more you compete, you know, the
more you train and the more you face it.

01:52:02.480 --> 01:52:05.820
Some people actually try
to avoid that moment.

01:52:06.380 --> 01:52:12.400
But the more you face it, the better
and better you'll become each time.

01:52:14.040 --> 01:52:18.020
It's the same with athletes.
They get nervous. That's what

01:52:18.080 --> 01:52:21.840
we call the jitters, right? At
first, whenever someone competes,

01:52:22.140 --> 01:52:27.740
they get the jitters. But when you keep
competing over and over again, you gradually

01:52:27.800 --> 01:52:31.980
learn how to channel those
nerves in the right direction.

01:52:33.100 --> 01:52:34.740
Then it gets easier for you.

01:52:35.700 --> 01:52:39.360
And in order to get there, you
have to compete. And what is

01:52:39.400 --> 01:52:43.060
competition? You meet this
fear face to face. You don't

01:52:43.140 --> 01:52:47.240
avoid it. A lot of guys are
staying away from competitions.

01:52:48.180 --> 01:52:51.980
When they're young, they have this
tension, this self-consciousness, this

01:52:52.020 --> 01:52:55.500
fear, the jitters. They're
nervous. They can't sleep at

01:52:55.580 --> 01:52:58.240
night. You have to go through
all of that yourself.

01:52:59.320 --> 01:53:03.160
Nobody can do it for you. A coach
can train you. A coach can teach

01:53:03.220 --> 01:53:07.300
you. But all he can say is,
"Practice, practice, practice."

01:53:08.130 --> 01:53:13.800
Then there's you, alone with your
own feelings. When you lie down,

01:53:14.360 --> 01:53:18.280
when you wake up, when you walk,
when you're by yourself, when

01:53:18.340 --> 01:53:22.260
you eat, there are so many
moments when a person has to go

01:53:22.340 --> 01:53:29.400
through all of this on his own. And when
you have to go through this over and over

01:53:29.480 --> 01:53:33.390
again, some people back
away. They don't want

01:53:33.420 --> 01:53:36.560
to face it. And what do you need for that?

01:53:37.410 --> 01:53:41.200
Patience. You have to
come and endure it and go

01:53:41.240 --> 01:53:45.100
through all of it, through your
skin, through your muscles, through

01:53:45.160 --> 01:53:49.020
your mind. You must let all of
this pass through you and overcome

01:53:49.060 --> 01:53:52.240
it. And each time you'll get better at it.

01:53:54.440 --> 01:53:57.680
- A good example of that is the
third person you mentioned, George

01:53:57.800 --> 01:54:00.800
St-Pierre, who by the
way sends his regards.

01:54:01.680 --> 01:54:05.440
I train with him a lot, and he has
a very deep respect for you. But

01:54:05.520 --> 01:54:09.260
he didn't give me much good advice because
when I told him I was going to train with you,

01:54:09.520 --> 01:54:13.400
he told me... What did he
say? He texted me, "If you

01:54:13.520 --> 01:54:15.300
die, you die." That's what he said.

01:54:17.060 --> 01:54:18.360
- Good advice. He's a wise man.

01:54:19.460 --> 01:54:21.520
- Didn't support me and said, "Say hi."

01:54:21.920 --> 01:54:23.000
- Tell him I say hi, too.

01:54:23.220 --> 01:54:26.980
- As we were talking about, he
himself has spoken about this,

01:54:28.670 --> 01:54:33.800
that he was afraid and always felt
fear before every single fight.

01:54:34.900 --> 01:54:38.300
That's interesting. One
of the greatest fighters-

01:54:38.840 --> 01:54:39.680
- In history, yeah.

01:54:40.010 --> 01:54:41.160
- And even he feels fear.

01:54:41.780 --> 01:54:45.260
- He's not afraid to admit
it. Everybody has fear.

01:54:45.580 --> 01:54:50.580
Everybody gets afraid. But I don't
know how big that fear was for him.

01:54:51.520 --> 01:54:55.290
I really don't know. We
never discussed it. But

01:54:55.420 --> 01:55:01.120
I've watched a lot of his interviews,
his fights, and his training camps.

01:55:02.120 --> 01:55:06.080
The way he analyzed his past
fights and all that, the way

01:55:06.200 --> 01:55:10.108
I see it, he's a very sensitive
person. There are people like that

01:55:10.128 --> 01:55:13.848
too. Some guys take all of
this very lightly. Like,

01:55:13.908 --> 01:55:17.828
"Okay, we fight tomorrow.
We'll see who my opponent is

01:55:18.028 --> 01:55:21.488
and how he shows up, right?"
But there are others who,

01:55:22.508 --> 01:55:25.608
who find it very difficult
to handle psychologically.

01:55:26.528 --> 01:55:32.428
But the Almighty gave me this kind of
calmness. Sometimes I'd get to the arena,

01:55:33.508 --> 01:55:38.448
and they bring you in four hours before
the fight. They have to weigh you,

01:55:39.428 --> 01:55:43.188
they have to do doping control,
and they have to tape you

01:55:43.328 --> 01:55:46.808
up. There are so many athletic
commission procedures.

01:55:47.388 --> 01:55:49.118
Every state has different rules,

01:55:50.188 --> 01:55:54.008
how they bring you into the arena
and all that. Sometimes I'd

01:55:54.048 --> 01:55:57.988
get there four hours early and
think, "What am I supposed to do?"

01:55:58.028 --> 01:56:01.918
So I make myself a little
bed and say, "Please,

01:56:02.048 --> 01:56:06.008
Keep it quiet." I would lie
down. I could sleep there for an

01:56:06.108 --> 01:56:09.868
hour, maybe an hour and a half.
That's it. Wake up, warm up, and

01:56:09.908 --> 01:56:13.688
then I could go out and fight.
And some guys, they don't

01:56:13.747 --> 01:56:15.368
sleep for two days before the fight.

01:56:16.688 --> 01:56:20.328
Believe me, there are fighters who
don't sleep the night before a

01:56:20.368 --> 01:56:24.098
fight, and it's very hard
on them. It's not really

01:56:24.148 --> 01:56:27.888
fear. It's hard to describe with just

01:56:28.008 --> 01:56:31.588
one emotion. It's too many
emotions that come over a

01:56:31.668 --> 01:56:38.148
person, and they can't fall asleep.
It's panic, it's fear, it's worry, it's

01:56:38.248 --> 01:56:42.148
nervousness, it's the jitters.
All of that builds up,

01:56:42.528 --> 01:56:46.108
and the person just can't fall
asleep. So in that regard,

01:56:47.768 --> 01:56:53.068
the Almighty gave me a little more
calmness than others. I had, you know,

01:56:53.968 --> 01:56:56.748
such a cool head and
calmness before the fights.

01:56:57.728 --> 01:57:01.948
- Now you're in this new
position as a leader,

01:57:02.908 --> 01:57:06.788
and you're looking at all these
athletes, these young guys who

01:57:06.808 --> 01:57:08.728
are already at the very top of the world.

01:57:09.628 --> 01:57:12.018
And I think they're all very
different from one another

01:57:13.068 --> 01:57:19.778
psychologically, physically, and
stylistically. What can you say as a

01:57:19.907 --> 01:57:23.668
coach? I know you're not a coach,
but as a leader, how do you figure

01:57:23.748 --> 01:57:29.508
out what each person needs? Like
now we were watching Umar prepare.

01:57:30.128 --> 01:57:31.908
That each person needs
something different.

01:57:32.548 --> 01:57:36.448
- Yes, everyone is different.
Different styles, different

01:57:36.548 --> 01:57:39.928
mentalities, different mindsets, different

01:57:40.028 --> 01:57:43.688
approaches to fight week and
to preparation as a whole.

01:57:44.408 --> 01:57:48.168
But you have to adapt to
all of it. Here, you can't

01:57:48.208 --> 01:57:52.188
just take one approach. Let's
say we take my approach

01:57:53.108 --> 01:57:58.168
and apply it to everyone
else, to Usman, Islam,

01:57:58.998 --> 01:58:01.648
and Umar. It doesn't work like that.

01:58:02.648 --> 01:58:06.528
Everyone needs their own approach.
I knew them since they were

01:58:06.608 --> 01:58:10.468
nobody, and I know them now
when they are the best. So you

01:58:10.508 --> 01:58:14.308
have to treat each one differently.
I'm very lucky to have Javier

01:58:14.368 --> 01:58:18.128
Mendez. He's been by my side since 2012.

01:58:18.688 --> 01:58:22.488
And when I became a coach
after I finished my career,

01:58:22.748 --> 01:58:26.417
he was there with me as a
coach. I switched from being a

01:58:26.448 --> 01:58:30.288
fighter to a coaching role. My
father was no longer with us.

01:58:30.848 --> 01:58:34.568
And together with Javier Mendez,
we've been guiding them for

01:58:34.648 --> 01:58:39.628
these last six years. And his
experience helps me tremendously

01:58:40.468 --> 01:58:43.568
because there is no coach, no MMA coach

01:58:44.448 --> 01:58:47.748
with the kind of results
Javier Mendez has. You

01:58:47.788 --> 01:58:50.948
know? So many champions, double champions,

01:58:51.388 --> 01:58:55.408
UFC fighters. So many people
have trained under him,

01:58:56.388 --> 01:58:59.208
from Frank Shamrock all the
way to Islam Makhachev.

01:59:00.768 --> 01:59:02.858
So many fighters have trained under him,

01:59:03.668 --> 01:59:07.417
which makes things very easy
for me because I have Javier

01:59:07.528 --> 01:59:11.708
Mendez by my side, and he can
help me with so many things.

01:59:12.388 --> 01:59:16.208
- What do you think is the secret
behind Javier's genius? Why is he

01:59:16.268 --> 01:59:18.988
such a great coach for so
many different athletes?

01:59:21.088 --> 01:59:24.628
- It's the mental side of things.
I'm not gonna say he's one of the

01:59:25.348 --> 01:59:29.088
best at holding pads or
anything like that, but

01:59:29.098 --> 01:59:33.068
his understanding of MMA
itself, his approach

01:59:33.078 --> 01:59:36.388
to training camp, to the
whole preparation process,

01:59:37.108 --> 01:59:41.028
and the amount of attention
he pays to sparring

01:59:41.088 --> 01:59:44.828
inside the cage. I'm not talking
about those sparring sessions

01:59:44.888 --> 01:59:48.798
with 50 people in the gym and
everyone is sparring with each

01:59:48.848 --> 01:59:52.648
other. For example, when I was
preparing for a five-round fight,

01:59:53.228 --> 01:59:56.628
he would always bring in two
or three high-level opponents,

01:59:56.968 --> 01:59:59.868
constantly rotating in
fresh sparring partners.

02:00:00.438 --> 02:00:01.948
- And he would watch and analyze.

02:00:02.108 --> 02:00:05.928
- I don't remember him ever missing
a single sparring session since

02:00:06.068 --> 02:00:12.048
2012. Can you imagine? 14 years have gone
by, and right now we're in training camp.

02:00:13.808 --> 02:00:17.568
He's there for every training
session, morning and evening.

02:00:17.608 --> 02:00:21.288
He's at every sparring session,
watching everyone, coaching them,

02:00:21.448 --> 02:00:25.388
giving advice, guiding them. He's
incredibly dedicated to what he

02:00:25.428 --> 02:00:29.328
does. He's either all in or
he steps away completely.

02:00:31.168 --> 02:00:34.308
He never shows up and gives only 50%.

02:00:35.248 --> 02:00:38.988
I remember one situation.
Abubakar was getting ready to

02:00:39.088 --> 02:00:43.008
fight in WSOF, and I had
another student fighting there

02:00:43.048 --> 02:00:46.498
as well. Abubakar went through
the entire preparation with us,

02:00:47.088 --> 02:00:51.028
but the other guy didn't. When we
got there, I told the coach, "Coach,

02:00:51.208 --> 02:00:54.868
can you work his corner with me
too?" He said, "You know, Khabib,

02:00:55.368 --> 02:00:59.088
I haven't seen his preparation. I
don't know what he's capable of. He

02:00:59.148 --> 02:01:02.628
didn't train under me. But since
you're asking, I'll do it.

02:01:03.108 --> 02:01:04.848
But for the future, remember,

02:01:05.708 --> 02:01:09.448
I don't really like to corner
guys I haven't prepared." I

02:01:09.488 --> 02:01:12.837
didn't understand him then,
but now I do. This was

02:01:12.968 --> 02:01:19.168
2016 or maybe 2017. I don't want
to be wrong. And now I understand.

02:01:20.308 --> 02:01:24.287
Now when people ask me, I'm
like, "I didn't prepare you.

02:01:24.448 --> 02:01:25.688
I don't know how ready you are."

02:01:27.028 --> 02:01:31.008
I'm not ready to go in there and
give all my energy to someone who

02:01:31.068 --> 02:01:32.528
didn't go through camp with me.

02:01:34.068 --> 02:01:38.028
Now I understand that. At that
time, I told the coach, "Can

02:01:38.068 --> 02:01:41.428
you come out and corner him with
me?" He said, "No problem."

02:01:42.608 --> 02:01:46.508
We went out there, Abubakar
won his fight, and the

02:01:46.588 --> 02:01:51.808
other guy's fight ended in a draw. But
that's not really what I'm getting at.

02:01:53.028 --> 02:01:56.968
I've come to the point where if I
haven't trained someone for two, three,

02:01:57.108 --> 02:02:01.088
or four months, I don't like to
corner them because they didn't

02:02:01.148 --> 02:02:04.388
go through a full training camp,
and I don't know how they'll

02:02:04.428 --> 02:02:07.648
respond in the third,
fourth, or fifth round.

02:02:09.228 --> 02:02:13.008
I don't know how he prepared. I
don't know how ready he is to go

02:02:13.068 --> 02:02:16.968
into those deep waters. That's
why I try not to corner

02:02:17.068 --> 02:02:19.648
just anyone. I learned that from Javier.

02:02:20.948 --> 02:02:23.688
- So you need to know he can
go into those deep waters.

02:02:24.708 --> 02:02:29.108
- How deep he can go. I need to know
exactly what he's capable of.

02:02:30.528 --> 02:02:34.228
- What can you say about one of the
greatest fighters and athletes ever,

02:02:34.828 --> 02:02:37.908
Daniel DC Cormier, who you train with?

02:02:39.228 --> 02:02:42.968
You guys joke around all the time, so it's
easy to forget how great he really is.

02:02:43.588 --> 02:02:48.188
What can you say about him as a training
partner, as a coach, as a person?

02:02:48.868 --> 02:02:56.128
- When we talk about DC, we have to go
back to 2004, 2008. He was a two-time

02:02:56.188 --> 02:03:00.138
Olympian. He represented Team
USA at the Olympic Games,

02:03:00.708 --> 02:03:06.328
and that carries a lot of weight in this
sport, especially in the 96-kilo division.

02:03:07.988 --> 02:03:11.628
And he is a two-time Olympian.
Then he transitioned to

02:03:11.728 --> 02:03:15.058
MMA, won a few fights on
the regional scene, went

02:03:15.108 --> 02:03:18.888
to Strikeforce, won fights there.
They put him into the Grand

02:03:18.968 --> 02:03:23.808
Prix. He won the Grand Prix, became heavyweight
champion, and then went to the UFC.

02:03:24.788 --> 02:03:28.338
When he moved to the UFC,
he cut down to 205 pounds

02:03:28.847 --> 02:03:32.048
to make room for Cain Velasquez
because they were best

02:03:32.108 --> 02:03:34.548
friends. Cain was the
heavyweight champion.

02:03:35.688 --> 02:03:39.088
So he moved to 205, and
they both became champions,

02:03:39.708 --> 02:03:42.328
one at light heavyweight
and one at heavyweight.

02:03:43.248 --> 02:03:47.768
Later, when Cain lost the title, he
moved to heavyweight and won there too.

02:03:48.588 --> 02:03:51.608
He's one of the most unique
athletes who has ever lived.

02:03:52.928 --> 02:03:56.818
How many athletes do you know who
competed at the World Championships,

02:03:57.288 --> 02:04:00.688
at the Olympic Games in
freestyle wrestling, then

02:04:00.748 --> 02:04:04.348
transitioned to MMA and
became Strikeforce champion,

02:04:04.988 --> 02:04:09.188
UFC light heavyweight champion,
and UFC heavyweight champion?

02:04:10.568 --> 02:04:14.148
And he's also remembered for
his rivalry with one of the

02:04:14.308 --> 02:04:18.208
greatest, if not the
greatest, Jon Jones. But

02:04:18.288 --> 02:04:22.628
by the time he fought Jon Jones,
he was no longer at his peak.

02:04:23.508 --> 02:04:31.148
I think he was already pushing 40. Even
though we have this friendly, joking

02:04:31.228 --> 02:04:37.388
relationship because he's such a fun,
interesting guy, as an athlete though,

02:04:38.488 --> 02:04:41.088
he's truly one of a kind.

02:04:41.868 --> 02:04:44.548
- As a commentator too, he's
very good with words.

02:04:45.908 --> 02:04:49.828
- Educated, well-mannered, strong.

02:04:49.908 --> 02:04:56.388
He has so many great qualities.
Daniel Cormier is one of those people

02:04:58.088 --> 02:05:00.948
who I think can truly inspire others.

02:05:02.348 --> 02:05:07.708
- So who usually wins when you
guys wrestle? You or DC?

02:05:08.788 --> 02:05:12.668
- Well, he's got a few years on
me. But back in my prime, when

02:05:12.788 --> 02:05:16.268
I was competing at lightweight,
I weighed around 84,

02:05:16.508 --> 02:05:24.008
85, maybe 86 kilos. DC was always
around 107, 110. Those are completely

02:05:24.088 --> 02:05:27.888
different weight classes. DC
was better at wrestling and

02:05:28.008 --> 02:05:31.148
as an Olympian too, all joking aside.

02:05:32.088 --> 02:05:32.828
- So you feel—

02:05:33.348 --> 02:05:37.238
- Back then it was really tough, of
course, but now it's different. Now

02:05:37.348 --> 02:05:41.318
I can go after him. But
I learned so much from

02:05:41.328 --> 02:05:45.048
Cormier. Honestly, so much. As a person, a

02:05:45.068 --> 02:05:48.708
wrestler, and a fighter. You
know, we spent our best years

02:05:48.748 --> 02:05:52.948
together at AKA from 2012 to 2020.

02:05:54.608 --> 02:05:58.328
We've shared so many memories
and moments together as well.

02:05:59.908 --> 02:06:03.768
- I just think it's a shame that people don't
talk about him as one of the top five, top

02:06:03.808 --> 02:06:04.108
10.

02:06:04.808 --> 02:06:08.028
- Well, top 10 for sure. One of the
top 10 greatest, no question.

02:06:08.128 --> 02:06:08.708
- Absolutely.

02:06:08.748 --> 02:06:10.388
- Yeah, I definitely think so.

02:06:10.488 --> 02:06:14.348
- It's a shame, but you see this in football
and in other sports too. One rivalry

02:06:14.408 --> 02:06:18.168
can overshadow an entire
legacy. For DC, it's Jon Jones.

02:06:18.688 --> 02:06:21.328
- Well, Jon Jones was the better athlete.

02:06:22.248 --> 02:06:26.068
There's no doubt about that.
But DC was right there with

02:06:26.108 --> 02:06:31.348
him. Yes, Jon Jones won, but
DC gave him a real fight.

02:06:32.848 --> 02:06:38.488
He was that very person, you know, who
actually made Jon Jones train harder.

02:06:39.748 --> 02:06:43.708
People shouldn't forget that.
I'm sure Jon Jones prepared for

02:06:43.748 --> 02:06:47.568
that fight like it was the
hardest fight of his life.

02:06:49.148 --> 02:06:52.448
- Well, we've talked about
different athletes, about Umar,

02:06:53.148 --> 02:06:57.028
but now let's talk about Islam
Makhachev, one of your closest

02:06:57.048 --> 02:07:01.008
brothers, who's at the very
top of the world. You've known

02:07:01.048 --> 02:07:04.408
each other. You've trained
together for more than 20 years.

02:07:05.228 --> 02:07:08.528
In your opinion, what makes him such
a special wrestler and fighter?

02:07:09.528 --> 02:07:13.088
Why has he reached such heights?
Why is he a great fighter?

02:07:14.328 --> 02:07:17.918
- Well, let's start with the fact
that Islam has been doing this for

02:07:19.076 --> 02:07:22.856
His whole life, since childhood. And
I've known him since childhood too.

02:07:23.276 --> 02:07:27.176
We went to the same school, lived in
the same neighborhood, trained at the

02:07:27.216 --> 02:07:29.436
same gym, and he was my father's student.

02:07:30.556 --> 02:07:36.816
And it's worth mentioning, he was one
of my father's favorite students.

02:07:36.896 --> 02:07:43.636
One of the best students. My father saw in
him this potential for greatness in how

02:07:43.696 --> 02:07:50.136
hard he trained. That's what I was talking
about at the beginning of the podcast.

02:07:51.736 --> 02:07:55.446
When you go into the dark,
uncomfortable room and

02:07:55.656 --> 02:07:59.176
stay there for a long time,
no matter how hard it gets.

02:08:00.436 --> 02:08:04.756
He knew how to make sacrifices.
He was very disciplined.

02:08:05.676 --> 02:08:09.336
Nobody reaches this
level by accident. It's

02:08:09.396 --> 02:08:17.396
impossible without exceptional natural
gifts. And he has them. He's gifted. Allah

02:08:17.456 --> 02:08:24.936
blessed him with those gifts. And he's
deeply devoted to what he does. We basically

02:08:25.636 --> 02:08:29.436
grew up together, right? I'm
three years older than Islam.

02:08:30.106 --> 02:08:33.736
And I was always a little
bigger, a little older than him.

02:08:34.656 --> 02:08:38.376
So when we were amateurs,
I competed in slightly

02:08:38.436 --> 02:08:42.216
heavier weight classes. But
we worked together a lot.

02:08:42.986 --> 02:08:46.736
And Islam benefited tremendously
from training with me.

02:08:47.496 --> 02:08:51.276
We trained together for many
years. And he never had to

02:08:51.376 --> 02:08:53.976
go anywhere to find a sparring partner

02:08:54.816 --> 02:08:58.716
because he always had one in
the gym. So I developed by

02:08:58.756 --> 02:09:02.616
training with the older guys in the
gym, the stronger guys, the better guys

02:09:03.016 --> 02:09:06.716
when I was young. Then when they
left, I was the one in front.

02:09:06.956 --> 02:09:10.416
Islam kept growing, growing,
growing. Now I left.

02:09:10.736 --> 02:09:14.656
Islam is the one in front. And now
other guys are developing through him.

02:09:15.096 --> 02:09:18.836
This is a kind of chain
that exists in a gym, in a

02:09:18.876 --> 02:09:22.516
training system that produces
the best fighters in the world.

02:09:23.356 --> 02:09:27.256
And in this chain, Islam was
always behind me. If I was

02:09:27.296 --> 02:09:30.976
behind someone, Islam was behind me. Now

02:09:31.056 --> 02:09:34.766
others are following.
Islam, Usman, Amru, and

02:09:34.896 --> 02:09:38.756
other guys. You could put them
against any of the best fighters in

02:09:38.816 --> 02:09:41.736
the world right now, and they
would perform very well.

02:09:43.216 --> 02:09:51.576
So in this chain we've had since 2003,
and now it's already 2026, that's

02:09:51.796 --> 02:09:56.416
23 years. Islam was the
one coming up behind me.

02:09:57.316 --> 02:10:00.876
And now there are younger
guys coming up behind

02:10:01.036 --> 02:10:04.976
Islam. And it's no secret
that Islam will turn 35 in

02:10:05.036 --> 02:10:08.796
October. But what makes me
happy is that there are younger

02:10:08.876 --> 02:10:12.316
guys coming up behind him.
I left. Islam stepped in.

02:10:12.736 --> 02:10:16.696
One day Islam will leave too, and
others will take his place. And this

02:10:16.736 --> 02:10:20.586
cycle, it never ends. It keeps
going, and it keeps pulling the

02:10:20.656 --> 02:10:21.876
next generation forward.

02:10:22.816 --> 02:10:26.476
At the beginning, a lot of people
are involved. But as you gradually

02:10:26.536 --> 02:10:29.996
climb higher, higher, and
higher, the road gets narrower,

02:10:30.456 --> 02:10:34.176
and fewer and fewer people
remain. And alongside Islam,

02:10:34.516 --> 02:10:37.516
a lot of guys were developing
while training with me.

02:10:38.656 --> 02:10:46.396
But Islam made it all the way because of
his work ethic, his discipline, and let's

02:10:46.436 --> 02:10:52.996
call it sacrifice. He gave up a lot
to reach this level. This isn't

02:10:53.076 --> 02:10:58.356
easy. You have to make
sacrifices. You have to make

02:10:58.416 --> 02:11:03.556
sacrifices. You have to give up
parties. You have to sacrifice

02:11:03.716 --> 02:11:07.626
sleep. You have to
sacrifice your time. There

02:11:07.636 --> 02:11:11.116
are so many things you have
to give up. You have to

02:11:11.256 --> 02:11:15.136
dedicate your entire life to this
sport, putting aside all the

02:11:15.236 --> 02:11:18.856
things that can distract
you. You train, you sleep,

02:11:19.196 --> 02:11:23.056
you eat, you wake up, and then
you do it all over again.

02:11:23.636 --> 02:11:28.476
Only then can you reach the level that Islam
reached. That's what makes him unique.

02:11:29.656 --> 02:11:35.356
- Were there any small but interesting
differences between you and Islam,

02:11:36.455 --> 02:11:37.496
psychologically speaking?

02:11:38.145 --> 02:11:38.746
- There are many.

02:11:39.576 --> 02:11:43.496
Psychologically, he's very solid
too. I've been around him through so

02:11:43.536 --> 02:11:48.656
many training camps and so many fights.
He's built very strong mentally.

02:11:49.536 --> 02:11:53.156
Of course, we're different in certain
things, but in terms of our fighter's

02:11:53.216 --> 02:11:57.536
attitude toward their work, we
have a lot in common. A lot.

02:11:57.936 --> 02:11:58.956
- You also train a lot.

02:11:59.156 --> 02:12:00.736
- Yeah, absolutely. A lot.

02:12:00.996 --> 02:12:07.166
- And I think he... Well, first I have
to say, I watched you train yesterday,

02:12:08.296 --> 02:12:11.896
and it made me really happy to
see that you still love this so

02:12:11.936 --> 02:12:16.976
deeply. You know, it's hard to love
something for such a long time.

02:12:17.696 --> 02:12:23.756
- People finish and move on. They retire
and move on. But me, it's been six years

02:12:23.816 --> 02:12:27.656
since I retired, and I still
haven't let go of wrestling, of

02:12:27.736 --> 02:12:31.376
competition. I still go
to the gym, do cardio,

02:12:31.816 --> 02:12:36.136
lift, wrestle, grapple. I never
let it go. It's still with me.

02:12:37.156 --> 02:12:43.276
And yesterday I told you that last week
I did 200 minutes of live grappling.

02:12:44.276 --> 02:12:48.036
This week is a little different
though, because I'm just focused on

02:12:48.216 --> 02:12:51.936
Umar, traveling, and everything
that comes with that.

02:12:53.016 --> 02:12:56.756
So this week it's going to be less.
Still, I wrestle. I don't let

02:12:56.856 --> 02:13:00.376
go. I love it. I love this
energy. I love that feeling after

02:13:00.416 --> 02:13:03.356
wrestling. You wrestle,
let's say 40 minutes.

02:13:03.856 --> 02:13:08.376
Yesterday I still wanted more. Just
now I wrestled with you, with Islam,

02:13:09.296 --> 02:13:13.656
maybe 20 minutes, but still it's
not enough to get that feeling,

02:13:14.496 --> 02:13:18.276
that 100% feeling. I need around 40

02:13:18.376 --> 02:13:22.236
minutes of wrestling, something
like that. I don't think

02:13:22.296 --> 02:13:25.436
most people listening to
us will understand that.

02:13:27.524 --> 02:13:33.164
But I think some people watching,
especially grappling and jujitsu fans,

02:13:33.964 --> 02:13:36.264
will understand me.

02:13:36.324 --> 02:13:40.004
- Well, to be honest, sometimes
there are cameras around you, and

02:13:40.224 --> 02:13:44.884
for me at least, that's harder.
I like it when there are none.

02:13:45.464 --> 02:13:49.304
- I usually train with no cameras.
With you was one of the

02:13:49.364 --> 02:13:52.034
rare occasions when I come to the gym and-

02:13:52.524 --> 02:13:53.064
- You allow?

02:13:53.244 --> 02:13:54.484
- When I allow it, yes.

02:13:54.904 --> 02:13:55.984
- Thanks for that.

02:13:56.024 --> 02:13:59.324
- Yeah, yeah, yeah. This is very,
very rare for me. Even with

02:13:59.423 --> 02:14:04.184
Embedded, with UFC, with everyone who
comes, I always say, "Don't film."

02:14:04.864 --> 02:14:08.804
- Thank you, brother. I don't
know why, but cameras get in

02:14:08.844 --> 02:14:12.644
the way for me because this sport,
this preparation, it happens

02:14:12.704 --> 02:14:17.564
here, and that whole world where
celebrities matter, it gets in the way.

02:14:18.264 --> 02:14:25.964
- Yes, it gets in the way. But 99.9% of our
training sessions happen without cameras.

02:14:26.004 --> 02:14:29.824
- Well, thank you, brother. Let me
ask you this. A lot of people say

02:14:29.864 --> 02:14:33.424
you're number one, the
greatest of all time. Do you

02:14:33.504 --> 02:14:39.284
think Islam has a chance to become
the greatest MMA fighter of all time?

02:14:39.684 --> 02:14:43.444
- Well, I've never said that, and
I never set out to become number

02:14:43.564 --> 02:14:48.564
one or to become the greatest
athlete and so on. I never had that.

02:14:50.024 --> 02:14:53.684
My goal was always to become
the best in my sport,

02:14:54.164 --> 02:14:57.384
to become a world champion,
to prove to myself

02:14:57.904 --> 02:15:02.224
that I could do it. But it's
hard to choose just one person.

02:15:03.044 --> 02:15:06.934
Even now, look, there's always
going to be St-Pierre, there's

02:15:07.064 --> 02:15:10.964
always going to be Demetrious
Johnson, there's always going to

02:15:11.004 --> 02:15:14.904
be Jon Jones, there's always
going to be Fedor. If people

02:15:14.964 --> 02:15:18.764
want to include me in that
conversation, if Islam gets into

02:15:18.824 --> 02:15:22.594
that conversation, if there's
Cain Velasquez, Cormier, and

02:15:22.724 --> 02:15:26.174
so on, I'd be happy about
that. And that's my goal,

02:15:26.544 --> 02:15:30.264
that someone from our team,
from the people closest to me,

02:15:30.704 --> 02:15:34.584
gets there too. If Jon Jones is
there, why can't Islam be there?

02:15:35.204 --> 02:15:38.784
If Demetrious Johnson is
there with 11 title defenses,

02:15:39.464 --> 02:15:42.864
why can't Islam be there? It's
a good question. Why not?

02:15:44.004 --> 02:15:47.784
I'd like him to break all
those records, to overtake

02:15:47.824 --> 02:15:51.544
Jon Jones, Demetrious
Johnson, and St-Pierre, no

02:15:51.644 --> 02:15:55.334
matter how much respect I have
for them. Islam has a chance,

02:15:55.964 --> 02:15:58.104
but time is starting to run out a little.

02:15:58.924 --> 02:16:02.764
This worries me because, for
example, Jones fought at light

02:16:02.844 --> 02:16:06.664
heavyweight and now at heavyweight.
Time is a little kinder

02:16:06.684 --> 02:16:10.424
to those divisions. Even
at 38, you can still

02:16:10.524 --> 02:16:14.324
compete at the heavier
weights. You don't need the

02:16:14.424 --> 02:16:18.384
same reaction speed and all
that. But when you compete in

02:16:18.424 --> 02:16:26.364
lighter weight classes, 155, 170, age
really catches up. I think active fighters,

02:16:26.744 --> 02:16:30.884
retired fighters, wrestlers, athletes,

02:16:31.944 --> 02:16:35.444
especially those who competed
in these weight classes,

02:16:35.724 --> 02:16:38.344
will understand what I'm talking about.

02:16:39.464 --> 02:16:42.964
When you're 35 and when
you're 32 and when you're

02:16:43.144 --> 02:16:45.484
38, you're a totally different athlete.

02:16:46.684 --> 02:16:50.604
- From where you are now, you can see
the entire fight business, and you

02:16:50.644 --> 02:16:53.884
know Dana White very well.
I'd say he's one of those

02:16:53.984 --> 02:17:00.244
rare geniuses in sports
business, in business, period.

02:17:01.184 --> 02:17:04.924
I don't think the UFC would exist
without Dana White. There are

02:17:04.944 --> 02:17:08.484
important people there, of course, but
he's really at the center of it all.

02:17:09.564 --> 02:17:13.184
What can you say about
him? What qualities,

02:17:14.164 --> 02:17:18.584
what traits have made him such
a successful businessman?

02:17:19.484 --> 02:17:20.444
- First of all, I think

02:17:22.724 --> 02:17:26.684
if you look at it, I also kind of...
Well, not kind of, I understand

02:17:26.724 --> 02:17:27.944
his position a little.

02:17:29.344 --> 02:17:32.844
I also ran my own league for a
couple of years. I know what the

02:17:32.884 --> 02:17:36.834
relationship between a
promoter, well, a league owner

02:17:37.004 --> 02:17:42.964
and the fighters is like, not just as
a fighter dealing with a league owner.

02:17:43.964 --> 02:17:46.004
I've been on both sides of it.

02:17:48.084 --> 02:17:51.844
He's very good at separating business
from personal relationships. For

02:17:51.944 --> 02:17:58.934
him, the two never overlap. He draws a
clear line between them. In business,

02:17:59.594 --> 02:18:04.644
he's extremely cold-blooded.
He's made decisions,

02:18:07.124 --> 02:18:11.024
some major decisions,
regardless of whether people

02:18:11.034 --> 02:18:14.744
like them or not. And those
decisions, they were best for the

02:18:14.784 --> 02:18:20.824
league and for the UFC. He always had
that mentality, you know, like in those

02:18:21.844 --> 02:18:25.684
mafia movies where family
comes first, right?

02:18:25.724 --> 02:18:27.924
Well, for him, the UFC always came first.

02:18:29.534 --> 02:18:33.464
That's the only way he was able to push
their brand forward. Of course, a lot

02:18:33.504 --> 02:18:38.084
of people contributed to that success.
There were a lot of them. You have to

02:18:39.024 --> 02:18:44.604
give credit to Bruce Buffer, to Joe Rogan,
to the commentators who were there.

02:18:45.464 --> 02:18:48.204
There were a lot of things
that made the UFC unique, but,

02:18:49.124 --> 02:18:53.104
but the cherry on top was always
Dana. Dana did an incredible job

02:18:53.144 --> 02:18:58.324
promoting the UFC, and he still does. No
matter what, the UFC is always there.

02:18:59.564 --> 02:19:04.824
You know? No matter how, no matter how
unsuccessful a particular event might be,

02:19:05.704 --> 02:19:09.524
the UFC is still the UFC. When
they go into a new market

02:19:09.584 --> 02:19:13.444
outside the United States, even if
a show doesn't go so well, people

02:19:13.484 --> 02:19:16.694
will still come back for the next
event simply because it's the UFC.

02:19:17.324 --> 02:19:21.124
Over the last 30 plus years, they've
established themselves as the

02:19:21.164 --> 02:19:24.834
premier MMA league in the world,
and they put on the best fights.

02:19:25.544 --> 02:19:29.524
So in that respect, I think a huge
part of the credit goes to Dana. The

02:19:29.564 --> 02:19:32.824
fact that the UFC has come this
far is a major achievement.

02:19:33.344 --> 02:19:35.564
- Building a league like that
is incredibly difficult

02:19:36.036 --> 02:19:39.436
- Yeah. A lot of people have money and a
lot of people have tried. Many have come

02:19:39.496 --> 02:19:43.456
in. But building what the UFC
has built is extremely hard. And

02:19:43.496 --> 02:19:45.276
what they've achieved now with Paramount,

02:19:46.226 --> 02:19:50.106
that's something unique. It's a unique
achievement. In just 30 years, they've

02:19:50.136 --> 02:19:55.296
grown to a level that some entire sports
needed 100 or 150 years to reach.

02:19:56.716 --> 02:20:00.476
And you have to give credit where it's due, and
that credit goes to one person, Dana White.

02:20:00.856 --> 02:20:06.296
So I think that his genius as a
promoter, the way he promotes

02:20:06.306 --> 02:20:11.726
and pushes fights, and pushes his
league as a whole, that is just

02:20:11.776 --> 02:20:16.216
remarkable. In my opinion, that's something
a lot of people could learn from him.

02:20:17.016 --> 02:20:19.956
- You mentioned Joe Rogan. You
know, he's a friend of mine.

02:20:21.556 --> 02:20:25.436
I love listening to his podcast, but
I've never actually heard the two of you

02:20:25.496 --> 02:20:29.316
have a conversation together. Do you think that's
something that could happen in the future?

02:20:29.816 --> 02:20:30.356
- Yes, of course.

02:20:30.596 --> 02:20:33.736
- I know you have a great deal of respect for
him, and he has a great deal of respect for

02:20:33.776 --> 02:20:36.716
you. I can't wait to hear
the two of you talk.

02:20:37.296 --> 02:20:40.796
- Same here. If we're talking about the
people most responsible for promoting the

02:20:40.856 --> 02:20:44.816
UFC, I'd put Dana first. But
second place, without question,

02:20:44.876 --> 02:20:48.476
goes to Joe Rogan. It's honestly
hard to imagine the UFC without him.

02:20:48.956 --> 02:20:50.636
It'll be hard when he's
no longer part of it.

02:20:52.416 --> 02:20:56.376
And what he's achieved in his own field,
what he's achieved in podcasting,

02:20:57.696 --> 02:21:01.536
he's the best. And what he's
built, the way he commentates,

02:21:01.856 --> 02:21:05.236
the way he communicates, his
knowledge, his charisma,

02:21:06.616 --> 02:21:10.496
the way he carries a conversation, I think
a lot of people could learn from him.

02:21:11.336 --> 02:21:15.326
And as for me going on his podcast, I
think it's inevitable. God willing, if

02:21:15.336 --> 02:21:19.276
we're given the time and the health,
we'll have a lot to talk about. There

02:21:19.296 --> 02:21:23.266
are so many different topics we could
discuss together, so a conversation with

02:21:23.296 --> 02:21:26.816
Joe Rogan would be very interesting.
I'd definitely like to talk to him.

02:21:27.476 --> 02:21:31.116
- I agree. But it's interesting, right
now you and I are speaking Russian.

02:21:31.576 --> 02:21:32.636
Language is a tricky thing.

02:21:32.976 --> 02:21:36.016
- Yeah, exactly. With him
you need to come prepared.

02:21:36.156 --> 02:21:40.016
Because if you show up unprepared,
he will just eat you alive.

02:21:40.796 --> 02:21:43.815
You have to be ready. It's
a mental battle, too.

02:21:43.936 --> 02:21:48.046
- Yeah. And by the way, you know,
you've mentioned a few times that

02:21:49.276 --> 02:21:53.516
you'd like to improve your Arabic.
Language is such an interesting thing.

02:21:54.456 --> 02:21:56.276
It opens doors to understanding.

02:21:57.136 --> 02:22:00.316
It allows you to understand culture
and history on a much deeper level.

02:22:01.016 --> 02:22:04.696
- Absolutely. Without the language,
it's very difficult. I'm starting to

02:22:04.836 --> 02:22:09.396
study Arabic now, but I've been reading
the Quran since I was a child. I know the

02:22:10.376 --> 02:22:14.696
the alphabet very well, the
pronunciation rules. I can read it well.

02:22:15.796 --> 02:22:19.216
But the Quran and Arabic as a
spoken language are different.

02:22:20.276 --> 02:22:23.736
I wouldn't say they're drastically
different, but they are different.

02:22:24.716 --> 02:22:28.596
If you want to speak it conversationally,
you need to study grammar and learn

02:22:28.656 --> 02:22:29.696
it in a different way.

02:22:31.276 --> 02:22:37.556
- I'd also like to ask you about Ali, Ali
Abdelaziz. He's such a central figure. He

02:22:37.616 --> 02:22:39.036
represents so many champions.

02:22:40.656 --> 02:22:43.996
What do you respect about him? What
do you think makes him a genius?

02:22:44.636 --> 02:22:46.256
Because he's such a unique person.

02:22:47.396 --> 02:22:53.736
- Well, I met Ali in 2014. We've
been working together ever since.

02:22:54.656 --> 02:22:59.856
Over the years, a lot of fighters came
to him through me, a lot of guys.

02:23:01.696 --> 02:23:09.836
And in all this time, in the 12 years
we've known each other, I don't--

02:23:11.116 --> 02:23:16.396
I can barely remember any fighters
leaving him, except maybe a couple.

02:23:17.576 --> 02:23:21.356
But hundreds of fighters have worked with
him, hundreds of fighters, and so many

02:23:21.396 --> 02:23:25.286
guys still keep in touch with him, and
he has very good relationships with

02:23:25.336 --> 02:23:31.176
them. I'd also like to note that Ali,
you know, he has one unique quality.

02:23:32.576 --> 02:23:36.216
People either really
love him or really hate

02:23:36.296 --> 02:23:40.176
him. You can't just be neutral about
him, you know, like, "Yeah, I know

02:23:40.256 --> 02:23:41.276
Ali. He's all right."

02:23:42.116 --> 02:23:45.696
No one will say about him, "Well, you
know, I know him. He's a good guy."

02:23:47.096 --> 02:23:50.296
You'll have strong feelings
for him. Either you hate him

02:23:50.996 --> 02:23:55.516
or you really love him. And I am in

02:23:56.616 --> 02:24:02.536
in that category of people who really...
How do I put it? Who are very fond of him.

02:24:02.576 --> 02:24:06.286
And I have a good relationship with
him. I know his family. I know his

02:24:06.316 --> 02:24:10.276
children. His kids grew up
before my eyes. When I first met

02:24:10.296 --> 02:24:12.956
him, he only had two or three
children. Now he has more.

02:24:15.556 --> 02:24:18.176
This is the kind of person he is.

02:24:19.736 --> 02:24:23.226
You need to get to know him
better to really appreciate him,

02:24:23.596 --> 02:24:26.736
and he will stand by
you until the very end.

02:24:27.856 --> 02:24:31.736
And yeah, of course,
there are people who have

02:24:31.756 --> 02:24:35.576
who have mixed feelings about him.
But that's my point. It's hard

02:24:35.656 --> 02:24:37.776
to feel indifferent about Ali.

02:24:37.906 --> 02:24:41.856
"I know Ali. He's a very good
guy." No, it's different with him.

02:24:42.096 --> 02:24:45.726
People will either tell you, "I know
Ali and I absolutely love him," or,

02:24:46.076 --> 02:24:47.936
"I know Ali and I can't stand him."

02:24:48.616 --> 02:24:51.265
- So, it's fair to say that most people who-

02:24:51.716 --> 02:24:52.136
- Yes, yes

02:24:52.156 --> 02:24:56.416
- ... who really know him and work with
him tend to stay with him for life?

02:24:56.956 --> 02:24:57.216
- Yes.

02:24:57.376 --> 02:25:00.956
- And respect him. That's important.
And his judo isn't bad either.

02:25:02.696 --> 02:25:02.895
- It was.

02:25:02.916 --> 02:25:03.356
- He showed me.

02:25:03.436 --> 02:25:04.796
- It was. It was, it was.

02:25:04.836 --> 02:25:08.246
- Oh, right, you were just
wrestling yesterday. All

02:25:08.296 --> 02:25:11.736
right. Let's talk about football
now. I want to ask you something,

02:25:12.256 --> 02:25:16.136
and I need to be careful here because
after yesterday, I realize just how

02:25:16.196 --> 02:25:17.455
much you know about the game.

02:25:18.616 --> 02:25:22.396
I can't even pretend I know what I'm
talking about anymore. I need to tread

02:25:22.516 --> 02:25:23.036
carefully.

02:25:23.776 --> 02:25:25.856
- Yes. If we're gonna go there-

02:25:26.016 --> 02:25:28.476
... don't make any mistakes.
I hope you did your research.

02:25:29.416 --> 02:25:29.996
- I'm shaking now.

02:25:30.236 --> 02:25:30.716
- You prepared?

02:25:30.796 --> 02:25:32.796
- Wait. We could talk about

02:25:34.056 --> 02:25:37.976
a lot of interesting teams. You talked about
the moment when you fell in love with football.

02:25:38.880 --> 02:25:42.920
That was the Champions
League final in 1998.

02:25:43.120 --> 02:25:47.360
It was Real Madrid against Juventus.
It was the moment when you,

02:25:48.620 --> 02:25:50.260
you realized that Real
Madrid was your team,

02:25:51.210 --> 02:25:54.420
the club you've continued to
love and support ever since.

02:25:54.460 --> 02:25:58.100
Tell me about that very first moment when
you watched the game with your father.

02:26:00.260 --> 02:26:04.180
What do you remember? You know,
this is such an important sport for

02:26:04.240 --> 02:26:08.300
you, for millions, hundreds of
millions, billions of people.

02:26:08.810 --> 02:26:09.410
- Billions, yes.

02:26:09.840 --> 02:26:12.430
- What do you remember most about that
game, and what did you fall in love with?

02:26:12.860 --> 02:26:16.620
- Well, first of all, my father was
a huge football fan. He was a good

02:26:16.660 --> 02:26:20.460
player himself. When we lived in the
village, they played almost every

02:26:20.500 --> 02:26:24.230
day. Football has always been very
popular where I'm from. The kids,

02:26:24.780 --> 02:26:28.580
the young people, everyone plays.
In fact, we still have this

02:26:28.600 --> 02:26:34.750
tradition in the village. As long as
I can remember, it was a tradition

02:26:34.800 --> 02:26:38.760
to... for each street to
put together its own team

02:26:38.820 --> 02:26:42.040
for a tournament with, eh, group
stages, then the quarterfinals,

02:26:42.340 --> 02:26:47.610
semifinals, and the final. So it became
a real tradition, and everyone would.

02:26:48.500 --> 02:26:51.000
Everyone there follows football,
the kids, the young people.

02:26:52.480 --> 02:26:54.700
And my father played, too.

02:26:55.800 --> 02:26:58.410
Whenever there was a big match
on, I'd always sit with him,

02:26:59.360 --> 02:27:02.180
in front of the television and watch.

02:27:03.740 --> 02:27:07.620
The first football memory I have that's
really clear and conscious goes back

02:27:07.660 --> 02:27:11.490
to 1996. I couldn't tell
you where we were watching

02:27:11.520 --> 02:27:14.610
it. I don't remember, but I
remember sitting next to my

02:27:14.640 --> 02:27:18.530
father. It was the European Championship
semifinal, Germany and England.

02:27:19.860 --> 02:27:24.000
England lost on penalties at home,
and Germany advanced to the final.

02:27:25.280 --> 02:27:27.220
A few days later, we
watched the final as well.

02:27:29.440 --> 02:27:33.340
We were at someone's place. We didn't have a TV at
the time, or maybe our channel wasn't showing the

02:27:33.400 --> 02:27:37.230
match. I don't remember. We
watched it at somebody's place. It

02:27:37.280 --> 02:27:40.940
was Germany against the Czech
Republic. Germany won that final.

02:27:42.040 --> 02:27:45.880
I remember Oliver Bierhoff coming off the
bench and scoring. That whole period is

02:27:45.960 --> 02:27:47.859
still very vivid in my memory.

02:27:48.870 --> 02:27:52.700
After that, I kept watching football
whenever I could. And I remember

02:27:52.740 --> 02:27:56.760
the 1998 Champions League final.
I watched it with my father.

02:27:57.880 --> 02:28:01.680
Again, I don't remember where we
watched it, but it was the final, 1 to

02:28:01.780 --> 02:28:05.720
0. Real Madrid won. I can still remember
the players who were on the pitch.

02:28:06.280 --> 02:28:09.840
Uh, I was obsessed with football myself.

02:28:10.180 --> 02:28:14.120
I'd spend hours, days outside
playing with a ball, sometimes on my

02:28:14.200 --> 02:28:20.380
own, sometimes with a group. There
was a period when, actually,

02:28:21.360 --> 02:28:25.060
in the settlement where
I lived, in the village,

02:28:26.180 --> 02:28:28.640
I was pretty much the only
kid who had a football.

02:28:29.560 --> 02:28:31.820
So the older guys would come over.

02:28:32.800 --> 02:28:36.670
I was maybe nine or ten, and these were boys
who were fifteen or sixteen. They'd say,

02:28:36.890 --> 02:28:38.600
"Habib, bring the ball out."

02:28:38.940 --> 02:28:42.480
But I'd give them a condition for bringing
the ball out. When you're ten years old,

02:28:43.120 --> 02:28:47.020
nobody is putting you on a team with
fifteen-year-olds, you know. The age gap is

02:28:47.060 --> 02:28:49.870
just too big. So I'd say, "If I
bring the ball, I'm playing."

02:28:50.680 --> 02:28:54.480
They go, "All right, bring it out," and
I'd get to play. And the kids my own age

02:28:54.520 --> 02:28:58.180
didn't make the team. You know how it
is when there are a lot of kids around.

02:28:59.120 --> 02:29:00.920
Of course, the older
boys would take charge.

02:29:02.400 --> 02:29:06.120
They decide who's playing. Normally, when
you're ten and they're fifteen, there was no

02:29:06.220 --> 02:29:09.520
chance you'd get picked. But I was
very lucky. I had the ball, so

02:29:10.400 --> 02:29:14.080
I always made the team and got to play with
them. That's where it all started, really, you

02:29:14.120 --> 02:29:17.180
know. That love for football
started in my childhood.

02:29:18.220 --> 02:29:22.120
My father and I always watched the
big matches together. I even remember

02:29:22.140 --> 02:29:25.920
the 1998. I remember being
somewhere and literally

02:29:25.960 --> 02:29:29.980
running home, so I wouldn't miss the opening
ceremony of the tournament in France.

02:29:31.960 --> 02:29:34.170
I was just running. I ran to my
grandfather's house and said,

02:29:35.700 --> 02:29:39.630
"Grandpa, put on channel one." I think
it was channel one that was showing the

02:29:39.680 --> 02:29:41.980
opening. Ronaldo was
playing in that tournament.

02:29:44.400 --> 02:29:47.679
So I sat there watching, and my
grandfather looked at me. I was ten.

02:29:49.220 --> 02:29:51.280
He looked at me and said,
"Why are you interested?"

02:29:52.200 --> 02:29:56.160
I said, "I want to watch." I sat
and watched. The Brazilians won

02:29:56.239 --> 02:29:59.679
both matches back then, I think. They
won against Morocco and lost to the

02:29:59.780 --> 02:30:00.560
Norwegians.

02:30:00.799 --> 02:30:03.040
- The opening match was Brazil
against Scotland, two-one, right?

02:30:03.520 --> 02:30:07.140
- Yeah, two to one. So
basically, from childhood, I

02:30:07.200 --> 02:30:08.810
already had that love for football,

02:30:09.640 --> 02:30:13.610
and then it just grew. I started watching
the Champions League, the Euros, the World

02:30:13.720 --> 02:30:17.470
Cup, and later the domestic
leagues, mostly the Premier League.

02:30:20.240 --> 02:30:22.660
So that's where this passion comes from.

02:30:22.740 --> 02:30:26.380
- And what about national teams?
Brazil was your team growing up,

02:30:26.440 --> 02:30:29.620
right? That legendary Brazil team.

02:30:30.520 --> 02:30:34.380
- I like Brazil, yes. I also like the
Netherlands. Those national teams,

02:30:34.680 --> 02:30:38.160
Brazil and the Netherlands, those were
the ones that really appealed to me.

02:30:39.060 --> 02:30:42.460
- And what about clubs? What were
the main teams that you supported?

02:30:43.120 --> 02:30:46.840
- It varied. I never had one national
team that I'd say was my favorite team.

02:30:47.300 --> 02:30:47.880
- Ah, you didn't?

02:30:48.010 --> 02:30:51.960
- I didn't. Today, I don't feel the same
way about the Netherlands. But back

02:30:52.020 --> 02:30:55.960
then, I really liked that
Dutch team a lot. Van der Sar,

02:30:56.220 --> 02:31:02.320
Reiziger, van Bronckhorst, Overmars,
Davids, Seedorf, van Nistelrooy, Makaay.

02:31:04.200 --> 02:31:06.020
That old lineup. Kluivert.

02:31:06.980 --> 02:31:10.600
Now it's not the same. That era is gone.
They just don't have players like that

02:31:10.660 --> 02:31:14.420
anymore. In that sense, there's been a
bit of a drop-off for the national team.

02:31:15.000 --> 02:31:18.920
The same with Brazil. Back then, they were
incredibly strong, but today it's not

02:31:18.940 --> 02:31:20.140
at that same level anymore.

02:31:21.160 --> 02:31:25.060
But when it comes to the club, the club I
follow, that's Real Madrid. That's the one.

02:31:25.520 --> 02:31:29.420
But if we're talking about leagues
overall, then because of the competition

02:31:29.440 --> 02:31:32.920
and the way the league is played,
I'd say the English Premier League.

02:31:34.300 --> 02:31:37.920
- And in the Premier League,
do you follow Man U, City?

02:31:38.020 --> 02:31:41.960
- Not in a way where I have one team,
no. I like them. They're both top

02:31:42.020 --> 02:31:45.700
clubs. They're big clubs. I watch
them. I follow them. It just

02:31:45.740 --> 02:31:49.376
depends. But it's not like I have
one favorite. I do like City,

02:31:49.556 --> 02:31:53.306
Manchester City. In recent years, they've
been winning almost everything. They've

02:31:53.336 --> 02:31:54.636
dominated for the last ten years.

02:31:56.116 --> 02:32:00.076
But if we look more closely
at Manchester City, there's

02:32:00.116 --> 02:32:02.636
been a bit of a dip over
the last two seasons.

02:32:05.116 --> 02:32:09.076
And then you also have the rise of Arsenal
in the last couple of seasons, a very

02:32:09.116 --> 02:32:12.976
well-structured team under Arteta.
They're really on the rise this season.

02:32:13.036 --> 02:32:16.476
They're leading the league, doing well
in the Champions League. They're leading

02:32:16.516 --> 02:32:19.876
domestically as well, so I
don't know how far they'll go,

02:32:20.456 --> 02:32:22.396
but they're aiming for
the treble this season.

02:32:23.936 --> 02:32:28.856
- I wanna ask because we lived through
such a moment in sports history-

02:32:29.116 --> 02:32:33.506
... that we might never see anything
like it again. I'm talking about this,

02:32:35.076 --> 02:32:37.416
this rivalry between Messi
and Cristiano Ronaldo.

02:32:38.596 --> 02:32:42.566
Of course, since you love
Real Madrid, you know, you

02:32:42.656 --> 02:32:46.616
naturally love Cristiano Ronaldo,
but what can you say about it? A

02:32:46.636 --> 02:32:50.816
lot of people call him the absolute
best, and for many, Messi is number one.

02:32:51.856 --> 02:32:54.896
There's also a very good book about
their rivalry. I recommend it.

02:32:56.716 --> 02:33:00.116
It basically tells the story of
modern football through their

02:33:00.136 --> 02:33:06.396
rivalry, Messi versus Ronaldo,
one rivalry, almost 20-year era.

02:33:06.516 --> 02:33:10.476
Uh, two GOATs in an era that remade
the world's game. So it's been

02:33:10.656 --> 02:33:13.996
20 years. So what can you say?
Why do you consider Cristiano

02:33:14.036 --> 02:33:18.956
Ronaldo a great footballer? What do you
like about him? Let's start with Ronaldo.

02:33:21.256 --> 02:33:24.996
- Well, he's a natural
born athlete. He is...

02:33:26.476 --> 02:33:30.096
God gave him the athleticism,
and he managed to maintain it.

02:33:30.416 --> 02:33:32.316
There are many who have lost it.

02:33:32.936 --> 02:33:37.476
There are many players who have talent,
physical ability, but they are lazy.

02:33:38.336 --> 02:33:43.476
But he has it all, athleticism,
talent, hard work, everything,

02:33:44.536 --> 02:33:48.435
and then there's this. He
played in Manchester. He moved.

02:33:49.896 --> 02:33:53.396
He shone in Portugal at Sporting,
then moved to Manchester.

02:33:54.036 --> 02:33:57.716
And it's not easy to make your
mark at Manchester. From 2003 to

02:33:57.916 --> 02:34:01.756
2008, he became a star there,
and what he achieved in 2008,

02:34:02.456 --> 02:34:06.416
just look at how many individual
trophies he won and how much he won with

02:34:06.436 --> 02:34:10.336
his team. You can even look it up.
Just search what Cristiano Ronaldo

02:34:10.376 --> 02:34:14.396
achieved in 2008. It's
simply unbelievable.

02:34:15.676 --> 02:34:19.576
And then he moved from Manchester.
He transferred to an even more

02:34:19.656 --> 02:34:23.776
difficult club, Real Madrid,
nine years at Real Madrid.

02:34:24.056 --> 02:34:27.736
Five years at Manchester United, then
nine years at Real Madrid. That's

02:34:27.816 --> 02:34:33.636
about 15 years. You're under constant
pressure at the two biggest clubs in the

02:34:33.676 --> 02:34:34.786
history of football,

02:34:35.756 --> 02:34:40.136
and at both clubs, at both clubs, he
became the best. That's very difficult.

02:34:40.746 --> 02:34:44.636
- Yeah, look here. Three Premier League titles
came during his time at Manchester United.

02:34:45.196 --> 02:34:48.916
- Look at 2008 specifically, what he
accomplished then. He won the Premier

02:34:48.996 --> 02:34:52.876
League. He won the UEFA Champions
League. Individually, he won

02:34:52.936 --> 02:34:56.756
the Golden Boot, top score in the
Champions League, the Premier

02:34:56.856 --> 02:35:02.186
League, every major trophy. But the point
is that at Manchester, he spent five

02:35:02.236 --> 02:35:06.036
years performing at the
highest level under enormous

02:35:06.056 --> 02:35:09.816
pressure. Then he spent
nine years at Real Madrid.

02:35:09.896 --> 02:35:13.676
After that, he moved to Juventus, but only
for a short time, and this is the part

02:35:13.716 --> 02:35:17.436
people tend to forget. He spent
three seasons at Juventus, and he

02:35:17.516 --> 02:35:22.476
also won both the Italian championship and
the Super Cup there and scored 100 goals.

02:35:23.616 --> 02:35:26.096
And just the fact that for 20 years,

02:35:27.096 --> 02:35:31.256
let's say 22 or 23 years, he's
been at the highest level.

02:35:32.326 --> 02:35:36.296
Look at how many goals he scored, and
now he's closing in on 1,000. Will Messi

02:35:36.356 --> 02:35:40.276
ever reach that number is the big
question. Next season, he scores 40

02:35:40.316 --> 02:35:43.916
goals in a season, and he's less
than 40 goals away from the

02:35:44.496 --> 02:35:48.236
1,000-goal mark. That's how
unique he is in that regard. But

02:35:49.336 --> 02:35:52.966
just because I'm a Real Madrid
fan doesn't mean I downplay what

02:35:53.056 --> 02:35:56.656
Messi has achieved. It's impossible
to downplay what Messi's done.

02:35:57.136 --> 02:36:01.036
You have to give him the credit he deserves,
but I wouldn't want to put one of them above

02:36:01.046 --> 02:36:03.945
the other. If I'm being
honest, both of them,

02:36:04.975 --> 02:36:08.276
watching each other, competing with
each other, they stayed there.

02:36:09.496 --> 02:36:13.466
Would Messi be Messi without Ronaldo? Would
Ronaldo be Ronaldo without Messi? Those are big

02:36:13.516 --> 02:36:17.226
questions. I'm convinced they watched
each other closely. They always

02:36:17.296 --> 02:36:18.476
have, and they still do now.

02:36:19.296 --> 02:36:22.946
For almost 20 years, they've been
watching each other, pushing each other,

02:36:23.216 --> 02:36:26.976
and constantly improving. They were
already the best, but that drove them even

02:36:27.016 --> 02:36:32.736
further. By competing against one another,
they pulled so far ahead of the rest.

02:36:33.016 --> 02:36:36.976
Whether you're talking about achievements,
trophies, and all that, and in that

02:36:37.056 --> 02:36:40.876
sense, their rivalry truly
elevated the entire sport. More

02:36:40.916 --> 02:36:44.646
people started following football
because of them, watching the games.

02:36:45.486 --> 02:36:48.636
So I believe both of them
deserve tremendous respect.

02:36:49.516 --> 02:36:53.496
- Yeah. I'd say I completely
agree with you that if Messi

02:36:54.536 --> 02:36:57.836
hadn't had Ronaldo and
Ronaldo hadn't had Messi-

02:36:58.136 --> 02:36:59.436
- Yeah, would they have gone that far?

02:36:59.696 --> 02:37:00.336
- I don't think so.

02:37:00.376 --> 02:37:01.196
- It's an open question.

02:37:01.416 --> 02:37:02.376
- I don't think so.

02:37:02.756 --> 02:37:06.316
But for me, there is nobody like Messi, his
pure love for the game for its own sake.

02:37:06.676 --> 02:37:08.356
- You're a Barca guy?

02:37:08.365 --> 02:37:11.356
Oh, really? Yeah, I can tell from the way
you're talking about it, the way you...

02:37:12.636 --> 02:37:13.416
- You got me.

02:37:13.476 --> 02:37:15.396
- Yeah, yeah, yeah. Nothing
against it, but...

02:37:16.036 --> 02:37:19.596
- Yes, sure. Well, I mean, Iniesta, Xavi,

02:37:19.696 --> 02:37:23.536
Busquets, Iniesta, Puyol,
that was a legendary team.

02:37:23.636 --> 02:37:32.556
- Yes. No, but look, you can compare them
like this. Iniesta, Xavi, Busquets, Messi,

02:37:32.956 --> 02:37:34.496
Ronaldinho, Piqué.

02:37:35.116 --> 02:37:35.696
- Ronaldinho, yes.

02:37:35.736 --> 02:37:39.296
- Ronaldinho, he was a bit
earlier. That Barcelona team

02:37:39.696 --> 02:37:43.136
from roughly 2008 to 2012,
that four-year period,

02:37:43.656 --> 02:37:49.676
only one team in history can compare with
them, Real Madrid from 2014 to 2018.

02:37:50.488 --> 02:37:53.777
They won in 2014, in 2016, 2017, and

02:37:53.968 --> 02:37:57.468
2018. The only interruption was
Barcelona's team with Neymar.

02:37:57.848 --> 02:37:58.788
- With Neymar, yes.

02:37:59.008 --> 02:38:02.428
- With Suarez and Messi, that was
2015. Well, let's leave that aside.

02:38:03.028 --> 02:38:07.028
But, you know, with Modric, Kroos,
Casemiro, Ronaldo, Benzema,

02:38:08.348 --> 02:38:15.768
Marcelo, Carvajal, Casillas, and
this team compared to Victor Valdes,

02:38:16.728 --> 02:38:21.748
Pique, Puyol, Dani Alves, Jordi
Alba, Busquets, Xavi, Iniesta,

02:38:22.728 --> 02:38:26.068
Villa, and Messi, for me, those
are the two greatest teams.

02:38:26.588 --> 02:38:31.888
Different generations, different
squads. For me, they truly represent a

02:38:33.428 --> 02:38:38.288
represent true dream teams at football,
at club level. That's the comparison.

02:38:39.288 --> 02:38:48.968
But if we look at the national team
level you could pick France in 1998.

02:38:49.068 --> 02:38:57.108
That 1998 France team is very close to
a perfect team, and also 2002 Brazil.

02:38:58.128 --> 02:39:00.108
In 2002, they were extremely strong.

02:39:00.248 --> 02:39:01.488
- Yes, you had Ronaldo.

02:39:01.728 --> 02:39:07.048
- You had Ronaldo, Rivaldo,
Ronaldinho, Roberto Carlos, Cafu.

02:39:07.928 --> 02:39:14.308
You had Emerson. You had Kleberson, I
think Roque Junior, Lucio, Edmilson.

02:39:14.628 --> 02:39:18.508
And beyond those superstar names,
there were also very strong

02:39:18.528 --> 02:39:21.838
players who kind of stayed in
the shadow of those stars.

02:39:22.168 --> 02:39:27.228
Stayed in their shadow. So
Brazil 2002, France 1998,

02:39:28.788 --> 02:39:35.528
and then there was later Spain from
Euro 2008, the 2010 World Cup, and Euro

02:39:35.628 --> 02:39:40.928
2012. That period, too, Spain. The Spanish
national team was close to perfect.

02:39:41.708 --> 02:39:45.578
- I wanted to go back to France.
You mentioned Zidane. I really,

02:39:45.808 --> 02:39:47.448
really respect and admire him.

02:39:48.708 --> 02:39:52.358
There was this moment psychologically
very interesting, almost

02:39:52.388 --> 02:39:55.098
Shakespearean moment in his
career when it ended with,

02:39:55.948 --> 02:39:58.138
ended with that... How
do you even call it?

02:39:58.908 --> 02:40:01.468
- A headbutt. A headbutt to the chest.

02:40:01.528 --> 02:40:05.748
- What's interesting is, I don't know,
looking at your fight with Conor-

02:40:06.008 --> 02:40:07.308
When emotion builds up,

02:40:09.528 --> 02:40:14.148
for most of it you're focused on the game,
but in the end you let that emotion out.

02:40:14.317 --> 02:40:16.048
What do you think about that headbutt?

02:40:17.228 --> 02:40:18.348
- I wouldn't want to judge him,

02:40:19.788 --> 02:40:23.508
wouldn't want to be some kind
of judge of what he did, no.

02:40:24.398 --> 02:40:27.648
Well, he did what he did. We don't
know what was really going on there.

02:40:29.168 --> 02:40:33.108
But as a player, of course, he was
unique. He won the Euros, the World

02:40:33.188 --> 02:40:36.848
Cup, reached finals, won the Champions
League, the Spanish and the Italian

02:40:36.908 --> 02:40:39.248
championships, and cups, too.

02:40:41.148 --> 02:40:45.008
It's just unique, and the way he played,
I just always remembered him for

02:40:45.048 --> 02:40:48.828
the way sweat was just pouring off
him, the way he ran, how he gave

02:40:48.908 --> 02:40:52.488
everything in every single match.
He had incredible stamina,

02:40:52.568 --> 02:40:56.407
incredible endurance. I think a lot
of people listening to this podcast

02:40:56.888 --> 02:40:58.548
may not have watched Zidane playing live.

02:40:59.948 --> 02:41:02.828
When I say live, I mean
watching him play in real time.

02:41:03.508 --> 02:41:06.368
I personally didn't see his
games live. I was younger then.

02:41:07.348 --> 02:41:11.258
But I did watch many of his matches
later on. I remember the goals he

02:41:11.348 --> 02:41:14.758
scored in World Cup
finals in 1998 and 2006,

02:41:15.148 --> 02:41:18.088
especially the headers,
and even that penalty,

02:41:19.048 --> 02:41:22.888
the Champions League final, European
championships, the way he played, the way he

02:41:22.948 --> 02:41:26.468
handled the ball, the way the ball was,

02:41:27.368 --> 02:41:31.648
the way the ball was glued to his feet,
his movements, and then his charisma.

02:41:32.748 --> 02:41:36.428
It's rare to find all of that in one
player. You don't see that combination

02:41:36.488 --> 02:41:40.068
often. There are many very good players,
but sometimes they're just not,

02:41:42.488 --> 02:41:44.968
not that interesting to
watch. You know what I mean?

02:41:46.248 --> 02:41:49.988
I don't want to give specific examples
because someone might take it the wrong way,

02:41:50.108 --> 02:41:56.648
so I won't name anyone. But there are
players who are phenomenal in terms of

02:41:57.208 --> 02:41:59.368
in terms of their physical attributes:

02:42:00.548 --> 02:42:04.388
fast, explosive, effective. But they
don't have charisma, that energy

02:42:04.448 --> 02:42:08.668
that makes people want to watch them,
and I don't think that's their fault.

02:42:10.328 --> 02:42:14.308
But with Zidane, everything came together.
You always wanted to watch him play.

02:42:15.268 --> 02:42:19.208
You always want to watch Ronaldo play. You
always want to watch Beckham play. You want to

02:42:19.228 --> 02:42:22.968
watch Edgar Davids running around the
field in those glasses, his hair bouncing

02:42:23.008 --> 02:42:27.248
behind him, right? You still wanted to
watch the way Barthez played in goal.

02:42:29.148 --> 02:42:32.728
No matter how many bizarre goals he
conceded, you still wanted to watch him

02:42:32.768 --> 02:42:36.988
play. Or Oliver Kahn, the way he stood
in goal and directed the whole team,

02:42:38.118 --> 02:42:42.048
the way he would rush out to the ball,
the way strikers were afraid of him.

02:42:43.188 --> 02:42:46.788
I mean, so many strikers
have admitted that they were

02:42:46.868 --> 02:42:51.128
afraid if they found themselves one-on-one
with Oliver Kahn in the penalty area.

02:42:52.148 --> 02:42:55.888
You always wanted to watch Roberto
Carlos take a free kick or storm

02:42:55.948 --> 02:42:57.808
down the left flank.

02:42:57.988 --> 02:43:01.428
You always wanted to see Cafu or Michel
Salgado make an overlapping run.

02:43:02.448 --> 02:43:06.408
The way Vieira stood there as the
defensive midfielder or somebody like

02:43:06.468 --> 02:43:10.068
Maldini or like Rio Ferdinand
or somebody like that,

02:43:10.628 --> 02:43:14.148
you wanted to watch Casillas
in goal. So, you know, for

02:43:14.208 --> 02:43:17.628
me, and maybe for our generation,
those players had something special.

02:43:18.088 --> 02:43:22.048
- Can you try to explain what charisma is?
Like you said, there are good players,

02:43:22.148 --> 02:43:23.068
and then there is Zidane.

02:43:23.368 --> 02:43:29.568
- It's very difficult to explain. You either
have it or you don't. Personality, right?

02:43:29.688 --> 02:43:33.378
- Yes, but there are so many different
forms of it. Zidane, in a way,

02:43:33.748 --> 02:43:36.028
almost moved slowly, in a way.

02:43:36.548 --> 02:43:39.828
- But he'd carry the ball from midfield
into attack incredibly quickly.

02:43:40.748 --> 02:43:44.208
So it's very hard to put into
words, hard to explain it.

02:43:44.928 --> 02:43:48.668
- And both Ronaldos had that. I mean,
the Brazilian Ronaldo too, the way he

02:43:48.708 --> 02:43:52.368
played in those World Cups.
It was just phenomenal.

02:43:52.398 --> 02:43:56.308
- '98, 2002. Even in 2006
when he was already

02:43:56.368 --> 02:44:00.344
past his peak. He was so fascinating to
watch. Seriously, how many people turned

02:44:00.404 --> 02:44:04.184
on their TVs just to watch Ronaldo?
People knew that was it, that in

02:44:04.284 --> 02:44:08.064
2010 he wouldn't be playing.
And even in 2006, he wasn't

02:44:08.104 --> 02:44:12.064
really in his prime. But still, so many

02:44:12.084 --> 02:44:17.504
people turned on the TV just to watch
him play. And Zidane, 2006 was really,

02:44:18.584 --> 02:44:22.444
you know, the peak of that whole generation
of legends that had been building

02:44:22.504 --> 02:44:26.494
since '98, 2000, 2002,
and '04. All the cream of

02:44:26.564 --> 02:44:30.364
the crop came in. Most of them weren't
at their physical peak anymore,

02:44:30.644 --> 02:44:34.393
but the names were still
there. Honestly, I think that

02:44:35.844 --> 02:44:39.424
for football fans, I'd recommend
just looking up the teams and the

02:44:39.464 --> 02:44:42.044
lineups from the 2006 World Cup.

02:44:43.304 --> 02:44:47.104
Look at the rosters. Every single
team had stars you wanted to watch.

02:44:48.404 --> 02:44:52.224
- Speaking of teams, what style of
football do you like? You prefer short

02:44:52.344 --> 02:44:55.104
passing, long balls, individual plays,

02:44:55.944 --> 02:44:59.384
you know, like Maradona carrying
the ball or, of course, Cristiano

02:44:59.424 --> 02:45:02.584
Ronaldo taking the ball
and scoring himself?

02:45:02.614 --> 02:45:04.614
- Mostly those who take the initiative.

02:45:05.204 --> 02:45:05.844
- You like that?

02:45:05.884 --> 02:45:08.904
- Yeah, definitely. I like players who
are willing to take the initiative.

02:45:09.924 --> 02:45:13.664
I was recently talking to Zlatan about
this, and he said, "It drives me

02:45:13.704 --> 02:45:17.814
crazy when I have players on my team
who, when you pass them the ball,

02:45:18.704 --> 02:45:22.584
they immediately get rid of it. They
don't want to take the initiative."

02:45:22.644 --> 02:45:26.584
When you have three or more players on
the team, if there are three people on

02:45:26.624 --> 02:45:30.464
the team who are not afraid to make
mistakes, that's usually enough. They

02:45:30.504 --> 02:45:34.264
take the initiative, take control of
the game, drive the team forward, make

02:45:34.284 --> 02:45:38.124
mistakes, but they keep trying. And
then there are others who hide, he

02:45:38.164 --> 02:45:42.044
says. They get the ball and immediately pass
it to someone else. Just don't give it to me.

02:45:42.524 --> 02:45:47.004
Not me. Not me. They just want to
get rid of the ball. It's like...

02:45:48.764 --> 02:45:52.644
You know, it's like something one of
Zidane's coaches once said. If you

02:45:52.684 --> 02:45:58.644
don't know what to do with the ball, give
it to Zidane. He'll figure it out, so...

02:45:59.504 --> 02:46:02.984
You see, that's the thing. It's a quality

02:46:03.844 --> 02:46:08.133
not every player has.
You're taking the ball.

02:46:09.024 --> 02:46:11.844
You're taking responsibility.
You're taking the initiative.

02:46:13.044 --> 02:46:16.504
You are not afraid to fail. You're
not afraid to make a mistake.

02:46:16.684 --> 02:46:20.664
Cristiano had a moment like that.
He missed a penalty, and later in

02:46:20.704 --> 02:46:22.924
the same match, he stepped
up to take another one.

02:46:24.104 --> 02:46:27.994
Think about that. You take the first
penalty. You miss it. Then another

02:46:28.024 --> 02:46:31.944
penalty comes along, and you still step
up to take it. Because if you miss

02:46:31.984 --> 02:46:35.704
two penalties in the same game, you're under
a lot of pressure, right? But that's the

02:46:35.744 --> 02:46:39.304
point. You're not afraid to fail. As a
leader, you take the initiative, and you're

02:46:39.684 --> 02:46:43.764
you're willing to take risks. That's
honestly the beauty of it, the beauty of

02:46:44.884 --> 02:46:48.284
sports in general. The bigger the risk,
the bigger the potential for greatness.

02:46:48.724 --> 02:46:51.524
If there's no risk, then there's no...

02:46:53.164 --> 02:46:56.824
You can't make, can't put yourself
in the category of athletes

02:46:56.904 --> 02:46:59.504
people will talk about as
some of the best ever.

02:47:00.904 --> 02:47:04.944
- Especially when it's a high-level
game like the World Cup and...

02:47:05.804 --> 02:47:08.664
- Yes. The more risk, the sweeter
the victory. There must be risk.

02:47:09.284 --> 02:47:13.184
- That's why they often speak of
Maradona as a great player. There are

02:47:13.224 --> 02:47:15.224
many great players. He's great-

02:47:15.784 --> 02:47:16.464
- One of them, yeah

02:47:16.504 --> 02:47:20.484
- ...for many reasons. But one reason
is that he kinda, kinda carried the

02:47:20.524 --> 02:47:21.644
team on his shoulders.

02:47:22.144 --> 02:47:24.144
- He took the initiative
and carried them. That's,

02:47:25.124 --> 02:47:29.104
that's when he led Napoli, you know? When
he led Napoli to the league title. Who knew

02:47:29.164 --> 02:47:35.634
Napoli then? Maradona came from Barcelona
to Napoli and made them champions,

02:47:36.144 --> 02:47:40.044
and the whole stadium was named after him.
Many guys played in Napoli, but there

02:47:40.104 --> 02:47:44.004
was never anyone like
Maradona. And recently, I

02:47:44.084 --> 02:47:47.364
saw somewhere, or I think
someone pointed out,

02:47:50.184 --> 02:47:52.904
the pressure on the guys who
are transferring to Napoli.

02:47:54.144 --> 02:47:56.464
Whoever you are, you will
always be in Maradona's shadow,

02:47:57.424 --> 02:48:01.404
and you stay in the shadow because
fans will never give you the

02:48:01.424 --> 02:48:05.264
credit you might deserve because
they will always compare you to

02:48:05.304 --> 02:48:09.084
him. Are you Maradona or not?
And if you're not Maradona,

02:48:09.324 --> 02:48:11.644
you will never get full recognition.

02:48:11.784 --> 02:48:15.684
- I want to say that one of the things that
makes Messi great, I think, is that he really

02:48:15.744 --> 02:48:19.844
had to live in such a shadow of Maradona,
and especially with the World Cup.

02:48:20.864 --> 02:48:21.084
- Yes.

02:48:21.224 --> 02:48:21.984
- It's not easy.

02:48:22.054 --> 02:48:23.024
- And he came out of it.

02:48:23.124 --> 02:48:24.564
- Hard to emerge from that shadow.

02:48:24.884 --> 02:48:32.104
- Yes. And he won the World Cup, yeah. In
that sense, you have to give Messi his due.

02:48:33.244 --> 02:48:38.244
I'm addressing the Real Madrid
fans now. Ours, yours, both sides.

02:48:38.264 --> 02:48:39.184
- You're explaining it to them.

02:48:39.244 --> 02:48:39.464
- Yeah, yeah.

02:48:39.764 --> 02:48:40.124
- I got you.

02:48:40.284 --> 02:48:44.904
- Most of them, I think even if
they don't like Barcelona,

02:48:45.864 --> 02:48:47.364
they still recognize Messi's greatness.

02:48:48.164 --> 02:48:53.004
- I know that you met Zlatan. You talked
to him. I think he's one of the most,

02:48:54.344 --> 02:48:55.484
most self-confident people

02:48:56.544 --> 02:49:00.484
in football, in the world in general. What
do you think about this confidence? Of

02:49:00.544 --> 02:49:04.884
course, he's joking around a little bit,
but I think he really believes in himself.

02:49:05.724 --> 02:49:06.964
- Well, yeah, he does. He does.

02:49:07.804 --> 02:49:11.624
I've known Zlatan for years now.
We get along well, and I know

02:49:11.664 --> 02:49:15.524
his kids, and I like his
personality. I like his confidence.

02:49:15.824 --> 02:49:17.324
I mean, he's a unique player.

02:49:18.704 --> 02:49:22.524
There are not many Zlatans in football. You
know, wherever he played, he always scored.

02:49:23.204 --> 02:49:25.484
He's a goal scorer. He is
that kind of center forward,

02:49:26.344 --> 02:49:29.774
and he always carried the team. He
was a leader wherever he transferred.

02:49:30.624 --> 02:49:34.384
It doesn't matter. Milan, Inter,
Juventus, when he moved to LA

02:49:34.464 --> 02:49:36.584
Galaxy or Paris Saint-Germain.

02:49:38.044 --> 02:49:41.964
He was a leader everywhere he went,
with the national team, with his clubs.

02:49:42.284 --> 02:49:45.664
He was always the guy driving the
team forward, taking the initiative.

02:49:46.664 --> 02:49:50.544
And honestly, he cemented his
place as one of the greatest

02:49:50.624 --> 02:49:55.704
players to ever play the game.
And yeah, maybe he was...

02:49:57.584 --> 02:50:01.424
When it comes to trophies, not individual
achievements, but team honors,

02:50:01.844 --> 02:50:03.064
Sweden wasn't such a

02:50:03.896 --> 02:50:07.896
Wasn't exactly the greatest team,
and some of the clubs he played for

02:50:09.896 --> 02:50:15.816
didn't always win the biggest
trophies. But as an athlete,

02:50:16.756 --> 02:50:22.536
as an individual, as a person,
he is a very strong athlete.

02:50:23.816 --> 02:50:27.776
- We talked a lot about Dagestan, talked
a lot about America. You're in Dubai

02:50:27.796 --> 02:50:31.696
a lot these days. Do you like
it there? Also, you have spoken

02:50:33.376 --> 02:50:34.896
very highly about Sheikh Hamdan.

02:50:36.276 --> 02:50:40.076
What do you like about Dubai? What do you
like about this region? And what do you

02:50:40.136 --> 02:50:42.076
respect about Sheikh Hamdan?

02:50:43.336 --> 02:50:47.316
- Our team's been in Dubai for seven
to eight years now. We train at the

02:50:47.356 --> 02:50:51.206
Nad Al Sheba Sports Complex.
The NAS Sports Complex,

02:50:51.316 --> 02:50:57.236
this is under Sheikh Hamdan's office. It's
like his home, his place. We train there,

02:50:58.156 --> 02:50:59.996
we eat there, we live there.

02:51:00.836 --> 02:51:08.256
Sheikh Hamdan helps us with everything and
takes care of us. The way he treats us,

02:51:09.176 --> 02:51:16.636
what he does for us, his support is just
limitless. He supports us in everything,

02:51:17.716 --> 02:51:21.336
and it's not just one, two, or three years.
It's already been seven or eight years.

02:51:23.216 --> 02:51:28.076
And I am very, very grateful to
him. The way he welcomed us,

02:51:29.916 --> 02:51:36.336
took us in, and helped us, continues to
help, it's hard to put it into words

02:51:36.346 --> 02:51:43.936
how grateful we are. Our entire team is very
grateful to him, and he knows it. So in

02:51:43.976 --> 02:51:46.796
general, Dubai has become... Actually, the

02:51:48.496 --> 02:51:52.456
Emirates, Dubai, and Abu
Dhabi have become like a

02:51:52.596 --> 02:51:56.336
second home for us. And so
it happened that over the

02:51:57.256 --> 02:52:01.156
last six or seven years, the
UFC has been holding events in

02:52:01.196 --> 02:52:04.996
Abu Dhabi, and the PFL has
been putting on events in

02:52:05.066 --> 02:52:08.996
Dubai. These are two of the biggest
MMA organizations in the world,

02:52:09.476 --> 02:52:13.395
and a lot of our guys compete there.
We arrive before the fight, we

02:52:13.476 --> 02:52:15.756
stay, we do training camps, they compete.

02:52:16.936 --> 02:52:21.476
We are incredibly fortunate that we
have support at the government level.

02:52:22.756 --> 02:52:26.256
In Abu Dhabi, we've been supported
by Sheikh Mohammed, Sheikh

02:52:26.336 --> 02:52:32.996
Tahnoon, and Sheikh Mansour. And in
Dubai, Sheikh Hamdan has really taken us

02:52:33.096 --> 02:52:36.496
in like his own team,
like younger brothers.

02:52:37.276 --> 02:52:41.066
In that respect, we've been incredibly
fortunate, and our entire team is

02:52:41.096 --> 02:52:44.276
deeply grateful for their support.

02:52:44.936 --> 02:52:48.696
- And they've created a country that
has become one of the world's central

02:52:48.796 --> 02:52:51.076
hubs. People from everywhere travel there.

02:52:52.076 --> 02:52:55.796
- It's unique, right? The local population
is only around one and a half

02:52:55.856 --> 02:52:58.376
million people, actually
even less than that.

02:53:00.236 --> 02:53:03.756
But, you know, there's such
an influx of tourists,

02:53:04.096 --> 02:53:06.656
people, business people, major events,

02:53:07.476 --> 02:53:11.056
and everything happening in terms
of business and sports is just

02:53:11.096 --> 02:53:16.896
incredible. What's happening in Dubai and
Abu Dhabi is, it's like nothing else.

02:53:17.816 --> 02:53:21.776
The only places you could really compare
it to are major global cities like New

02:53:21.816 --> 02:53:25.576
York, London, Paris, Tokyo. But those are

02:53:25.636 --> 02:53:29.596
huge metropolitan areas with
populations of well over

02:53:29.636 --> 02:53:33.596
twenty million people, and they have
centuries of history behind them.

02:53:34.556 --> 02:53:40.596
And here, they've built cities, an entire
country really, in just a few decades

02:53:41.556 --> 02:53:48.266
and turned it into a kind of magnet. It's
like a magnet that attracts everyone.

02:53:49.536 --> 02:53:53.396
The country's location, its
logistics, the sheer number of people

02:53:53.496 --> 02:53:57.336
coming in, not just visiting,
but wanting to move there, live

02:53:57.396 --> 02:54:00.016
there, and start businesses,
it's remarkable.

02:54:00.996 --> 02:54:04.836
It's a unique place in today's world. With

02:54:04.856 --> 02:54:08.536
everything that's happening around the
globe right now, this country really

02:54:08.636 --> 02:54:13.376
stands out because of the opportunities
it offers. It's unique in that sense.

02:54:14.676 --> 02:54:18.156
- Now I'd like to ask you this. For you,

02:54:19.076 --> 02:54:25.676
your Muslim faith and God, it's obviously
central to your life and to who you are.

02:54:27.176 --> 02:54:30.576
Can you explain what role
God plays in your life?

02:54:32.976 --> 02:54:36.636
- Well, first of all, I was
born, raised, and brought up

02:54:37.316 --> 02:54:39.576
in a Muslim family, in a Muslim community.

02:54:44.076 --> 02:54:47.976
Since childhood, religion's always been
the most important thing for me. And

02:54:48.056 --> 02:54:51.436
when I say religion, the
first thing I mean is Salah,

02:54:51.676 --> 02:54:55.336
Namaz. And my entire life
in general, my daily

02:54:55.356 --> 02:54:59.076
routine, the way I lived,
trained, studied, and everything

02:54:59.136 --> 02:55:01.816
else has always been built around Salah.

02:55:03.296 --> 02:55:06.996
The rest was scheduled around
it. Even today, when I was

02:55:07.016 --> 02:55:10.736
choosing time for this podcast, I
knew we'd be talking for a couple of

02:55:10.756 --> 02:55:14.736
hours, so I deliberately picked a
time right after the midday prayer.

02:55:16.016 --> 02:55:19.636
I pray, come here, finish up here
with you, and then go pray again.

02:55:21.896 --> 02:55:25.696
That's how my life's been, and still
is, and that's how it will continue to

02:55:25.736 --> 02:55:29.416
be, I hope. I've always tried to

02:55:29.496 --> 02:55:33.396
organize my life around worship,
because first and foremost, my

02:55:33.516 --> 02:55:36.216
purpose in this life is
to worship the one God.

02:55:37.416 --> 02:55:43.796
Everything that happens after that
is, it's secondary, so to speak.

02:55:45.576 --> 02:55:49.466
And faith in God and learning
more and just striving to

02:55:49.556 --> 02:55:53.636
get closer to God, that continues
throughout our entire lives.

02:55:55.236 --> 02:56:04.156
So in that regard, I'm very grateful
to the Almighty for opening this door

02:56:05.376 --> 02:56:09.196
of understanding and reflection
for me, for helping me

02:56:09.276 --> 02:56:13.556
realize that we are all servants
of God and that our purpose

02:56:15.056 --> 02:56:18.456
in life is to serve one God.

02:56:18.516 --> 02:56:21.636
- And as you were explaining
that helped you in your

02:56:21.696 --> 02:56:25.116
career in the sense that, you know,

02:56:25.176 --> 02:56:28.336
psychologically, by keeping you from
letting success go to your head?

02:56:28.726 --> 02:56:34.396
- That helped me with everything. I've always
placed my only hope in the Almighty.

02:56:35.156 --> 02:56:39.116
Whatever situation I might face,
I've always believed that only the

02:56:39.176 --> 02:56:42.036
Almighty can truly make it easier for me.

02:56:43.416 --> 02:56:47.196
And when it comes to not letting
success go to your head,

02:56:47.496 --> 02:56:51.436
not becoming proud, not
becoming full of yourself,

02:56:51.876 --> 02:56:55.036
religion and my faith in Allah
have helped me with that too.

02:56:55.856 --> 02:57:01.436
I truly believe that only faith can help a
person get through life's hardest trials.

02:57:02.436 --> 02:57:09.866
And sometimes I think about people
who suffer the loss of a loved one,

02:57:10.676 --> 02:57:14.266
someone very close to them.
For example, when I lost my

02:57:14.296 --> 02:57:18.096
father, I remember thinking to
myself, "If I didn't believe in the

02:57:18.156 --> 02:57:20.756
Almighty, if I didn't have faith in God

02:57:22.536 --> 02:57:26.316
and understand that it was
predestined, that we all come into

02:57:26.326 --> 02:57:29.936
this world and then leave it,
that no one stays here forever,

02:57:30.796 --> 02:57:34.416
that no one belongs to anyone, and
we all belong to the Almighty,

02:57:34.716 --> 02:57:38.676
and that we will all return to Him, I
don't know how I would have coped with

02:57:38.716 --> 02:57:42.516
it." I'm a very emotional
person. I think it would have

02:57:42.536 --> 02:57:46.496
been extremely difficult for me to go
through all of that without faith.

02:57:47.476 --> 02:57:50.356
In that sense, I feel fortunate
to have this understanding.

02:57:51.276 --> 02:57:55.596
- So faith really helped you
when you lost your father?

02:57:57.226 --> 02:57:59.426
- This was the only thing that
helped. Nothing else worked.

02:58:00.516 --> 02:58:05.256
I know a couple of people who have
lost someone very close to them,

02:58:07.516 --> 02:58:15.415
and they struggle to accept it. They would
ask, "Why? Why? Why?" So many questions.

02:58:16.296 --> 02:58:19.876
I told them, to answer that
question, you need to go deeper into

02:58:19.896 --> 02:58:22.036
religion and try to understand

02:58:23.276 --> 02:58:26.996
why were you even born in the first place,
and why do these thoughts even come

02:58:27.036 --> 02:58:30.796
into your head? And what if you didn't
have any thoughts at all? What if you

02:58:30.836 --> 02:58:34.105
couldn't think, couldn't
understand anything? These

02:58:34.116 --> 02:58:38.016
thoughts, this awareness, your
mind, your abilities, none of

02:58:38.076 --> 02:58:41.596
that was there before.
Then it appeared. Why?

02:58:42.376 --> 02:58:46.216
That's the question. None of us existed.
Then we were born. We were little.

02:58:46.316 --> 02:58:50.236
We weren't aware of anything. We didn't
remember anything. Then little by little, we

02:58:50.336 --> 02:58:52.396
grew up and became adults.

02:58:53.876 --> 02:58:57.636
We became successful, became
somebody. We became who we are.

02:58:58.096 --> 02:59:01.706
So why did all of this come
specifically to you? There are so many

02:59:01.736 --> 02:59:03.696
questions like that. And so

02:59:05.196 --> 02:59:12.076
these questions are, let's say, a kind
of guide through life. And for me

02:59:12.896 --> 02:59:15.976
that's what religion is.
It guides me and helps me.

02:59:17.836 --> 02:59:20.856
- You often say, "Alhamdulillah,

02:59:21.976 --> 02:59:26.316
all praise to God." That's very powerful,
that sense of gratitude. Can you tell us,

02:59:27.496 --> 02:59:29.156
can you explain why you say it so often?

02:59:31.036 --> 02:59:34.336
- You know the saying, the tongue
speaks what the heart is full of.

02:59:36.356 --> 02:59:39.996
The way you speak shows what's
in your heart. If you have

02:59:40.056 --> 02:59:43.176
something dark in your heart, your
words will show it, no doubt.

02:59:43.956 --> 02:59:47.456
We all have some dark parts in our hearts.

02:59:48.836 --> 02:59:52.156
Every single person has them.
Sometimes they come out in

02:59:53.936 --> 03:00:00.436
what we say, how we express ourselves,
what we share with people, and so on.

03:00:01.496 --> 03:00:05.006
Everyone has that. But if your
heart is mostly good and pure,

03:00:05.456 --> 03:00:10.516
then that will also be
reflected in your speech. And

03:00:12.136 --> 03:00:16.096
And I just like praising the
Almighty and giving all the

03:00:16.156 --> 03:00:20.816
credit for my success back to Him because

03:00:22.176 --> 03:00:25.515
everything we have is given to us
by the Almighty. Throughout the

03:00:25.556 --> 03:00:29.426
history, there have been many
people who made big mistakes after

03:00:29.496 --> 03:00:33.076
becoming successful, powerful,
and wealthy, and they

03:00:33.156 --> 03:00:39.336
started saying, "No, no, this happened to
me because I'm so great, because I'm so,

03:00:41.856 --> 03:00:45.816
I'm so intelligent, because
I had vision, because I've

03:00:45.856 --> 03:00:49.676
got the brains, because I'm
extremely capable. That's why

03:00:49.756 --> 03:00:53.376
all of this came my way." But at
the same time, there were many

03:00:53.456 --> 03:00:59.736
others who worked just as hard,
who weren't any less intelligent,

03:01:00.136 --> 03:01:05.496
and the Almighty just didn't give it to
them. He chose you and gave it to you,

03:01:06.176 --> 03:01:10.016
and you just have to give the credit back
to Him. You shouldn't take the credit for

03:01:10.076 --> 03:01:13.036
yourself. That's what I believe.

03:01:14.616 --> 03:01:17.996
And it's something I've come to
through studying my religion.

03:01:20.456 --> 03:01:24.376
I understand that everything the
Almighty has given to me is not because

03:01:24.436 --> 03:01:28.396
of who I am. It's simply something
God has given me as a test

03:01:29.016 --> 03:01:32.776
to test me and, in the end, see
which of my deeds outweigh the

03:01:32.836 --> 03:01:35.156
others, the good or the bad.

03:01:38.016 --> 03:01:41.836
Because as a believer, I believe
that there will be judgment

03:01:43.676 --> 03:01:46.916
and everything we have, everything
we do, every action we take,

03:01:48.536 --> 03:01:52.436
we will be held accountable
for it. There will be a day

03:01:52.516 --> 03:01:56.076
of judgment, there will be scales,
and everything will be weighed.

03:01:58.716 --> 03:02:05.156
- What advice would you give young
people about life and their careers?

03:02:06.636 --> 03:02:09.246
Millions of people are watching
you and deeply respect you.

03:02:11.440 --> 03:02:13.260
What would you say about
how to live your life?

03:02:15.040 --> 03:02:17.950
And how do you find your
path and stay on it?

03:02:19.620 --> 03:02:24.460
- Well, I wouldn't want to speak just from my
own perspective. I'd rather bring people

03:02:27.160 --> 03:02:30.920
back to the scripture, to what
the Almighty said, "Read."

03:02:31.940 --> 03:02:38.340
That's the first word revealed in the Holy
Quran. The first command is read or ikra.

03:02:39.740 --> 03:02:43.580
So to everyone listening to or
watching this podcast, I would want

03:02:43.679 --> 03:02:50.040
to bring them back to that. Read,
meaning what? Educate yourself, observe,

03:02:50.180 --> 03:02:58.280
learn, improve, but don't stay in one
place. And anything a person is truly

03:02:58.300 --> 03:03:02.100
looking for, if they genuinely
want to find it, not just

03:03:02.160 --> 03:03:06.040
find an answer, but find
the truth, they can reach

03:03:06.120 --> 03:03:10.040
it, but they can never reach
it without reading. They

03:03:10.140 --> 03:03:14.800
need to read. When a person
reads, what they read enters them

03:03:15.619 --> 03:03:19.900
into their body, their heart, their
mind, and it triggers a search.

03:03:21.140 --> 03:03:24.940
A search for knowledge begins inside them,
but if you don't read, you won't find

03:03:24.980 --> 03:03:28.870
anything. Just listening to someone is
one thing, but reading is something

03:03:28.960 --> 03:03:32.320
different. So my advice would be read

03:03:33.900 --> 03:03:37.860
and find within yourself the
strength to come closer to

03:03:37.920 --> 03:03:42.980
one God and seek to understand why we
were sent here in the first place.

03:03:45.740 --> 03:03:50.640
Today, you and me, we're
almost the same age. So let's

03:03:51.520 --> 03:03:57.160
just think about this. For example,
my grandfather on my mother's side

03:03:57.660 --> 03:04:04.620
passed away in two thousand and
three. Now it's twenty twenty-six.

03:04:06.120 --> 03:04:08.220
He's been lying in the grave
for twenty-three years.

03:04:11.140 --> 03:04:12.400
And then there was his grandfather,

03:04:13.340 --> 03:04:17.160
or let's say his great-grandfather
who also died and has been in the

03:04:17.220 --> 03:04:19.700
grave for over a hundred
years, roughly speaking.

03:04:21.620 --> 03:04:25.580
But what about people who died two thousand
years ago? They've been in the grave

03:04:25.800 --> 03:04:28.240
for two thousand years. What's next?

03:04:30.220 --> 03:04:34.160
It's a good question, isn't it? They've
just been in the grave all this time, and

03:04:34.200 --> 03:04:34.580
that's it.

03:04:36.400 --> 03:04:40.240
So what was the purpose of their life?
A person has been in the grave for two

03:04:40.280 --> 03:04:43.260
thousand years, but maybe
lived for seventy years.

03:04:44.320 --> 03:04:48.240
Roughly speaking, let's say one hundred.
They lived for one hundred years

03:04:48.300 --> 03:04:50.460
and have been in the
grave for two thousand.

03:04:51.320 --> 03:04:53.610
So what matters more,
those one hundred years

03:04:55.140 --> 03:04:58.960
or these two thousand years? And what
happens after those two thousand

03:04:59.040 --> 03:05:02.960
years end? People believe different
things, whether there is a day

03:05:03.020 --> 03:05:06.930
of judgment or not. That's where the
difference lies between a believer and

03:05:06.980 --> 03:05:10.860
a non-believer. After everyone is
resurrected, there will be the day of

03:05:10.900 --> 03:05:14.500
judgment. There will be judgment,
and after that, people will

03:05:14.580 --> 03:05:17.980
enter eternity. Eternity
compared to eternity,

03:05:18.640 --> 03:05:22.280
what are two thousand years? They're
nothing. And we live for, let's say,

03:05:22.400 --> 03:05:29.800
seventy years. And many people choose
those seventy years over eternal life.

03:05:31.240 --> 03:05:35.100
And if a person understands that
these seventy years they have

03:05:35.160 --> 03:05:38.520
here, roughly speaking,
seventy or eighty years

03:05:40.940 --> 03:05:44.770
are meant to be used to prepare for
what comes after, so that when the

03:05:44.840 --> 03:05:48.720
day of judgment arrives, and after that
judgment they can live forever, not in hell,

03:05:48.980 --> 03:05:52.800
but in heaven. If that becomes
their ultimate goal, then they

03:05:52.860 --> 03:05:57.540
will strive to do good deeds because
they will fear the day of judgment.

03:05:58.370 --> 03:06:02.620
And that's really what life is about,
preparing yourself for the day of judgment.

03:06:03.569 --> 03:06:07.380
- That's true. To truly understand life and
its deeper meaning, you have to think

03:06:07.460 --> 03:06:12.800
about eternity. Only then can
you really understand your role.

03:06:13.100 --> 03:06:18.600
- Actually, it can keep a person
away from evil. From evil and,

03:06:20.740 --> 03:06:23.320
and it can stop them from
treating other people badly.

03:06:24.580 --> 03:06:28.420
It can hold a person back. But if you
don't have that understanding, if a

03:06:28.440 --> 03:06:31.420
person lives completely convinced
that there's nothing after death,

03:06:32.259 --> 03:06:36.220
what's there to stop him from doing
evil? Just like that. What do

03:06:36.260 --> 03:06:40.480
you think? And on top of that, an
even more dangerous combination.

03:06:42.140 --> 03:06:47.600
You become powerful, wealthy,
and you become famous. What now?

03:06:49.320 --> 03:06:53.060
Suddenly every door is open to you.
Just imagine a person like that.

03:06:53.620 --> 03:06:57.480
And on top of all that, he doesn't
believe in the day of judgment.

03:06:58.020 --> 03:07:01.940
He doesn't believe he'll be held
accountable for his actions. That's, you

03:07:01.980 --> 03:07:07.500
know, it's like a nuclear bomb. He can
end up causing harm to people everywhere.

03:07:08.720 --> 03:07:12.220
But if you know that for
everything you have here, you'll

03:07:12.300 --> 03:07:16.160
eventually have to answer, that
there will be a day of judgment

03:07:16.420 --> 03:07:20.280
and that you'll be called to account,
and if you fail that test, you'll

03:07:20.360 --> 03:07:24.200
burn in hell forever. That'll make
you rein yourself in a little.

03:07:25.940 --> 03:07:29.780
You'll have to take that into consideration.
You'll have to tighten things

03:07:29.880 --> 03:07:32.440
up a little. You'll begin to understand

03:07:34.220 --> 03:07:38.000
that you're not nearly as important
as the image you've created of

03:07:38.040 --> 03:07:45.320
yourself in your own mind. You start becoming
more, as Americans say, down to earth.

03:07:45.440 --> 03:07:50.100
You become more grounded. You're not
flying, you're walking on the ground.

03:07:50.580 --> 03:07:52.160
- You once put it beautifully.

03:07:54.260 --> 03:07:59.120
You can allow money and fame to stay
in your hand, but not in your heart.

03:08:01.960 --> 03:08:05.720
You've still got many years ahead of
you, but you're already an example of

03:08:05.800 --> 03:08:11.100
someone who's become famous, made
money, and remained the same person.

03:08:11.160 --> 03:08:13.860
- If money stays in your
hands and not in your heart

03:08:14.772 --> 03:08:19.112
That's a good thing. But the moment it gets
into a person's heart, it corrupts them.

03:08:19.272 --> 03:08:23.172
Once it's there, it's very difficult to
pull it out of your heart. If you have

03:08:23.212 --> 03:08:26.752
wealth in your hands and
use it properly for good

03:08:26.812 --> 03:08:30.711
causes, for the right purposes,
for people in need, for

03:08:30.752 --> 03:08:35.752
your family, relatives, friends,
people from your hometown,

03:08:36.812 --> 03:08:40.732
that's a good thing. But as soon as that
wealth gets into your heart, it becomes

03:08:40.772 --> 03:08:44.652
very difficult to share
it with others. And this

03:08:44.712 --> 03:08:49.772
is a very dangerous place
for a person to end up.

03:08:50.792 --> 03:08:54.552
And I'm sorry, but as a believer,
I believe that most people in

03:08:54.612 --> 03:08:56.232
hell will be wealthy.

03:08:59.032 --> 03:09:02.561
- One of the reasons millions of people find
you so interesting to watch is that you're

03:09:03.812 --> 03:09:11.052
constantly growing. First, you were a
great fighter. Now you're becoming a

03:09:11.112 --> 03:09:15.152
leader. Your wisdom just keeps increasing.

03:09:16.112 --> 03:09:20.032
You're evolving as a person, but still
the core values remain. It's good to

03:09:20.131 --> 03:09:21.372
see, inspiring for people.

03:09:22.272 --> 03:09:26.212
Money and fame haven't changed you.
You're just growing as a human

03:09:26.252 --> 03:09:27.832
being, as you always have.

03:09:28.352 --> 03:09:32.192
- I hope it's in the right direction. It's
just, the thing is people only see the

03:09:32.232 --> 03:09:35.072
good. They don't see the bad.

03:09:35.472 --> 03:09:39.652
And we all have the good side and
the bad side. Everyone, sadly.

03:09:41.212 --> 03:09:43.352
And especially when it
comes to celebrities,

03:09:44.432 --> 03:09:47.412
we only show the best side of ourselves.

03:09:48.412 --> 03:09:51.912
Even when you post photos,
you only post the good ones,

03:09:52.492 --> 03:09:57.572
the pictures where you look
your best. So I hope that

03:09:58.952 --> 03:10:06.602
the way people see me, as you
pointed out, is the way I really am,

03:10:07.792 --> 03:10:11.562
because it's difficult to judge
yourself objectively. You're always

03:10:11.572 --> 03:10:15.412
biased when it comes to yourself. That's
how it is. We tend to think highly

03:10:15.512 --> 03:10:18.992
of ourselves, right? You can't
really see yourself objectively.

03:10:20.612 --> 03:10:24.572
We hide our flaws and put our
best qualities on display.

03:10:25.552 --> 03:10:30.152
Sometimes people even start believing
in a fake version of themselves.

03:10:33.272 --> 03:10:36.972
For me, I try to stay on the
honest path throughout my

03:10:36.992 --> 03:10:42.132
life to remain true to who
I really am and to show

03:10:43.552 --> 03:10:47.492
my true self to people. Maybe
not everyone agrees with my

03:10:47.592 --> 03:10:51.972
behavior, my opinions, my
worldview, or my attitudes toward,

03:10:53.812 --> 03:10:58.392
towards sports, religion, and other
things. Not everyone shares my views.

03:10:59.372 --> 03:11:02.772
Not everyone is a fan of
mine. There are people who,

03:11:04.832 --> 03:11:09.492
let's say, see me very
differently. So I'm just...

03:11:12.292 --> 03:11:16.692
I've tried, I continue to try
to be who I am. That's all.

03:11:18.692 --> 03:11:23.092
And I hope that my ending
will be a good one, too.

03:11:24.352 --> 03:11:27.462
Well, you know, at least that's my hope.

03:11:28.332 --> 03:11:32.152
- You've stayed the same, but you've
also grown. I mean, today you even

03:11:32.192 --> 03:11:38.372
managed to say something nice about Barça.
So see, that's progress. Yeah. Well done.

03:11:39.212 --> 03:11:43.192
- Come on, without Barcelona, there wouldn't
be Real Madrid. If the Spanish league

03:11:43.232 --> 03:11:47.161
didn't have either Real Madrid or
Barcelona, it just wouldn't be the same.

03:11:47.172 --> 03:11:51.012
They've been competing against each other
for how many years? One hundred twenty.

03:11:51.052 --> 03:11:55.012
It's hard to imagine Barcelona and Real Madrid
without each other, and so is the Spanish

03:11:55.072 --> 03:11:56.792
league without that rivalry.

03:11:57.032 --> 03:12:00.972
It's impossible to imagine. So yeah, we
have to give you some credit, but Real

03:12:01.052 --> 03:12:05.832
Madrid is the royal club. Come
on. It's the best. You know?

03:12:06.412 --> 03:12:10.392
- And thanks to both of them, we've been able
to enjoy truly incredible football for so

03:12:10.452 --> 03:12:10.971
many years.

03:12:11.112 --> 03:12:12.232
- For so many years, yeah.

03:12:12.992 --> 03:12:17.542
- Um, Khabib, brother, I don't
even have the words to explain

03:12:18.852 --> 03:12:22.782
how honored I am that you're here talking
with me today and showing me so much

03:12:22.812 --> 03:12:23.161
warmth.

03:12:24.802 --> 03:12:28.242
There's no way to fully express what you mean
to millions of your fans around the world,

03:12:28.632 --> 03:12:32.011
including me. Thank you so much for
everything. Thank you for this conversation.

03:12:32.212 --> 03:12:33.631
- Thank you. Thank you very much, Lex.

03:12:35.112 --> 03:12:39.432
- Thank you for listening to this conversation
with Khabib Nurmagomedov. And again,

03:12:40.372 --> 03:12:47.272
